1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Son of Grace Dorothy (Jones) Martin and an unknown man.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://web.archive.org/web/20041019140503/http://www.asimovs.com/_issue_0410/theheartofasmall.shtml link] to Martin, George R. R. (October 2004). "The Heart of a Small Boy". Asimov's Science Fiction. Archived from the origi
2 CONC nal on October 19, 2004.
2 CONT
2 CONT >By that time, only my mother, the youngest of Thomas Brady’s eleven children, was still living at home. A number of her siblings had died in childhood; the others had grown up, moved away, and started families of their own. My mo
2 CONC ther was actually younger than a number of her nieces and nephews. When the wealth and the house were gone, she and her mother moved into a modest apartment. Once out of high school, she took a job at Westinghouse, and supported m
2 CONC y grandmother until Catherine passed away in 1941. Some six years after that, Margaret met my father.
2 CONT
2 CONT >His name was Raymond Collins Martin . . . that "Raymond" is where the first of my two Rs originated. My mother called him Ray, and everyone else called him Smokey. ....
2 CONT
2 CONT >.... My father was a veteran of World War II . . . and according to one man, at least, a war hero. He kept a shoebox full of old photographs from his service, small grainy black and white snapshots taken with some old Brownie. Th
2 CONC e pictures look to have been taken in North Africa. There’s sand, tents, barechested GIs . . . and my father, grinning into the camera, looking impossibly young. In one picture there’s a camel in the background. In most of the oth
2 CONC ers Smokey has a Camel dangling from his lip . . . he didn’t switch to Luckies until after the war. Some of his buddies are in the pictures, clowning around with their rifles, posing with their arms around his shoulders. I don’t k
2 CONC now their names. I never will.
2 CONT
