1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Thomas Pidge (Pigge)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Anderson's Great Migration Project:
2 CONT
2 CONT THOMAS PIDGE 
2 CONT * ORIGIN: Saffron Walden, Essex
2 CONT * MIGRATION: 1633 
2 CONT * FIRST RESIDENCE: Roxbury
2 CONT *  CHURCH MEMBERSHIP: "Thomas Pigge" was admitted to Roxbury church as member #84, among those who arrived in 1633 [RChR 79]. 
2 CONT * FREEMAN: 14 May 1634 [MBCR 1:368]. ESTATE: In the Roxbury land inventory of about 1652 the heirs of "Thomas Pigge" held ten parcels of land, all of which had been granted to him: "house, barn and lot with a swamp, three and a ha
2 CONC lf acres"; three acres upon Black Neck; one acre and a half in the calves' pasture; one acre and a half of salt marsh; five acres of land on Dorchester Brook; five acres of land; eight acres of land; two acres of fresh meadow; twe
2 CONC nty-four and a half acres, being the twentieth lot in the second allotments in the last division; and twenty acres in the thousand acres near Dedham [RBOP 31-32]. In his undated will, proved 12 (or 14) September 1644, "Thomas Pig"
2 CONC  bequeathed to "his son Thomas Pig the house with the homelot two acres of fresh marsh ... my lot by the dead swamp ... all the land in the neck both upland marsh the five acres at the great lots end, he paying to his brother John
2 CONC  ten pounds, five pounds at twenty-one years of age the other five pounds a year after this also I do give to my son Thomas Pig the land in the calves' pasture paying to his sister Hany Pig six pounds, three pounds a year after hi
2 CONC s mother's death the other three pound the year after that," to "my daughter Sary my daughter Mathew [i.e., Martha] my eight acre lot lying upon Pig's Hill I also give to them my last division of ground," to "my daughter Mary my a
2 CONC llotment in the thousand acres lying at Dedham," and to "my wife ... all I have so long as she lives to bring up my children after her death all my children to have their portions as aforesaid" [SPR 1:33]. ^ BIRTH: By about 1594 b
2 CONC ased on date of marriage. 
2 CONT * DEATH: Roxbury 30 December 1643 ("Thomas Pig died of a dropsy, a godly Christian man. He had a fall a bruise on his back, which hurt his kidneys not carefully cured they utterly wasted away many other of his entrails" [RChR 171]
2 CONC ). 
2 CONT * MARRIAGE: Saffron Walden, Essex, 1 November 1619 Mary Sothy; "Mary Pigge the wife of Thomas Pigge" was admitted to Roxbury church as member #85 [RChR 79]. She married (2) Dedham 13 August 1645 Michael Metcalf, as his second wife
2 CONC  [DeVR 126]. 
