1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mary (Sothy) Metcalf migrated to New England during the Puritan Great Migration (1621-1640).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Sothy-3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mary Sothy''' was probably born in England about 1590,[1] or perhaps a little later, say 1598, as her last child was born in 1643.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married first on 1 November 1619 in Saffron Walden, Essex, England, to Thomas Pidge.[2] "Mary Pigge the wife of Thomas Pigge" was admitted to Roxbury church as member #85, among those who arrived in 1633.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT After Thomas' death from dropsy and a fall which injured his kidneys in December 1643,[1] she married Michael Metcalf on 13 August 1645 in Dedham, (then) Suffolk County, Massachusetts Bay Colony.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary (Sothy) (Pidge) Metcalfe died on 12 February 1672[/3?] in Dedham.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Mary (Sothy) Pidge Metcalf
2 CONT *'''Birth: 	1589  Essex, England
2 CONT *'''Death: 	Feb. 12, 1673  Dedham, Norfolk County, Massachusetts, USA
2 CONT *'''A TAG article (Vol 36,pg 113) gives Mary's parents as''' Richard Sothy and Mary Bentley. '''Mary Sothy married Thomas Pidge in Saffron Walden, Essex, 1 November 1619; "Mary Pigge the wife of Thomas Pigge" was admitted to Roxbu
2 CONC ry church as member #85. 
2 CONT *'''They had nine children:''' Mary Wood, Ann, Sarah, Hannah, Thomas, Thomas again, Sarah Metcalfe, John, & Martha Bullard. 
2 CONT *'''She married (2) Dedham 13 August 1645 Michael Metcalf, as his second wife.
2 CONT *'''She died at Dedham on Feb 12,1673. 
2 CONT *Family links: 
2 CONT *''' Spouses:
2 CONT *'''  Thomas Pidge (1594 - 1643)*
2 CONT *'''  Michael Metcalf (1591 - 1664)*
2 CONT *''' Children:
2 CONT **  Mary Pidge Wood (1622 - 1663)*
2 CONT *'''Burial:  Old Village Cemetery, Dedham, Norfolk County, Massachusetts, USA
2 CONT *Find A Grave Memorial# 35389321
2 CONT *From: http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=35389321 _________________
2 CONT *'''Descendants of John Gamage of Ipswich, Mass
2 CONT *https://books.google.com/books?id=QidMAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA64&lpg=PA64&dq=Leonard+Metcalf+1545&source=bl&ots=lCDYiRkpz5&sig=ZxqldVz90ovkeUGHQEa2lf4tbJY&hl=en&sa=X&ei=w-5nVfKtE9DLogSe6ICYBg&ved=0CC8Q6AEwBDge#v=onepage&q=Leonard%20Metcalf
2 CONC %201545&f=false
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/descendantsofjoh00mort
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/descendantsofjoh00mort#page/64/mode/1up
2 CONT *METCALF.
2 CONT *'''   1. REV. MICHAEL METCALF''', born at Taterford in 1592, was the son of Rev. Leonard Metcalf of Taterford, England, who was born in 1545, and was grandson of [Thomas Metcalf, a goldsmith of London, whose wife was Alice Cook.]
2 CONC  On September 21, 1618, the freedom of the City of Norfolk, where he was a minister, was granted him by the King. Being persecuted for his religious principles by Bishop Wren, he succeeded, after much difficulty, in fleeing the co
2 CONC untry. He arrived in New England June 20, 1637, with his wife, nine children and servant. He married, Oct. 13, 1616, Sarah Ellwyn of Waynburn, who was born June 17, 1593, and died Feb. 21, 1645. '''He married, (2), in 1645, Mary P
2 CONC idge of Roxbury'''. He was Selectman, and held other offices of trust. He died December 24, 1674, leaving an estate of £364.
2 CONT *His son,
2 CONT *   2. JOHN, was born in Norwich, England, in 1622. He settled in Dedham, Mass., and married, March 22, 1647, Mary Chickering.
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/descendantsofjoh00mort#page/65/mode/1up
2 CONT *They moved to Medfield, where he died Nov. 27, 1675. His wife died in 1698.
2 CONT *Their daughter,
2 CONT *   3. Hannah, was born October 13, 1664, and married, 1690, Elisha Bullens. She died in 1719.
2 CONT **CHICKERING.
2 CONT * .... etc. ____________________
2 CONT *'''A Genealogical Dictionary of the First Settlers of New England: Showing Three Generations of ... Vol. III.     by James Savage, ....
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/agenealogicaldi03dextgoog
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/agenealogicaldi03dextgoog#page/n213/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.203
2 CONT * .... '''MICHAEL, Dedham''', b. 1586, at Tatterford, in Co. Norfolk, was a dornock weaver at Norwich, and free of the city, where all his ch. were b. m. 13 Oct. 1616, Sarah, had Michael, b. 13 Nov. 1617, d. soon; Mary, 14 Oct. 16
2 CONC 18, but the geneal. of the fam. in Reg. VI. 173, says 14 Feb. 1619 ; Michael, again, 29 Aug. 1620 ; John, 5 or 15 Sept. 1622, bef. ment ; Sarah, 10 Sept. 1624; Eliz. 4 Oct 1626; Martha, 27 Mar. or Oct. 1628; Thomas, 27 Dec 1629 or
2 CONC  30; Ann, 1 Mar. 1631 or 34, d. soon; Jane, 24 Mar. 1632; and Rebecca, 5 Apr. 1635; his w. was b. at a village near Norwich, he says, 17 June 1593, but possib. the figures have been mistaken, as in the examin. one week bef. the sa
2 CONC il, of the ship, call. I think, the Rose, of Yarmouth, from Yarmouth, Apr. 1637, he calls hims. 45 yrs. of age, and w. 39. "From the relig. tyranny" exercised by Wren, then Bp. of .Norwich, he felt forced to esc. even at the expen
2 CONC se of separ. from his fam. for a time, and emb. at London, 17 Sept. 1636, for N. E. but was sadly tormented by equal tempests on the water, and the ship at Christmas put back to Plymouth ; and so far was this a happiness that in A
2 CONC pr. foll, he had license for the whole fam. to come ; only 8 ch. are ment. in the custom-ho. docum. but I can hardly doubt that the name of John was casually overlook. At Boston he arr. "three days bef. mid. sum. with w. nine ch. 
2 CONC and a serv." wh. was Thomas Comberbach, aged 16, 1 presume in one of the three ships ment. by Winth. as coming in, from Ipswich, 20 June. He may have been br. of Joseph ; was freem. 13 May 1640, or 18 May 1642, and, perhaps, swore
2 CONC  on both days, tho. more prob. it is a fault of the Secr, as in the list appears. His w. d. 21 Feb. 1645, and '''he m. 13 Aug. foll. Martha, wid. of Thomas Pigg, or Pidge''' ; and he d. 27 Dec. 1664. A very, curious docum. his eng
2 CONC agem. aft. 70 yrs. of age to keep the town sch. in 1656, is giv. in Geneal. Reg. X. 282. His will, made six wks. bef. gives to eight ch. Michael being d. and ea. of the ds. m. Martha hav. then a sec. or third h. to gr.ch. Michael,
2 CONC  s. of Michael, and gr.ch. William Brignall, s. of d. Martha, wh. was a mem. of Roxbury ch. by her first h. and to Martha Bullard, d. of his w. Mary m. 24 Nov. 1642, Henry Wilson ; Sarah m. Robert Onion, as his sec. w. the first h
2 CONC av. d. in Apr. 1643; Eliz. m. 15 Sept. 1648, Thomas Bancroft of Reading; Martha m. William Brignall, next, 2 Aug. 1654, Christopher Smith as the Geneal. Reg. VI. 173, says, tho. I doubt its correctness, for Christopher Smith in  6
2 CONC 68, is nam. in his will by Jonathan Fairbanks as h. of his d. Mary, and third h . . . . .  Stow; Jane m. 1654, Samuel Walker, 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/agenealogicaldi03dextgoog#page/n214/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.204
2 CONT *(tho. strong doubts of error in the name are felt) of Rehoboth ; and Rebecca m. 5 Apr. 1659, John Mackintosh. .... etc. ___________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Links 
2 CONT * http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=rtate&id=I280
