2 NOTE Thanks Claire, but I, too, only found these things out recently with the break through of you finding Angela's tree and articles sent to by Richard in England.  So I cannot accept any credit alone.  It's been a joint effort with m
3 CONC e trying to sort what belongs to who and "package" the whole thing so that it is easier to understand.  Nothing is cast in stone and open to criticism and review at any time along the way.  I will also be going there one fine day.
2 NOTE Alain, that piece of family history was really mastery ... you must read it, live it, dream it... you've made it so alive to me... I wished I'd know all this years ago. I really want to go now. Should be in UK around 23rd july... 
3 CONC so I'd say the week after I'll walk into the door !!! ...I'll enquire and make sure it's open...thanks for the tip about the engraving, really beautiful, my kids don't know it, but it's their  next Xmas present!!!! Maria don't wor
3 CONC ry not knowing much about genealogy. It requires incredible brains, like Alain and Philippe Busch to work it all out and we girls can enjoy it!!!! thanks Alain and Philippe
2 NOTE Hi Maria
3 CONT
3 CONT During her long widowhood, Elizabeth the wife of Richard 1563-1602, occupied Allerton Hall. And by her Will and Codicil, both executed in July, 1624, gave her second son, Edward, the occupation and profit of “this my hall in Aller
3 CONC ton," and the houses and lands for three years after her death for the better discharging of her debts and to keep her other sons, Richard and John, “with dyet." and until her grandson, Richard (of Parbold, son of Thomas who died 
3 CONC in 1623), then an infant of about two years old, the heir of Parbold and Allerton, "came of age".  
3 CONT
3 CONT "The commissioners" (of parliament who had confiscated the estate) sold it in 1654 to a John Sumpner of Midhurst, Sussex. for £2,700. Notwithstanding that the Lathoms, father, mother, and children, now dispossessed, were frequentl
3 CONC y convicted of recusancy and suffered the penalties accordingly, they contrived to hang on to both Parbold and Allerton a little longer. Indeed it was not until 1670 that the Sumpners managed to eject the Lathoms, and only then by
3 CONC  increasing the amount of the original purchase price.  
3 CONT
3 CONT Edward's (1&2) family were living there first, but the house went to cousin Richard (who married Katherine Massey) probably around 1643 (and family). Whether Richard's family ever took occupation or Edward stayed on, we will never
3 CONC  know.  Either way the Lathom's were kicked out by the Sumpner's in 1670.
2 NOTE Hi dear cousins
3 CONT Genealogy is such a mystery to me... Who was the last Latham (or Lathom)  living there? I would like go there too
2 NOTE we can, at least, by an engraving (see http://www.heatons-of-tisbury.co.uk/lancashire.htm).  The one I like is the antique engraved print from Beauties of England & Wales by John Britton (1771. .1857), English antiquary and topogr
3 CONC apher and E. W. Brayley. The friends wrote most of Beauties of England and Wales (18 vol., in 25, 1801 . .15) image 11 x 16cm images printed close to edge of page unmounted £12. ..  x 4
2 NOTE Can you put the photo on cousinade??? I'm sure everyone would be interested.
2 NOTE you make it sound so exciting. How about buying it back!!!!!they are enough Lathams, Lathoms, et tous les français to put in enough to get it back. Then name you, archivist, I'm happy to make cups of tea, etc...I'm sure we'll find
3 CONC  a few volonteers to do the garden;.. Bravo anyhow for this amazing photo. You're amazing. I'm going to UK end of July and shall visit...I'll take photos and see if they want to sell!!!!
2 NOTE "It was not until 1670 that the Sumpners (future owners) managed to eject the Lathoms, and only then by increasing the amount of the original purchase price".  So it seems it was a forced sale at a pre-determined price set by Parl
3 CONC iament (not necessarily the real
3 CONT market value), but the Lathom's managed to squeeze out a little bit more from the eventual buyer.
2 NOTE I think it may be owned by the government and is open for public visits.  Did you see how I have reallocated the "Richard and Edward's" (who supposedly lost their Estates to Parliament for treason) to that of their father Edward a
3 CONC nd uncle Richard.  Their uncle Richard died in action in the early years of the war, Elizabeth allowed Edward to have the house until Edward's (then infant) cousin Richard was 22 and become legible as heir (around 1646).  Then Edw
3 CONC ard and family (either would have moved out or rented it from cousin Richard). Either way, the house was lost to Parliament in 1652 and thus began dark times for us Lath(o)am's.  Edward (2) nephew John started a charity (that stil
3 CONC l exists today) which would almost certainly been inspired by the need to assist many dispossessed Royalist families in these parts including immediate at family.  He is to be applauded.
3 CONT Despite the resurrection of the Monarchy (Charles II)  in May 1660, no measures were taken to re-instate the Royalist (Catholic)
3 CONT families who had stood by Charles I.  These families simply disappeared into a mediocre obscurity.
2 NOTE Brillant, dear cousin Alain!!! where did you find this photo... finally I get to see it!!! what a shame we don't own it!!! but I expect there's a lot of gardening. 
