1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (iii) Mary Venables m. Sir Richard Assheton of Middleton (d 07.11.1618)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Venables was born in October, 1576.  She married Richard Assheton of Middeton in Lancashire, esquire.  Richard Assheton and Thomas Holcroft purchased Whalley Abbey in 1544 during the dissolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT She had a younger half sister also named Mary, who married Humphrey Mackworth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Ashton died 7 November 1618 and was buried at Middleton; an inquisition post mortem was held 12 January 1619. His widow (described as 'a right worthy and truly religious matron') died 24 February and was buried at Middleto
2 CONC n, 27 February 1644/5. A brass at Middleton commemorates both husband and wife and their six sons and two daughters
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.ancestry.com/genealogy/records/richard-ashton_44421745
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT #Ralph Ashton 1605 - 1650
2 CONT #James Ashton 1608 - 1611
2 CONT #Thomas Ashton 1612 - Unknown
2 CONT #William Ashton 1609 - Unknown
2 CONT #Richard Ashton 1604 - Unknown
2 CONT #John Ashton 1607 - 1640
2 CONT #Dorothy Ashton 1610 - Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Named in her father’s will
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://venablesancestry.wordpress.com/introduction/sir-thomas-venable-21st-baron/
2 CONT
2 CONT The Will of Thomas Venables, Esquire, Baron of Kinderton, 1606
2 CONT
2 CONT >”December 7, 1604.  Thomas Venables of Kinderton, Esquire. I will that my Ladie Gargrave and all my brothers in lawe and sisters in law which shall come to my funeral, and likewise my brother Mainewaring and my sister Mainewaring
2 CONC e and all such theire children, and likewise such other of my kinsfolkes as shall come to my funeral shall have blackes, viz., the gentlemen clokes and the gentlewomen gownes, but I would not have them invited to my said funeral b
2 CONC ecause I would have noe great pompe or solemnitite there at.  Item my will is that my servants in liverie which shall be at my funeral have everie of them a black liverie coate, and my executors to have a blacke gowne or cloake.  
2 CONC Mr. Watts, if he continue preacher at Middlewiche and do preach at my funeral, to have a blacke gowne and 40s.  To the poore of Middlewich £10. To the poore of Eccleston and Hawley 40 s each, of Wilton £3 6s. 8d., of Marshton and 
2 CONC Winnington 40s.  ...
2 CONT
2 CONT >”My gould, silver, plate, etc. the third of my lands, etc. which did discend to me from my late father Sir Thomas Venables, knight, deceased, etc., and my purchased lands, to my executors to pay my debts and pay £1,000 to my daug
2 CONC hter Egerton, according to the covenants between me and Sir Thomas Egerton, knight, now Lord Chancellor of England; to pay £500 to Sir Richard Aston, knight, for the payment of which Sir Peter Leigh, knight, standeth bounden for t
2 CONC he last part of my daughter Marie Aston’s marriage money.  To my daughter Marie Venables, by Anne now my wife £1,000 and £1,000 which my Laide Gargrave and Sir Richard Gargrave, knight, do owe me in part of my marriage money.  If 
2 CONC my wife be with child £1,000 to it.  To my daughter Egerton £100, and to her three children Elizabeth, Marie, and Vere Egerton £100 each, if she do not inherit my auntient lands.  To my daughter Ashton £100. To my wife Anne her th
2 CONC irds, etc. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT >”... To my sonne in law Mr. Richard Ashton my best horse or gelding after my Ladie Gargrave and my exeutors.  My executors not to take from my wife the juells, etc. which she brought out of Yorkeshire with her nor the gray geldin
2 CONC g comonlie called my wives gelding.  The residue to my sonne Peter and his heires male, with remainder to my three daughters, the Ladie Elizabeth Egerton, Marie Ashton, and Marie Venables.  Sir Peter Leigh, knight, Sir Richard Gar
2 CONC grave, knight, and Henrie Mainwaring, Esquire, executors.  Proved at York, 22 February, 1605-06. “
2 CONT ----
