1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ROBERT COLEIRE de DARLASTON (c.1453- c.1505)
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Coleire was born in France about 1453 and died in Staffordshire, England about 1505. He married lsabella Doddington, daughter of Sir John Doddington, about 1483. Robert Colleire came to England about 1482, long preceeding t
2 CONC he arrival of other members of his family in England and Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He settled in the market town of Stone Manor, in Straffordshire County, England. The town stands on the river Trent and Trent Mersey Canal, 7 miles north nortwest of Strafford, 7 miles south of Stoke-upon-Trent, and 137 miles from
2 CONC  London. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a taylor (tailor), draper (a dealer in cloth or in (clothes), then a woolbuyer. Some of these staplers (dealers in staple goods) grew to great wealth. In the year 1503, two years before his death, Robert and Isabella moved 
2 CONC to Darlaston Manor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert and his son Thurston leased Darlaston Manor, in the county of Staffordshire, from Thomas Whally, then in 1537, Robert's son, James purchased the manor from Richard Whalley.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Collier-3175
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT "ROBERT COLEIRE de DARLASTON (c.1453- c.1505)
2 CONT Robert Coleire was born in France about 1453 and died in Staffordshire, England about 1505. He married lsabella Doddington, daughter of Sir John Doddington, about 1483. Robert Colleire came to England about 1482, long preceeding t
2 CONC he arrival of other members of his family in England and Ireland. He settled in the market town of Stone Manor, in Straffordshire County, England. The town stands on the river Trent and Trent Mersey Canal, 7 miles north nortwest o
2 CONC f Strafford, 7 miles south of Stoke-upon-Trent, and 137 miles from London. He was a taylor (tailor), draper (a dealer in cloth or in (clothes), then a woolbuyer. Some of these staplers (dealers in staple goods) grew to great wealt
2 CONC h. In the year 1503, two years before his death, Robert and Isabella moved to Darlaston Manor. Robert and his son Thurston leased Darlaston Manor, in the county of Staffordshire, from Thomas Whally, then in 1537, Robert's son, Jam
2 CONC es purchased the manor from Richard Whalley.
2 CONT Except for a brief time Robert's great grandson, James Collier, sold the manor to his father-in-law in 1597 and until his son Francis re-purchased it from his grandfather in 1597, there was a Collier (Coleire) as Lord of Darlaston
2 CONC  over 180 years, until a great-great-great-great-grandson, James Collier (Coleire) sold it to William Jervis in 1685.
2 CONT [Source: Anne Laidley Collier, 210 West Main Street , Uniontown, PA Chapter did an genealogy study of relatives that served in the Revolutionary War for the DAR Chapter GREAT MEADOWS and it was forward to: Daughters of the America
2 CONC n Revolution, Washington, D. C. It was based on Col. Charles Swan service during the Revolutionary War.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Additionally, there was also this information posted to the same forum from Donna Colyer Hunt:
2 CONT The Dictionary of English Surnames gives us the following: COLIAR, COLIER, COLIERE, COLLIAR, COLLIER, COLLEER, COLLYER, COLYEAR, COLYEAR, COLYER formed from "col" a derivative of coal, a maker or seller of charcoal in ancient time
2 CONC s (a1375 MED).
2 CONT [Source - Dictionary of English Surnames 1995 pub Oxford Press by P H Reaney. ]
2 CONT [a1375 MED - Middle English Dictionary ed H Kurah, SM Kuhn and J Reidy, Ann Arbor 1954]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes -
2 CONT 1.Being a referred to by your occupation was a common practice in early times, This was a migratory occupation, workers moved along with their jobs, much like many of us today, Movement from one coal producing area to another, per
2 CONC haps leaving relatives along the way, created pockets of surname. Many of these pockets survived, multiplied and give us the origins of our personal lines today.
2 CONT 2.From research done by Ronald Collier, (Appalachian Genealogist, Cumberland, KY) England, Ireland, Wales and Scotland certainly had coal producing areas. The names survive in many modern families, particularily in England. COLYEA
2 CONC R was the most common in Scotland where the family was a branch of the powerful and famous Robertson Clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some examples from the Dictionary of English Surnames with sources and notes from the editors.-
2 CONT RANUFF COLLIER at 1150 (L) found in Lincolnshire
2 CONT [Source: FM Stenton, Documents illustrative of the Social and Economic History of the Danelaw, London England 1920]
2 CONT BERNARD LE COLIERE, 1172 SO Somerset
2 CONT [Source: (P) Pipe Rolls: Record Commission. 3 vol, London 1833-44: Pipe Roll Soc (in progress); The Great Roll of the Pipe for the 26th year of Henry the Third, ed. H L Cannon, Yale Hist, Pub 1918.]
2 CONT In Ireland, the first appearance of the name COLLIER is for Colliertown in County Meath 1305. The name also extended into Carlow Kilkenny and Wexford Counties.
2 CONT In France: COLLIER developed fram a place name CAULLIERS and the noble French family of COLYEAR DE PORTMORE of Ecosse had a Scottish heritage.
2 CONT The German version KOHLER, KOLLAR and KOLLER developed also from the place of Kohler.
2 CONT The earliest COLLIER in America appears to be WILLIAM, appearing on tax records 1633 and SARAH COLLIER married Love Brewster in 1634 in Plymouth, MA. Love Brewsters' father was William, the Captain of the Mayflower which landed at
2 CONC  Plymouth Rock, MA 1620.
2 CONT From 1620 until today, COLLIER remains an active surname. Colyer is less active with approximately 1000 hits in the 1990 (US) census. COLLYER is even more rare.
2 CONT [https://robotwars101.org/thecollierfamily/name.html]
