1 NAME James /Leveson/
2 GIVN James
2 SURN Leveson
1 NAME James Leveson, of Lilleshall
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1502
2 ADDR
3 CITY Prestwood
3 STAE England
1 BURI
2 DATE OCT 1545
2 PLAC St Peter's Collegiate Churchyard
2 ADDR
3 CITY Wolverhampton
3 CTRY England
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 13 OCT 1545
2 ADDR
3 CITY Wolverhampton
3 STAE England
1 OCCU Merchant of the Staple of Calais, Merchant of London
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT '''James Leveson of Lilleshall''', a wealthy Wolverhampton businessman, one of the Merchants of the Staple who had the monopoly of wool exports from England. Leveson had made a fortune not only from trade but also from leasing the
2 CONC  property of St Peter's Collegiate Church in Wolverhampton: in 1550, James's cousin John and Robert Brooke took on most of the college property at fixed low rents on perpetual leases - a ruse by the prebendaries to profit doubly f
2 CONC rom the dissolution of the institution. James Leveson had bought Lilleshall Abbey, a dissolved Augustinian house in Shropshire in 1539,  and thereafter it became the seat of his branch of the Leveson family.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference in "Donington Church and Lordship," by H. F. J. Vaughan, in Transactions of the Shropshire Archaeological and Natural History Society, vol. 6, publ. 1883, p. 16, states: 
2 CONT
2 CONT James Leveson of Wolverhampton, merchant of the Staple, and brother of Nicholas, married twice. By his first wife (a daughter of Wrottesley) he had issue Sir Richard of Lilleshall, Edward of Perton, and Walter, and a daughter, Joa
2 CONC n, wife, firstly of Skeffington, and secondly of Fowke; by his second, Margaret, daughter of William Offley, he had Elizabeth, wife of Walter Aston of Tixall ..., and Joyce, obt. 1608, wife of Sir John Giffard of Chillington, obt.
2 CONC  1611, ...
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
2 CONT Levesons were active in wool trade in Wolverhampton since ca 1354. From: http://www.localhistory.scit.wlv.ac.uk/articles/electronic/wool.htm   (broken link)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Retrieved from https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Leveson-6 and then edited
2 CONT
2 CONT James was born in 1502 the son of Richard Leveson of Prestwood and Joan Bradbury
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT He married firstly Alice Wrottesley daughter of Sir Richard Wrottesley of Wrottesley.
2 CONT
2 CONT Together they had children (See research notes)
2 CONT
2 CONT # Richard Leveson
2 CONT # Joan Leveson
2 CONT # Walter Leveson
2 CONT # Edward Leveson
2 CONT
2 CONT He married secondly Margaret Offley.  She married . firstly Thomas Michell, of London, Ironmonger, " One of the richest Commoners within the City of London," but by him had no issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married secondly, James Leveson, merchant of the Staple at Callice. They had issue —
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT #Joyce Leveson, b. abt 1533, d. 1608. Married John Giffard.
2 CONT #Elizabeth Leveson, mar. at Wolverhampton (Sir) Walter Aston, son and heir of Sir Edward Aston of Tixall, Knight, bur. at. Wolverhampton who had
2 CONT ##Sir Edward Aston, J. P. for Stafford and Leicester, died 1597.
2 CONT ##Robert Aston, Recorder of Stafford.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1536 Named along with his wife Margaret in the Will of brother Nicholas Leveson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his brother Nicholas, were successful wool merchants. Nicholas moved from Wolverhampton to London and became a Merchant of the Staple. James remained in Wolverhampton where he invested in property.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1528 he began systematically purchase land in the South Staffordshire area and the dissolution of the monasteries gave him further impetus to establish a landed dynasty. Between 1537 and 1540 over 20000 acres were added to hi
2 CONC s portfolio.[1] [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1540 he purchased the priory of Trentham[3]
2 CONT * c1538 Wombridge Priory[4]
2 CONT * 1543 Bought the manor of Lilleshaw from the Crown.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also eager to trade in the effects of the monasteries and after John Scudamore the “Receiver General of dissolved possessions” made available the contents of the priories of Staffordshire in 1538, James made many purchases.
2 CONC [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT He died a very rich man on 23 Oct 1545
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughter of Richard Wrottesley, who married James Leveson, was named Alice, and not Margery or Margaret. She was his first wife, and mother of his three sons. Sir Richard Leveson, Walter Leveson and Edward Leveson, of Perton. 
2 CONC See a note to the Pedigree of Offley, printed in The Genealogiaty New Series, vol. xix. (April, 1903.)[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT In his will James mentions his first wife Alice.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Leveson was a Wolverhampton wool merchant. He bought Lilleshall Abbey in 1539 and the following year, Trentham Priory, followed by the manor of Lilleshall in 1543. He passed away in 1547.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
