1 NAME Andrew /Corbet/ MP
2 GIVN Andrew
2 SURN Corbet
2 NSFX MP
2 _MARNM Corbet
1 NAME Sir Andrew Corbet, MP
1 SEX M
1 OCCU unknown
2 PLAC Sheriff of Shropshire
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 2 NOV 1521
2 PLAC Moreton Corbet, Shropshire, England
2 ADDR
1 BURI
2 DATE 16 AUG 1578
2 ADDR
3 CITY Moreton Corbet
3 STAE Shropshire
3 CTRY UK
1 DEAT
2 DATE 16 AUG 1578
2 PLAC Moreton Corbet, Shropshire, England
2 ADDR
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Sir Andrew Corbet===
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Andrew is the mutual great grandfather between Harry Duke of Sussex and his wife Meghan Duchess of Sussex, making them 13th Cousins to each other.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage/Family: Corbet married Jane, the daughter of Sir Robert Needham of Shavington Hall, Shropshire, with whom he had six sons and five daughters. They included: Robert Corbet (died 1583), Sir Andrew's heir and successor, who 
2 CONC had already represented Shropshire in the parliament of 1563, and who died of plague while visiting his uncle Walter Corbet in London. Richard Corbet (died 1606), a future MP for Shropshire, who succeeded to Sir Andrew's estates o
2 CONC n the death of his elder brother. Sir Vincent Corbet, Sir Andrew's youngest son, inherited the family estates on the death of his brother Richard. Sir Andrew Corbet, Sir Vincent's son, was a prominent Shropshire MP. Sir Vincent Co
2 CONC rbet, 1st Baronet, the second Sir Andrew's son, was a Royalist soldier of the English Civil War Richard Corbet, another of the second Sir Andrew's sons, was also a prominent Royalist soldier. Anne Corbet, who was the first wife of
2 CONC  Sir Walter Leveson, a wealthy Shropshire and Staffordshire landowner and MP who engaged in piracy and later became mentally incapacitated.[26] Richard Leveson (died 1605), their son and Sir Andrew's grandson, was a distinguished 
2 CONC Elizabethan seaman who rose to the rank of Vice-Admiral.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and Education b. 1 Nov. 1522, 1st s. of Roger Corbet† of Moreton Corbet by Anne, da. of Sir Andrew Windsor†, 1st Lord Windsor; bro. of Jerome. m. Jane, da. of Sir Robert Needham of Shavington, Salop, 6s. inc. Richard Corbet
2 CONC  II and Robert 5da. suc. fa. 1538. Kntd. Oct. 1547.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT Escheator, Salop 1546-7; j.p. Salop 1547-58, Glos., Herefs., Worcs. 1554, q. Salop from 1561; sheriff, Salop 1550-1, 1555-6, 1569-70; commr. relief 1550, goods of churches and fraternities 1553, loan 1558, musters 1562; member, co
2 CONC uncil in the marches of Wales 1553, v.-pres. Aug. 1575-Aug. 1577; steward, Ruyton Patria, Salop by 1566; lt. Salop 1569.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Corbet was a leading Shropshire landowner, who by 1578 owned property in 40 parishes in the county, in addition to manors in Bedfordshire, Buckinghamshire, Cornwall, Essex, Herefordshire and elsewhere. He was a minor whe
2 CONC n his father died, and in July 1539 his wardship was granted to an uncle, Richard Corbet I; he received livery of his lands in November 1543. Before the end of Henry VIII’s reign he had added to his already extensive estates by bu
2 CONC ying from the Crown for over £550 the manor of Redcastle, Shropshire, with other lands in the county. He leased further Shropshire property under Mary, and at some time acquired houses in Shrewsbury. He lived mainly at Moreton Cor
2 CONC bet, rebuilt in the Italian style by his sons Robert and Richard. In 1561 Sir Andrew sold a considerable amount of his Essex property, including the manor of Woodham Mortimer.3
2 CONT
2 CONT His long career as an official was uninterrupted by the religious changes of the period. In 1555 he opposed a major government bill and did not sit in Mary’s last Parliament. The bishops’ letters to the Privy Council in 1564 descr
2 CONC ibed him as sound in religion, and as one whose advice had been taken on the religious position of justices of the peace. During the Norfolk plot Thomas Ashton, headmaster of Shrewsbury school, told Cecil that Corbet, though seeki
2 CONC ng quietness and loving ‘to have no dealing in things’, was
2 CONT
2 CONT the only staid man, most secret, true and faithfullest to his prince ... in all these parts of the realm ... the fittest man for a charge wherein consisteth the stay of the country. He allowed the parishioners of Moreton Corbet to
2 CONC  choose their own pastor, William Axton, a Cambridge contemporary of Cartwright.4
2 CONT
2 CONT Corbet was knighted early in Edward VI’s reign for service against the Scots. In 1560 he was in command of 200 men sent to Berwick, and in June that year was present at a banquet with the French in Scotland. He took an active inte
2 CONC rest in his local county musters: in August 1562 he submitted a report on the state of armour in Shropshire, and a number of the visits he made to Shrewsbury, where the town records refer to wine and other presents given to him, w
2 CONC ere connected with the musters.5
2 CONT
2 CONT He was apparently an energetic and successful official. It is significant that he was the only Shropshire recipient of the Privy Council letter of 1571 urging ‘good choice to be made of knights, citizens and burgesses of the parli
2 CONC ament’ in the county. A letter survives from him to the bailiffs of Shrewsbury, appointing a date early in March for the election of burgesses ‘in pursuance of letters from the Queen’. In September of the same year he wrote again,
2 CONC  apologising for not being able to help the corporation over the collection of the subsidy, as he was occupied with the arrest of Lawrence Banester. In addition to his other offices in Shropshire, Corbet served between 1573 and 15
2 CONC 78 as a commissioner for oyer and terminer, for sewers, and for the survey of tanning houses. He was one of a number of government officials appointed as feoffees of the property by the will of the 1st Earl of Essex, who died in 1
2 CONC 576.6
2 CONT
2 CONT By January 1574 he was signing orders as a member of the council in the marches of Wales, and in the summer of 1575 Sir Henry Sidney, before his departure for Ireland, invested him as vice-president at Shrewsbury. There was some o
2 CONC pposition to the appointment, William Gerard complaining that Corbet was ‘a very sickly man, not able to take the toil of that service’. In fact, he proved to be a vigorous and efficient administrator who made a determined effort 
2 CONC to put down corruption among the minor officials. In 1577 the Queen accepted his resignation as vice-president ‘in consideration of the illness of your body and to give you some relief after your travail, although we could be well
2 CONC  content to have used you longer in our service’. She asked him to come to her as soon as possible, to discuss the state of the marches.7
2 CONT
2 CONT During Corbet’s period as vice-president there was plague in Shrewsbury, and he did what he could to ensure supplies of grain. When he died, intestate, on 16 Aug. 1578, the town chronicle described him as ‘such a jewel to all Shro
2 CONC pshire that the like was not for many years before, for the loss of whom there was many a sorrowful heart, especially in the town of Salop’. He was buried at Moreton Corbet, administration of the estate being granted on 22 Aug. to
2 CONC  his son Robert, and a new grant being made on Robert’s death in 1583.8
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1558-1603 Author: N. M. Fuidge Notes 1. C142/60/119; Vis. Salop (Harl. Soc. xxviii), 136; Trans. Salop Arch. Soc. (ser. 3), vii. 388 seqq.; A. E. C[orbet], Fam. of Corbet, passim. 2. CPR , 1547-8, p. 88; 1553-4, pp. 1
2 CONC 9-20, 23, 25; 1553 and App. Edw. VI, pp. 358, 415; Trans. Salop Arch. Soc. (ser. 2), ii. 221; lii. 116; P. H. Williams, Council in the Marches of Wales, 347; Flenley, Cal. Reg. Council, Marches of Wales, 111, 144, 173; HMC Hatfiel
2 CONC d, i. 443. 3. C142/60/114-23; 187/56; LP Hen. VIII, xiv(1), pp. 590-1; xviii(2), p. 243; xxi(1), p. 145; HMC 15th Rep. X, 75; J. B. Blakeway, Sheriffs of Salop, 230; CPR, 1560-3, p. 137. 4. Guildford Mus. Loseley 1331/2; Cam. Misc
2 CONC . ix(3), p. 44; CSP Dom. 1547-80, p. 426; P. Collinson, Eliz. Puritan Movement, 139, 337. 5. CSP Scot. 1547-63, pp. 161, 438; CSP Dom. 1547-80, pp. 164, 339; CSP For. 1560-1, p. 133; Trans. Salop Arch. Soc. (ser. 2), ii. 221, 231;
2 CONC  CPR, 1553 and App. Edw. VI, pp. 358, 415; APC, vi. 192; H. Owen and J. B. Blakeway, Shrewsbury, i. 350, 360. 6. Add. 48018, f. 294; HMC 15th Rep. X, 50, 51; APC, viii. 124; Flenley, 105, 125; HMC 10th Rep. IV, 443; Lansd. 23, ff.
2 CONC  152 seq. 7. Flenley, 14, 25, 111, 144, 173 et passim. 8. HMC 15th Rep. X, 52; Owen and Blakeway, i. 370; C142/60/114; 187/56; Flenley, 173 n.; Early Chrons. Shrewsbury, ed. Leighton, 280; Trans. Salop Arch. Soc. (ser. 2), ii. 226
2 CONC ; PCC admon. act bk. 1578, f. 152.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
