1 NAME Ralph /Assheton/
2 GIVN Ralph
2 SURN Assheton
2 _MARNM Assheton
2 NICK Ralph Ashton of Middleton
1 NAME Maj. Gen. Ralph Assheton
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC St. Leonard’s 
2 ADDR
3 CITY Middleton
3 STAE England
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1596
2 PLAC Of
2 ADDR
3 CITY Middleton
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 FEB 1651
2 ADDR
3 CITY Middleton
3 STAE England
1 OCCU C-in-C Parliamentary Forces, Lancashire, Gentleman
1 TITL Maj. Gen.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ralph Assheton (1596 – 17 February 1650) was an English politician who sat in the House of Commons from 1640 to 1649. He was a general in the Parliamentary army in the English Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ralph_Assheton_(general)_
2 CONT
2 CONT Assheton, Maj-Gen. Ralph (1606-51). Second but eldest surviving son of Richard Assheton (c.1578-1618) and his wife Mary, daughter of Thomas Venables of Kinderton (Cheshire), born 31 March and baptised 6 April 1606. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He refused a knighthood in 1631 and was MP for Lancashire, 1640-48 (when he was excluded for opposing the trial and execution of the King). He was a strong Presbyterian and one of the leaders of the Parliamentary party in Lancashi
2 CONC re, and was made a Deputy Lieutenant and Col. of the Lancashire Levies by Parliament in 1642. He was early in the field at the commencement of the Civil War, preventing the King's Commissioners from seizing Manchester in June 1642
2 CONC , and sending 150 of his tenants in arms to defend the town in September that year. Parliament allowed him a "small brass piece" (i.e. a cannon) for the defence of Middleton Hall in November 1642, indicating that he was recognised
2 CONC  as a military target.  He was Colonel in command of the 500 men who guarded Bolton against the attacks of Lord Derby's forces in February 1643, and defeated Lord Derby at Whalley, April 1643. He drove the Royalists out of Wigan, 
2 CONC 22 April 1643, before going on to capture Liverpool, Hornby Castle and Thurland Castle in May and June, 1643. He was surprised and overpowered by Lord Byron, near Middlewich (Cheshire) in December 1643 but escaped and took part a 
2 CONC few days later in the relief of Nantwich, being particularly praised by Fairfax. He took part in the first siege of Lathom, Feb-May 1644, and was raised to the rank of Major-General and made commander of all the Parliamentary forc
2 CONC es in Lancashire. In April 1643 he was appointed to the committee for sequestering delinquents' estates. In the second Civil War he commanded the Lancashire forces against the Duke of Hamilton, June 1648, and he and his men were p
2 CONC raised by Cromwell for their gallantry at the battle of Preston, August 1648; he later relieved Cockermouth and took Appleby, October 1648. His disbanded troops mutinied at Clitheroe in March 1649. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 4 May 1623 at Woodsome (Yorks WR), Elizabeth (c.1608-62), daughter of John Kaye esq. of Woodsome and had issue, with other children who died young:
2 CONT
2 CONT * (1) Richard Assheton (c.1625-30), born about 1625; died in the lifetime of his father, 25 March 1630, supposedly as the result of witchcraft, for which one Utley was hanged at Lancaster; buried at Middleton, 27 March 1630;
2 CONT * (2) Sir Ralph Assheton (1626-65), kt. and 1st bt. (q.v.);
2 CONT * (3) John Assheton (d. 1678) of Burne (Yorks) and Middleton; married, 1 September 1663 at Penwortham (Lancs), Elizabeth, daughter of John Fleetwood of Penwortham and had issue two daughters; buried at Middleton, August 1678;
2 CONT * (4) Elizabeth Assheton (b. 1628), born 16 March 1627/8; married, 1651, Adam Beaumont (d. by 1668), son of Sir Thomas Beaumont of Whitley Beaumont (Yorks WR), and had issue one son and two daughters;
2 CONT * (5) Mary Assheton (1636-80), born 7 December and baptised 15 December 1636; married Christopher Banastre (c.1635-90) of Bank Hall, Bretherton (Lancs) and had issue two daughters; buried at Croston (Lancs), 20 October 1680;
2 CONT * (6) Anne Assheton; died unmarried .
2 CONT
2 CONT He inherited the Middleton Hall estate from his father in 1618. He died 17 February 1650/1 and was buried at Middleton where he is commemorated by the only brass to record a Civil War soldier in full armour. His widow died 22 Marc
2 CONC h 1662.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
