1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will
2 CONT
2 CONT Selections from http://www.oxford-shakespeare.com/Probate/PROB_11-43-645.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT SUMMARY: The document below is the last will and testament, dated 13 March 1559 (will of lands) and 1 August 1560 (will of goods) and proved 20 December 1560, of '''Dionyse (nee Bodley) Leveson''' (d. 2 December 1560), grandmother
2 CONC  of William Leveson (d.1621), who acted as trustee to the Lord Chamberlain’s Men, including William Shakespeare of Stratford upon Avon, in the allocation of shares in the ground lease of the Globe Theatre in 1599.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The testatrix was the daughter of Thomas Bodley (d.1492) and Joan Leche (c.1450-1530), daughter of Denis Leche of Wellingborough, Northamptonshire, and his wife, Elizabeth. Joan Leche’s brother, John Leche, was vicar of Saffron Wa
2 CONC lden from 1489-1521. For the will of the testatrix’ father, Thomas Bodley, see TNA PROB 11/9/374.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of the testatrix’ father, the testatrix’ mother married Sir Thomas Bradbury (d.1510), Lord Mayor of London in 1509, by whom she had no issue. For the will of Sir Thomas Bradbury, see TNA PROB 11/16/90. For the test
2 CONC atrix’ mother, Joan (nee Leche) Bodley Bradbury (d.1530), see her will, TNA PROB 11/23/272, and the Wikipedia article edited by the author of this website.
2 CONT
2 CONT The testatrix married Nicholas Leveson (c.1490 - 20 August 1539), the son of Richard Leveson (d.1503) of Prestwood and Jane Bradbury, who appears to have been the sister of the testatrix’ stepfather, Sir Thomas Bradbury (d.1510), 
2 CONC Lord Mayor of London, 
2 CONT
2 CONT See the Sutherland Collection at the Staffordshire Record Office: https://www.search.sutherlandcollection.org.uk/search.aspx?SearchType=2&ThemeID=1
2 CONT 32
2 CONT
2 CONT See also the Leveson pedigree in ‘Star Chamber Proceedings Henry VIII and Edward IV’, Collections for a History of Staffordshire, (London: Harrison and Sons, 1912), pp. 1-206 at p. 34A:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/stream/collectionsforhi03staf_3#page/n57/mode/2up
2 CONT
2 CONT See also the will of Nicholas Leveson, TNA PROB 11/27/552.
2 CONT
2 CONT By Nicholas Leveson, the testatrix had eight sons and ten daughters. In his own will, Nicholas Leveson mentions four sons, John, Thomas, Nicholas and William, and five daughters, Grisell, Joan (or Jane), Alice, Mary and Dionyse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children include:
2 CONT * 1. Joann LEVERSON b: ABT 1518 in London, England
2 CONT * 2. Grisell LEVERSON b: ABT 1520 in London, England
2 CONT * 3. Alice Elizabeth Nicholson LEVERSON b: ABT 1523 in Halling Kent, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT * 4. Mary LEVERSON b: ABT 1522 in London, England
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_Leche
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan Leche (c. 1450 – March 1530), benefactress, was the wife successively of Thomas Bodley, and of Thomas Bradbury, Lord Mayor of London in 1509. ... 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dionysia Bodley (d.1561), the youngest daughter.[8] She married the citizen and Mercer Nicholas Leveson (d.1539), second son of Richard Leveson Esquire of Prestwood, Staffordshire (d.1503) by his wife Jane Bradbury. (Leveson and h
2 CONC is younger brother James (d. 1547)[9] were leading merchants of London and Wolverhampton.) They had eight sons and ten daughters,[10] including at least three sets of twins.[citation needed] Of these,
2 CONT
2 CONT *John Leveson, eldest son, was killed in Kett's Rebellion.[11]
2 CONT *Thomas Leveson (1532-1576), second son,[12] married Ursula Gresham (1534-1574), daughter of Sir John Gresham (c.1495–1556), Lord Mayor.[13] Of their children,
2 CONT **Sir John Leveson (1555–1615) was instrumental in putting down the Essex rebellion of 8 February 1601[14][15]
2 CONT **William Leveson (d.1621) was one of two trustees used by William Kempe, Thomas Pope, Augustine Phillips, John Heminges and William Shakespeare (1564-1616) to allocate shareholdings in the Globe Theatre in 1599.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Bodley left an estate of £1000, of which his wife, Joan, was entitled to a third, and his children another third, according to the custom of the City of London.[17] Sutton considers it likely that she continued to run her l
2 CONC ate husband's business.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
