2 DATE 14 MAR 1609
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===BIography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Leveson-50
2 CONT
2 CONT Joyce was born about 1533. She is the daughter of James Leveson and Margery Offley.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married John Giffard
2 CONT
2 CONT A tomb at Brewood Church with effigies of John Giffard (d. 1615) and Joyce (Leveson), his wife (d. 1608), has an arcaded base bearing the figures of eight sons, five of them in armour, and six daughters. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ______
2 CONT John Giffard (1534–1613) was a Staffordshire landowner and Member of the English Parliament, notable as a leader of Roman Catholic Recusancy in the reigns of Elizabeth I and James I.
2 CONT Background and early life
2 CONT John Giffard's father was Sir Thomas Giffard of Caverswall Castle. The Giffards had their seat at Chillington Hall, near Brewood, from the late 12th century.[1] Sir Thomas, like his father, Sir John Giffard, had considerably expan
2 CONC ded the family estates until they were the wealthiest landed gentry family in Staffordshire. Sir John was still alive when his grandson John was born, so Thomas Giffard was living at Caverswall, which he had acquired through his f
2 CONC irst wife, the heiress Dorothy Montgomery. Both Sir John and Sir Thomas were MPs of religiously conservative disposition, although both had generally acquiesced in the legislation that carried through the English Reformation.
2 CONT John Giffard's mother was Ursula Throckmorton, daughter of Robert Throckmorton of Coughton Court, Warwickshire, and Elizabeth Baynham. She was Thomas Giffard's second wife: Dorothy had died by 1529, leaving Thomas with a daughter,
2 CONC  but no surviving sons. He married Ursula in 1529. She was part of a wealthy landowning family, generally of a similar religious conservative outlook to the Giffards. Her brother, George Throckmorton, was MP for Warwickshire in th
2 CONC e English Reformation Parliament, elected in 1529. His sympathies were strongly Catholic and he was arrested in 1537, in the aftermath of the Pilgrimage of Grace, with which he was thought to sympathise. He made a rambling and con
2 CONC fused confession of his part in the Catholic opposition, narrowly escaping with his life.[2]
2 CONT In 1539, when John was still a child, Thomas Giffard bought the site of Black Ladies Priory, a dissolved Benedictine nunnery near Brewood.[3] Shortly after, the family moved into the house,[4] which Thomas had rebuilt as fine Tudo
2 CONC r brick residence, set on a moated site, with fishponds. At about the age of 16, John Giffard married Joyce Leveson, and their first child, Walter, was born about a year into the marriage. By the age of 21, he was considered ready
2 CONC  for parliament.[5]
2 CONT Marriage and family
2 CONT John Giffard married Joyce Leveson on 10 April 1550. she was the daughter of James Leveson of Lilleshall, a wealthy Wolverhampton businessman, one of the Merchants of the Staple who had the monopoly of wool exports from England. L
2 CONC eveson had made a fortune not only from trade but also from leasing the property of St Peter's Collegiate Church in Wolverhampton:[14] in 1550, James's cousin John and Robert Brooke took on most of the college property at fixed lo
2 CONC w rents on perpetual leases – a ruse by the prebendaries to profit doubly from the dissolution of the institution. James Leveson had bought Lilleshall Abbey, a dissolved Augustinian house in Shropshire in 1539,[15] and thereafter 
2 CONC it became the seat of his branch of the Leveson family.
2 CONT John Giffard and Joyce had at least 14[5] surviving children.
2 CONT These included eight sons:[4]
2 CONT *Walter – John's heir and successor
2 CONT *Richard
2 CONT *Thomas
2 CONT *Gilbert – the spy, who died in Paris, 1590
2 CONT *Giles
2 CONT *George – a priest, who died in 1585
2 CONT *Gerard
2 CONT *Edward
2 CONT There were also six daughters:
2 CONT *Mary – who married Robert Brooke of Lapley, Staffordshire
2 CONT *Cassandra – who married Thomas Cassey, of Whitfield, Gloucestershire
2 CONT *Ursula – who married John Wakeman, of Beckford, Gloucs.
2 CONT *Frances – who married Edmund Powell, of Sandford, Oxfordshire
2 CONT *Jane – who married Sir John Dormer, of Dorton, Oxfordshire
2 CONT *Dorothy – who married Sir Walter Leveson, of Wolverhampton
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Giffard_(died_1613)
2 CONT _____________
