2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/74721378/bethiah-chapin
2 CONT
2 CONT Bethia was the mother of thirteen children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lydia was the daughter of Capt. Seth Chapin and Bethia Thurston. America's First Legal Woman Voter
2 CONT
2 CONT Lydia Chapin Taft was an early forerunner in Colonial America who was allowed to vote in three New England town meetings, beginning in 1756. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lydia and her siblings are listed as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT Seth, jun , b. Medfield, July 2, 1692; one of our most conspicuous early citi-
2 CONT
2 CONT zens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bethiah, b., place not given, Feb. 16, 1693; m. Jonathan Thayer, jun., Oct.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1, 1714.
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah, b., place not given, March 1, 1695-96; untraced.
2 CONT
2 CONT John, b., place not given, May 13, 169S; always res. here; dea. of the ch., etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary, b. hereabouts April 30, 1700; m. Robert Taft.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel, b. hereabouts June 2, 1702; not well traced.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deborah, b. hereabouts June 14, 1704; m. Nathaniel Nelson, April 15, 1725.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hopestill, b. hereabouts Nov. 27, 1715; m. the 2d Dr. John Corbett of Bellingham, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph, b. hereabouts March 6, 1707; m. Mary Nelson, Feb. 5, 1729.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abigail, b. hereabouts June 10, 1710; untraced.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lydia, b. hereabouts Feb. 2, 1712; m. Josiah Taft.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin, b. hereabouts April 6, 1713; m. Sarah French, Feb. 23, 1732-33.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ebexezeb, b. hereabouts Dec. 23, 1714; lived and d. in old Mendon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Japheth, b. hereabouts Feb. 24, 1716; d. April 15, 1717. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bethia is the wife of Seth Chapin and the mother (mr.) mentioned below:
2 CONT
2 CONT The mr. d. March 2, 1744; the fr. in April, 1746. It appears from the old 
2 CONT
2 CONT proprietary records that Capt. Seth Chapin had acquired a family homestead 
2 CONT
2 CONT and domicile near the " Post Lane " bridge on Mill River some time previous to 
2 CONT
2 CONT May 26, 1700; for at that date he had the following-described parcel of land 
2 CONT
2 CONT laid out to him: "Forty-five acres of the 4th division laid out to Seth Chapin, 
2 CONT
2 CONT and in possession of said Chapin. encompassing the said Chapin's homestead 
2 CONT
2 CONT and meadow upon the Mill River," etc. (See Part I. of this work, Chap. III. 
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 38.) He went on adding parcel after parcel to his real estate, till he became 
2 CONT
2 CONT the owner of several hundred a?res on our territory. In 1713 he and his wf. 
2 CONT
2 CONT made a gift-deed of 60 acres to their son Seth, jun., situated in what we now 
2 CONT
2 CONT call So. Hopedale. Aug. 31, 1715, they sold and deeded to Josiah Wood, for- 
2 CONT
2 CONT merly of Concord, their homestead, consisting of 150 acres, with the buildings 
2 CONT
2 CONT thereon. They then removed to Mendon Town, to live thenceforth with or near 
2 CONT
2 CONT his venerable fr., Josiah Chapin, Esq. There, while fulfilling his filial duties 
2 CONT
2 CONT with exemplary fidelity, he was welcomed to the front rank of official dignity, 
2 CONT
2 CONT and finally closed a life of 78 yrs. with very similar manifestations of public 
2 CONT
2 CONT confidence and respect to those enjoyed by his honored father. 
