1 NAME Ephraim /Cutter/ I
2 GIVN Ephraim
2 SURN Cutter
2 NSFX I
2 _MARNM Cutter
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 5 SEP 1651
2 PLAC Charlestown
2 ADDR
3 CITY Boston
3 STAE Massachusetts
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 SEP 1731
2 PLAC Watertown Farms
2 ADDR
3 CITY (now Weston)
3 STAE Massachusetts
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 OCCU glazier
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A History of the Cutter family of New England pp 30-31
2 CONT
2 CONT  Ephraim, b. 1651, and baptized at Cambridge, was a glazier by trade, and resided successively in Cambridge, Charlestown, and Watertown Farms, now Weston. Feb. 11. 1678—9, he married Bethia Wood, probably daughter of Nicholas and 
2 CONC Mary (Williams) Wood, of Medfield, and born July 28, 1660. While resident at Charlestown he drew pay as an officer in Philip's War, and was probably the " Captain Cutler " who commanded a company in the expedition sent to " range 
2 CONC the woods towards Hassanamesit," now Grafton, April 27, 1676. The forces comprising this expedition " succeeded in capturing small parties of the enemy, among whom were some of'considerable rank;' but the season proving rainy, and
2 CONC  many being sick, after a short campaign the troops were released ' for the recovery of their health,' and returned for the most part to Boston." According to tradition he once commanded in a fight with Indians at Fort Hill, in Th
2 CONC ompson, Conn.*
2 CONT
2 CONT March 19, 1678-9, Ephraim Cutter, " of Cambridge, glazier," purchased of Jonathan Bunker, " planter," one and a half acres meadow in Menotomy; his brother William Cutter being a witness of the deed. June 12, 1680, Ephraim Cutter, 
2 CONC "of Charlestown," bought of Samuel Andrews, " marriner," for <£26, a house with land
2 CONT
2 CONT * Morse. Gyneul. Xante Cutler, 45; Barry, Hist. Mass. i. 412; Trumbull, Indian Wars, 61.
2 CONT
2 CONT in Cambridge.* His eldest son, Ephraim, was born in Charlestown the following August; and he appears first at Watertown, Aug. 18, 1G89. when three of his children were baptized.
2 CONT
2 CONT June 23, 1682, he sold a "parcel of meadow" in Ciiarlestown on " Mcnotomyes river," to his father Richard. April 9, 1703, ho bought of Daniel Child, for £50, two acres of land with a mansion house, barn, and orchard, at Watcrtown.
2 CONC t July 6, 1696, he sold a 41 homestead of ten acres," with nine acres meadow, and a farm of one hundred acres in Watcrtown to his brother William Cutter.X The " Cutter Farm." located in Weston, Ephraim and his brother sold to Will
2 CONC iam Williams, "clerk," Dec. 3, 1713. Ephraim, Jr., and John Cutter (William's son) were witnesses of the sale.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ephraim Cutter is mentioned in his father's and brother William's wills. The date of his decease is unknown. His wife was interred, in Watcrtown.
