1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biography== 
2 CONT Samuel Fisher was born 29 Sep 1685 in Medfield, Norfolk, Massachusetts son of John Fisher 1661-1755 and Mary Medcalf.[1][2][3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1690 during the expedition to Canada under Captain Andrew Gardner 60 men were lost. On 4 Jun 1736 the House of Representatives of Massachusetts constructed a list of heirs of these fallen soldiers to receive land grants in cons
2 CONC ideration of the loss to their families. The Grant known as Gardner's Canada would become incorporated later as Warwick. In the third list of grantees/heirs in the proprietors records we find "Samuel Fisher of Wrentham, eldest mal
2 CONC e heir to Samuel Metcalf." Samuel Metcalf was Samuel Fisher's brother-in-law. In other words Samuel Metcalf was the brother of Samuel Fisher's wife Mary (Metcalf) Fisher and son of Deacon Thomas Metcalf 1629 - 1702. (See Roster of
2 CONC  the Expedition of 1690 to Canada, NEHGR Vol 99 1945 p 307 - 314)[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT This information is extremely interesting in terms of the identity of Samuel Fisher's mother Mary (Metcalf) Fisher, wife of John Fisher. Mary Metcalf daughter of Deacon Thomas Metcalf was baptized in Dedham, Norfolk, Massachusetts
2 CONC  in 25 NOV 1660.("Mary ye daughter of of brother Thomas Metchalf was baptized 25 9 1660." p 35}[4] She was the only Mary Metcalf to have a brother Samuel Metcalf. Samuel Metcalf was the second of the name. He was born on 17 Sep 16
2 CONC 68 in Dedham, Norfolk, Massachusetts, and died in the expedition to Canada under Captain Andrew Gardner in 1690.[1] He had no children and hence his only heir was his brother-in-law Samuel Fisher who received the land grant descri
2 CONC bed above.[1] This brings up two errors frequently encountered: 1) This Samuel died in 1690 not in 30 JUN 1713. The Samuel who died on 30 JUN 1713 in Dedham therefore had to be the young son of Thomas 1671 - 1704 and Sarah Avery i
2 CONC .e. his nephew. 2) The wife of John Fisher was not the Mary Metcalf daughter of John Metcalf 1622 - 1675 who was born 2 Jun 1668 in Medfiled. ("Medcalfe, Mary, d. John and Mary, June 2, 1668." p 71)[5] That Mary is therefore most 
2 CONC certainly the Mary Metcalf who married Eleazar Ellis on on 26 Oct. 1687 in Medfield[5] because she had no brother Samuel so she could not have been the wife of John Fisher. These two errors are ubiquitous in the literature and on 
2 CONC the Internet so I encourage the reader to look at these sources themselves. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Fisher Married Mary Rockwood on 16 Dec 1707 in Wrentham, Massachusetts.[6][3] She was the daughter of daughter of Benjamin and Judith (Ellis) Rockwood of Wrentham, who was b Oct 2 1683 and d April 12 1763.[3] According to P
2 CONC hilip A Fisher he settled in Wrentham where all his children were born. “At his father's death in 1755 the ancestral place was given to him. He seems to have resided here (Medfield) for a short time but in 1758 he gave the place t
2 CONC o his son John.” 
2 CONT == Children == 
2 CONT # Samuel, b. Nov 25, 1709 m Ruth Wight Sept 9 1740[3] 
2 CONT # Mary, b. March 4, 1712; m. Samuel Hill of Medway, March 1, 1732 [7][3] 
2 CONT # Bathsheba, b. May 4, 1714; m. April 9, 1736, Elisha, son of Malachi and Bethia (Wight) Bullard[8][9][3] 
2 CONT # John, b. July 1, 1717 m Thankful Clark Feb 16 1749[3] 
2 CONT # Nathan, b. Aug 28 1720 m Abigail Harding Feb 7 1745[3] 
2 CONT # Elizabeth, b. Dec 17 1722[3] 
2 CONT # Patience b. Jan 15 1726, m John Holbrook, April 7 1747[10][11][3] 
2 CONT == Death and Will == 
2 CONT Samuel Fisher died on 19 Jan 1769 in Wrentham, Norfolk, Massachusetts.[1][12][3] His will was dated 17 Jun 1761, probated 24 Feb 1769 and his son John was executor. He names his father's widow and seven children including his four
2 CONC  daughters and sons Samuel, Nathan and John.[3] "To son John he gave land on Stop River near Noon Hill in Birch Street Plain in Medfield, he to pay Samuel 53L 6s 8d, the four daughters 5L each and his father's widow 4L. To son Sam
2 CONC uel he gave all his land in Medway, he to pay his father's widowed mother if alive 2L per annum. The son John was also to pay to Samuel a further sum of 26L 3s 4d. To the son Nathan he gave the remainder of his real estate, he to 
2 CONC pay to Samuel 26L 13s 4d and to his father's mother 2L."[3] 
2 CONT Biography and sources by [[https://www.geni.com/people/Roland-Henry-Baker-III/6000000028772339285 Roland Henry Baker, III]]
2 CONT == Sources == 
2 CONT
