1 NAME Sir Thomas Fitzherbert, MP, of Norbury
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1514
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 DEC 1591
1 OCCU Sheriff of Staffordshire
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Family and Education b. 1513/14, 1st surv. s. of Sir Anthony Fitzherbert of Norbury by Maud, da. and coh. of Richard Cotton of Hamstall Ridware; bro. of William. educ. G. Inn, adm. 1533. m. by Oct. 1537, Anne, da. 
2 CONC of Arthur Eyre of Padley, s.p. suc. fa. 26 May 1538. Kntd. 22 Feb. 1547.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT J.p. Staffs 1540-7, q. 1554-62; sheriff 1543-4, 1554-5; commr. array, Derbys. 1546, relief, Staffs. 1550, goods of churches and fraternities 1553.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Thomas Fitzherbert’s father, the eminent judge, inherited Norbury as a last surviving son in 1531, and Thomas Fitzherbert succeeded him there seven years later. Norbury lies on the Staffordshire border and it was with th
2 CONC at county, where he inherited Hamstall Ridware from his mother, that Fitzherbert was to be chiefly identified; his brother William was to marry into the leading Staffordshire family of Swynnerton.3
2 CONT
2 CONT Fitzherbert’s early years in public life did not foreshadow his later ostracism. His election to the last Henrician Parliament was a natural sequel to his first term as sheriff, and the knighthood and continued employment brought 
2 CONC by the new reign implied that he was as capable of adjusting to change as his father had been. Retained on the Staffordshire bench under Mary, and again pricked sheriff, he neither reappeared in Parliament nor received any further
2 CONC  mark of favour. Yet his rejection of the Elizabethan settlement, and the subversive activities of some of his younger kinsmen, were to bring him 30 years of persecution, long spells of imprisonment and finally death in the Tower 
2 CONC on 2 Oct. 1591. He was succeeded by his nephew and namesake, a Member in 1593, who with Archbishop Whitgift’s consent destroyed his uncle’s will disinheriting him.4
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1509-1558 Author: Alan Davidson Notes 1. Date of birth estimated from age at fa.’s i.p.m., C142/61/15. Derbys. Arch. Soc. Jnl. vii. 221-59; Wm. Salt Arch. Soc. iii(2), 73-74; VCH Derbys, ii. 27; PCC 20 Dyngeley. 2. LP
2 CONC  Hen. VIII, xv, xxi; CPR, 1547-8 to 1563-6 passim. 3. DNB (Fitzherbert, Sir Anthony); B. Camm, Forgotten Shrines, 22; SP12/183/58. 4. J. C. Wedgwood, Staffs. Parl. Hist. (Wm. Salt Arch. Soc.), i. 315; VCH Derbys. ii. 23; Derbys. A
2 CONC rch. Soc. Jnl. vii. 240; Cath. Rec. Soc. i. 49; xxi. 347; xxii. 88; lii. 6; Wm. Salt Arch. Soc. (ser. 3) 1915, p. 367; Lansd. 6(14), 56; CSP Dom. 1547-80, p. 372; 1581-90, pp. 280, 286, 365, 617; 1591-4, pp. 21, 136; APC, viii. 23
2 CONC 4; xiii-xviii passim; xx. 62, 100, 175, 207; SP12/183/58, 71(1), 184/61, 189/54; J. Morris, Troubles of our Catholic Forefathers, ii. 25; DNB (Fitzherbert, Nicholas and Thomas); Bodl. Tanner 118, ff. 129-31v; HMC Shrewsbury and Ta
2 CONC lbot, ii. 146, 150, 171, 194, 197, 200, 201, 307.
