1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From: Starkey, Thomas in The Oxford Dictionary of the Renaissance:
2 CONT English humanist and political theorist. Educated at Oxford, Starkey was in the early 1530s a member of Reginald Pole's household in Padua and Venice. In 1534, returning to London ...
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia:
2 CONT Starkey was born in Cheshire, probably at Wrenbury, to Thomas Starkey and Maud Mainwaring. His father likely held office in Wales and was wealthy enough to pay for his son's education. His mother, Maud, was a daughter of Sir John 
2 CONC Mainwaring, one of the wealthiest men in the palatinate.
2 CONT
2 CONT He attended the University of Oxford and gained an MA at Magdalen College in 1521. - 
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Starkey, Thomas	B.A. 30 June, 1516, M.A. 19 Feb., 1520-1, held a proctor's place by Wolsey's direction from May to Michaelmas, 1522; "the author." See O.H.S. i. 100.' - Alumni Oxonienses 1500-1714.
2 CONT
2 CONT He went to Padua with Thomas Lupset in 1523. Here he studied the works of Aristotle and admired the government of Venice. By 1529 he had entered the service of Reginald Pole as secretary. Together with Pole, Starkey went to Avigno
2 CONC n in 1532 where he studied civil law, before returning to Padua.
2 CONT
2 CONT Starkey returned to England in late 1534 and caught the eye of Thomas Cromwell, chief minister to Henry VIII, in early 1535. Cromwell used Starkey to handle intelligence from Italy and as a royal propagandist.
2 CONT
2 CONT His deep ties to Reginald Pole proved somewhat detrimental to his career, especially after a manuscript of Pole's De unitate, a savage attack on Henry VIII, arrived in England in 1536. These same ties to Pole and his family made h
2 CONC im a subject of investigation in the 1538 Exeter Conspiracy. He died on 25 August 1538, perhaps of plague.
2 CONT
2 CONT added by TPD 15/6/2024
