1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Family and Education b. 1567/8,1 1st. s. of Robert Needham of Shavington and Frances, da. of Sir Edward Aston of Tixall, Staffs.2 educ. Shrewsbury g.s. 1577; St. John’s, Camb. 1582; I. Temple 1584.3 m. (1) settleme
2 CONC nt 10 Aug. 1586, Joan (d. 16 July 1591), da. of John Lacy, alderman of London and Borston, Som. 1s., 2da. d.v.p.;4 (2) settlement Dec. 1594, Anne (d. aft. 1613), da. of Henry Doyley of Shottesham, Norf., wid. of Thomas Townshend (
2 CONC d.1591) of Brakenash, Norf. and George Willmer of London (d.1593/4), s.p.5 (3) 1627, Catherine (admon. 8 Dec. 1628), da. of John Robinson of London, wid. of George Huxley (d.1627) of Edmonton, Mdx., s.p.;6 (4) Dorothy (d. by 1639)
2 CONC , da. of Humphrey Smith, Grocer of London, wid. of Benedict Barnham† (d.1598), Draper and alderman of London and Sir John Pakington (d.1625) of Westwood, Worcs., s.p. kntd. 1 Sept. 1594;7 suc. fa. 1603;8 cr. Visct. Kilmorey [I] 8 
2 CONC Apr. 1625.9 d. 24 Nov. 1631.10 sig. Robert Nedham.
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT Vol. [I] 1594; capt. of horse [I] 1596-8, ft. 1599.11
2 CONT
2 CONT J.p. Salop 1596-d., Cheshire 1614-d.;12 freeman, Southampton, Hants 1599, Shrewsbury, Salop 1614;13 dep. lt., Salop 1600-d.,14 sheriff 1606-7;15 commr. subsidy, Salop 1608, 1621-2, 1624, aid 1609, swans, Midlands 1627;16 member, C
2 CONC ouncil in the Marches 1609-d., v.-pres. 1614;17 commr. Forced Loan, Salop 1626-7.18
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Originally from Needham Grange, Derbyshire, the Needhams acquired Cranage, Cheshire by marriage at the end of Edward III’s reign. The first member of the family to serve as an MP, the lawyer John Needham (who sat for New
2 CONC castle-under-Lyme and London in the reign of Henry VI) built up a substantial estate in Nantwich hundred, and established his main residence just across the county line at Shavington Hall, Shropshire.19 Sir Robert Needham spent mu
2 CONC ch of his early adult life in Ireland, and was knighted by lord deputy Sir William Russell† at the relief of Eniskillen in 1594. He brought over fresh recruits in 1596 and, despite some misgivings about his youth, was given comman
2 CONC d of a horse troop, which was ambushed south of Dublin a few months later. He missed this action, and may have returned to England before his unit was disbanded in 1598, but in the following year he was listed on Essex’s Irish est
2 CONC ablishment as a garrison captain.20
2 CONT
2 CONT While Needham’s Irish career ended at this point, his military experience brought him an early appointment as deputy lieutenant in 1600. His main estates were situated in Cheshire, but his administrative experience lay in Shropshi
2 CONC re, and it was for the latter county that he was returned to Parliament as junior knight of the shire for a second time in 1604. The county seats usually rotated among the Shropshire gentry, and Needham, having just inherited his 
2 CONC estates, had a particularly good claim to election. Moreover, the junior knight for the previous Parliament, (Sir) Roger Owen*, a personal friend, was ruled out of contention because he was then serving as sheriff.21
2 CONT
2 CONT Needham was not prominent within the Commons, and his appointment as sheriff of Shropshire caused him to miss the 1606-7 session entirely. However, his status as a shire knight brought some wider responsibilities, such as attendan
2 CONC ce at the conference with the Lords of 14 Apr. 1604, at which the king’s initial proposals for the Union were unveiled, and membership of bill committees for important measures such as the continuance and revival of statutes (24 M
2 CONC ar. 1604) and Anne of Denmark’s jointure estates (24 May 1604). The Shrewsbury drapers arranged for both shire knights to be named to their Welsh cloth bill (10 Mar. 1606), while Needham was one of a large group who volunteered to
2 CONC  testify about the abuses of purveyors (7 May 1604). Another local concern, the navigation of the river Severn, was reflected in his nomination to committees for the weirs’ bill (23 June 1604, 7 Feb. 1606), and he was included on 
2 CONC committees for two private bills sponsored by neighbours, Robert Devereux, 3rd earl of Essex and Sir George Booth.22 He is known to have made two speeches, one an appeal for privilege to suspend a Chancery case (15 May 1604), the 
2 CONC other an unsuccessful defence of the bill to restrict hunting rights to the gentry, which was apparently a particular interest, as he was named to committees for four other game bills.23 Finally, when wild rumours of the king’s as
2 CONC sassination panicked the House on 22 Mar. 1606, Needham, largely because he had a saddled horse ready to hand, was one of three MPs chosen to ride post haste to Woking, Surrey to confirm that James was safe.24
2 CONT
2 CONT Needham does not appear to have stood for election again, although he secured his son a seat in the Addled Parliament for Newcastle-under-Lyme. He remained active in local affairs for the rest of his life, particularly in the lieu
2 CONC tenancy, and at his death the armoury at Shavington contained weapons for several dozen men.25 His purchase of an Irish viscountcy in the final weeks of James’s reign was something of a novelty, but Charles, with a war to fund, op
2 CONC enly sold such honours for cash, and several members of the Cheshire faction to which Needham was linked quickly followed his example.26 In 1629 the House of Lords questioned the Irish and Scottish viscounts’ claims to precedence 
2 CONC before English barons, and George, Lord Chaworth* mounted a spirited defence of their interests before the Privy Council. However, Needham, when approached for a donation towards this cause, rather feebly protested that his creati
2 CONC on was justified because he had had no knowledge of it ‘till my patent was brought to me to my house in the country, which I conceive was [granted] of mere grace from His Majesty in recompense for the service I had done in Ireland
2 CONC , for so it is expressed in my patent’.27
2 CONT
2 CONT Needham was well able to afford his peerage, and his fortunes were further augmented by those of the two widows he married in quick succession at the end of his life, who had generous jointure estates and were respectively worth £
2 CONC 20,000 and £5,000 in goods.28 His will of 22 Dec. 1630 insisted that his funeral be conducted ‘without giving blacks or mourning attire (for that I hold it to be a very idle ceremony)’, and offered small legacies to his immediate 
2 CONC family, but his estates were already entailed upon his eldest son, to whom they passed at his death on 24 Nov. 1631. His widow married the 1st earl of Kellie, but was dead by the time of the latter’s demise in 1639.29
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1604-1629 Author: Simon Healy Notes 1. C142/290/101. 2. Vis. Salop (Harl. Soc. xxix), 371-2. 3. Shrewsbury Sch. Regestum Scholarium ed. E. Calvert, 52; Al. Cant.; I. Temple Admiss. 4. Salop RO, 946/A/149; Vis. Salop (
2 CONC Harl. Soc. xxix) 371-2. 5. C142/231/71; C2/Chas.I/T19/17; C8/18/72. 6. C142/435/130. 7. Shaw, Knights of Eng. ii. 91. 8. C142/290/101. 9. CP. 10. CHES 3/104/12. 11. CSP Carew, 1589-1600, pp. 222, 244, 248; HMC Hatfield, vi. 543; i
2 CONC x. 147; APC, 1597-8, p. 616. 12. C231/1, f. 18. 13. HMC 11th Rep. iii. 22; Trans. Salop Arch. Soc. (ser. 2), x. 306. 14. APC, 1599-1600, pp. 399-400; CSP Dom. 1603-10, p. 238; SP14/28/84, 14/33/4; Cheshire Archives, DNE 16; Salop 
2 CONC RO, 3365/198/I/1-2; SP16/408/73. 15. List of Sheriffs comp. A. Hughes (PRO, L. and I. ix), 119. 16. SP14/31/1; 14/43/107; C212/22/20-23; C181/3, f. 226v. 17. NLW, 339F, pp. 34, 75; C181/3, f. 191. 18. C193/12/2. 19. Vis. Salop, 37
2 CONC 1-2. 20. CSP Carew, 1589-1600, pp. 222-3, 244, 248; CSP Ire. 1592-6, p. 519; APC, 1597-8, p. 616; HMC Hatfield, ix. 147. 21. APC, 1599-1600, pp. 399-400; C219/35/2/37; List of Sheriffs, 119. 22. CJ, i. 152b, 172a, 202a, 224a, 245b
2 CONC , 265a, 281a, 283b, 414a. 23. Ibid. 211a, 240b, 414a, 415a, 416a, 975b. 24. Hull RO, L.160; CJ, i. 288b, Bowyer Diary, 89. 25. HMC 10th Rep. iv. 365-70, 374. 26. CSP Dom. 1623-5, p. 503; CSP Ire. 1625-32, p. 1; J.S. Morrill, Chesh
2 CONC ire, 32-4. 27. Fairfax Corresp. ed. G.W. Johnson, i. 156-9; C115/99/7238-42, 7244, 7250; Salop RO, 946/B/758. 28. C142/418/69, 142/435/130; HMC 10th Rep. iv. 374; C2/Chas.I/F3/46. 29. PROB 11/161, f. 285; CHES 3/104/12; CP sub Kel
2 CONC lie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Needham, created 1st Viscount Kilmorey in Queen's County by King Charles I, on 4-18-1625, one of the Council of the President of Wales Sir Robert Needham received a grant of the Barony of Orhera, (Orier Bar) and other lands
2 CONC  in Co Armagh from King James I in the 10th year of his reign. The 1st Feudal Baron of Orhera, Co Armagh. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The eldest son, Sir Robert Nedham, of Pool Park, Derbyshire, and of Lambeth, Surrey, was knighted in 1630, [and] married, firstly, Mary, daughter and heiress of John Hartop of Surrey, and by her had issue two sons and ... [two dau
2 CONC ghters, one of whom, Eleanor, was mistress of the Duke of Monmouth, to whom she bore four illegitimate children]. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Needham's lengthy Will, dated December 22nd, 1630, but signed and sealed on February 22nd, 1630/1631, and probated March 2nd, 1631/1632, mentioned his wife, Dorothy, his son Robert, his sisters, Dorothy Chetwood, Mary Verno
2 CONC n, Jane Hocknell, and Maude Aston, his grandchildren, Robert Needham, Charles Needham, George Needham, an unnamed Needham (probably Thomas), Elyn (Eleanor) Needham, Mary Needham, Suzan (Susanna) Needham, and Frances Cotton (the wi
2 CONC fe of Thomas Cotton), and his sons-in-law, Sir Rowland Cotton (former husband of Robert Needham's deceased daughter, Frances) and Sir William Owen (who married Robert Needham's daughter, Eleanor, who was probably deceased since sh
2 CONC e was not mentioned in the Will). 
2 CONT == Other References ==
