1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elizabeth preston of Holker.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Preston who married first Thomas Tildesly of Morley, by whom she had two sons, one of whom was the renowned Sir Thomas Tildesley, the celebrated Royalist, who lost his life at the battle of Wigan Lane. (Tildesley spelled
2 CONC  also Tildesly ??) She married secondly, Thomas Latham, Esq., of Parbold. She married thirdly Thomas Weslby, Esq., of Mowbreck. She had children by both husbands but no account is available.
2 CONT
2 CONT Holker Hall is a country house with a celebrated garden situated on the Cartmel Peninsula, which was historically part of the county of Lancashire, but is now part of the county of Cumbria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before the Dissolution of the Monasteries in 1536 the land on which Holker stands belonged to Cartmel Priory. When the land came up for sale it was acquired by the Preston family, who were established local landowners. Since then 
2 CONC it has passed from the Prestons to the Lowthers and then the Cavendishes by inheritance, but it has never been sold.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Prestons are believed to have made Holker their main residence in about 1610. The estate was briefly sequestered by the Parliamentarians during the Civil War, but was restored upon the payment of fine. The last owner from the 
2 CONC Preston family died in 1697 and left the house to his daughter Catherine, who was married to Sir William Lowther. Sir William's son Sir Thomas laid out formal gardens and added a north wing to the house.
2 CONT
2 CONT The third and last Lowther to own the property, another Sir William, died childless before the age of thirty and left the estate to his cousin Lord George Augustus Cavendish, a younger son of the 3rd Duke of Devonshire, who served
2 CONC  for a time as Comptroller of the Royal Household, and in his old age became Father of the House of Commons. Lord George Augustus commissioned John Carr to make alterations to the east and north wings of the house in a gothic styl
2 CONC e, and replaced the formal gardens with an informal natural landscape, thus beginning the evolution of the grounds towards their current form.
2 CONT
2 CONT The estate passed through the hands of two more members of the Cavendish family until in 1834 it was inherited by William Cavendish, 2nd Earl of Burlington. The Earl reconstructed the house between 1838 and 1842 with the assistanc
2 CONC e of the local architect Webster of Kendal. It acquired a gothic appearance throughout, with the addition of ornamental chimneys, gables and mullioned and transomed windows. The gardens were also enlarged at this time. In 1858 the
2 CONC  Earl became the head of the Cavendish family, inheriting the title of Duke of Devonshire, along with Chatsworth House, which is one of the grandest houses in England, and several other residences. However, he continued to spend m
2 CONC uch time at Holker, partly because it was near to the town of Barrow-in-Furness, which he largely owned, and where, in an unusual move for a Victorian landowner, he made vast (and ultimately unsuccessful) investments in heavy indu
2 CONC stry.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1871 the west wing of the house, containing the main family apartments, was destroyed by fire. Most of the contents were lost, including many of the fine paintings collected by the second Sir William Lowther in the mid 18th cen
2 CONC tury. The Duke had it rebuilt to much the same plan, but in a grander style. It was described by Sir Nikolaus Pevsner: as "the grandest of its date in Lancashire, and it is moreover by the best architects living in the county Pale
2 CONC y and Austin. It is their outstanding work, red sandstone in the Elizabethan style." The interiors are comfortably domestic, well lit and airy, with much wood panelling; a relaxed family retreat built for a man whose main country 
2 CONC and London houses were opulent showplaces. The whole of the two principal floors of this wing are now open to the public, while the family lives in the older part of the house. Holker later passed to Lord Richard Cavendish, a youn
2 CONC ger brother of the 9th Duke of Devonshire, whose descendants including Hugh Cavendish still live there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Holker Hall's 25-acre (100,000 m2) garden is a fine example of a mixed English garden, with formal and informal elements. The plantsmanship is of the first order. Features include an arboretum, herbaceous borders, a Victorian rock
2 CONC ery and a late 20th century cascade. The garden houses the National Collection of styracaceae. In 1991 Holker won the Historic Houses Association Garden of the Year Award and in 2002, Holker's Great Lime, which probably dates from
2 CONC  the early 17th century, was selected as one of Britain's 50 Great Trees in honour of Queen Elizabeth II's Golden Jubilee. The garden blends into the surrounding 200-acre (0.81 km2) park. The current Lady Cavendish, Grania, has de
2 CONC veloped the garden and also the visitor facilities at the house.
2 CONT
2 CONT The estate covers 17,000 acres, and consists predominantly of livestock farms. Cartmel Racecourse is part of the estate. The Lakeland Motor Museum used to be located at the hall, but it moved to new premises a few miles away in 20
2 CONC 10.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In Sir William Dugdale's Visitation of Lancashire, recorded in 1664, Anne Ersfield (Eversfield) is listed as having died without issue - no children.  However on http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=:2799272&i
2 CONC d=I25589, it states that Anne Ersfield and Thomas Lathom had a daughter called Agnes.
2 CONT
2 CONT From: http://genforum.genealogy.com/travers/messages/478.html
2 CONT
2 CONT "I found an error in the problematic pedigree of the Travers family of Nateby.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes Lathom, born circa 1563, is often given in pedigrees as the daughter of Thomas Lathom of Parbold and Anne Eversfield (abbreviated as Ersfield).
2 CONT
2 CONT This cannot be true:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Thomas Lathom, who married Anne Eversfield, was 15 years old in 1602 according to his father's portmortem inquisition (Richard Lathom d. 1602).
2 CONT
2 CONT Therefore Thomas Lathom was born in the year 1587 (he was born 24 years after his alleged daughter Agnes).
2 CONT
2 CONT Also, Thomas Lathom's first wife: Anne Eversfield was born after 1583 (the date of her parents' marriage) and her grandfather, Sir John Eversfield of Worth, names her as a minor under 18 in his will dated 1594. This proves that An
2 CONC ne Eversfield was born at least 20 years after Agnes Lathom's birth. How can a daughter be 20 years older than her own father and mother?
2 CONT
2 CONT Another item of note is the Lathoms of Parbold pedigree in Sir William Dugdale's Visitation of Lancashire. Recorded in 1664, it lists Anne Eversfield as having died without issue - no children! The individual who related this info
2 CONC rmation to Dugdale was Richard Lathom, son of Thomas Lathom by his second wife, Elizabeth Preston. It's doubtful Richard Lathom was completely ignorant of the existence of a half-sister by his father's first wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT We know for a fact that William Travers (1563-1627) did marry an Agnes Lathom who was the daughter of a "Tho. Lathom of Parbold" on the 25th of January in 1584 because he tells us so himself in a pedigree he compiled for Richard S
2 CONC t. George, the King's Herald, in 1613.
2 CONT
2 CONT This leaves only one Thomas Lathom of Parbold as a possible candidate for Agnes' father: the Thomas Lathom born circa 1526 who married Isabel Standish. This Thomas Lathom (who was the grandfather of the Thomas who married Anne Eve
2 CONC rsfield) is the right age to be the father of Agnes Lathom - it is extremely improbable that it could be anyone else.
2 CONT
2 CONT So Agnes Lathom was the aunt of the Thomas Lathom who married Anne Eversfield - NOT his daughter."
