1 NOTE {geni:about_me} This could be the Edward found in a register at the Church of St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, as follows (see link http://www.lan-opc.org.uk/Ormskirk/index.html). Although on a page of Baptisms between 1653 and 167
2 CONC 4, the entry is in fact correct. There are 3 'stray' christenings recorded in this register between the marriages which took place in 1667-1668 and Edward Lathom is one of them.  It is not unusual in these very old registers to fi
2 CONC nd entries are out of date sequence as the same book was used to record baptisms, weddings and burials. It may be that the baptisms were recorded before the marriages on a random page in the register or they could have been entere
2 CONC d at the same time as the marriages after discovering they had been missed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptism: Dec 1647 St Peter and St Paul, Ormskirk, Lancashire, England Edward Lathom - Son of Edward Lathom. Born: 11 Dec 1647 Abode: Burscough Register: Baptisms 1653 - 1674, Page 141, Entry 3 Source: LDS Film 1849663
2 CONT
2 CONT If this is indeed him, it would have made him almost 5 years old when the family's Estate and that of his Uncle William and Richard of Allerton and cousin Richard of Parbold (30 at the time) were forfeited for treason by Cromwell'
2 CONC s parliament for being pro-royalist "traitors" soon after the English Civil War. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During her long widowhood, Elizabeth the wife of Richard 1563-1602, occupied Allerton Hall. And by her Will and Codicil, both executed in July, 1624, gave her second son, Edward, the occupation and profit of “this my hall in Aller
2 CONC ton," and the houses and lands for three years after her death for the better discharging of her debts and to keep her other sons, Richard and John, “with dyet." and until her grandson, Richard (of Parbold, son of Thomas who died 
2 CONC in 1623), then an infant of about two years old, the heir of Parbold and Allerton, "came of age".
2 CONT
2 CONT "The commissioners" (of parliament who had confiscated the estate) sold it in 1654 to a John Sumpner of Midhurst, Sussex. for £2,700. Notwithstanding that the Lathoms, father, mother, and children, now dispossessed, were frequentl
2 CONC y convicted of recusancy and suffered the penalties accordingly, they contrived to hang on to both Parbold and Allerton a little longer. Indeed it was not until 1670 that the Sumpner's managed to eject the Lathoms, and only then b
2 CONC y increasing the amount of the original purchase price.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward's (1&2) family were living there first, but the house went to cousin Richard (who married Katherine Massey) probably around 1643 (and family). Whether Richard's family ever took occupation or Edward stayed on, we will never
2 CONC  know. Either way the Lathom's were kicked out by the Sumpner's in 1670.
2 CONT
2 CONT Any Lathom's living on the estate would have vacated to the nearby towns.  It is assumed that Edward settled in Scarisbrick where his son Thomas is known to have resided.  It is not known what he would have done for a living, but 
2 CONC it is almost certain that the family would have been reduced to poverty along with other Royalist family's at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://wiki.standish.org.uk/index.php/Standish_and_the_English_Civil_War:
2 CONT
2 CONT "One tragic result of the Civil War was the suffering inflicted on the Royalist sympathisers. The Langtree family were reduced to poverty; others, such as the Woodward's of Shevington and Worthington's, of Blainscough, hardly reco
2 CONC vered from the fines imposed.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was, perhaps, some retaliation, when the fortunes of the King's party were in the ascendant. Parishioners complained that their cattle had been taken for rent, and they themselves made prisoners of war for carrying food to p
2 CONC arliamentary soldiers and refugees, when Prince Rupert came into the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT But the exactions imposed on Royalists were severe. Edward Chisnall, Mr Standish of Standish, and William Hoghton of Park Hall, were said to be among those present at the burning of Lancaster in 1643, from whose estates the inhabi
2 CONC tants of the town who were not delinquents were to be compensated to the extent of £8,000. The third Act of Sale, 1652, contained the names of those who were to lose their estates entirely. The following were included: Edward Stan
2 CONC dish of Woolston (eldest son of Ralph Standish); George Hornby, Lawrence Standish, Edward Prescot, John Brown, John Rigby, all of Standish; Hugh Pilkington and James Rigby of Coppull; William Norris of Adlington, John Hoghton of P
2 CONC ark Hall, William Anderton of Anderton, and Thomas Langtree of Langtree.[2]"
2 CONT
2 CONT see also http://wn.com/English_Royalists and http://lazacode.com/war-history/battle-of-worcester-aftermath-and-legacy-part-4/4
