2 CONT “The Last Will & Testament of Lieut: Francis Pebody of Topsfield in ye county of Es[sex] [in] Newe England: I Francis
2 CONT Pebody taking into consideration the uncertainty of my life and ye certainty of my death being of perfect understanding &
2 CONT memory have seen good to m[ake] a disposall of the temporall estate which God of his grace hath given me in this w[orld] as
2 CONT followeth,
2 CONT “Impr. I committ my immortall soul into the hands of God & my body to a decent buriall [when] God shall take me out of this
2 CONT world
2 CONT “Secundo I give to my son John Pebody & Joseph Pebody all that tract of Land which I bought of marchant Joseph Juett of
2 CONT Rowly which Land lyeth in Boxford, I give to my son John two thirds of ye aforesaid tract of Land & to my son Joseph ye
2 CONT other third which I give to them & to their Heirs for ever & moreover I do give to them both in c[ountry] pay (not mony) five
2 CONT pounds to each of them, yt is five pounds apiece, besides what I have already given them
2 CONT “Item, I do give to my son William Pebody all that Land which I bought of John Tod Senr [of] Rowly & of John Perley
2 CONT (excepting one hundred acres) which land I do give to him & his Heirs for ever, moreover I do give to him five pounds
2 CONT besides what he hath [...] me already, which I do the rather on consideration of his heing (by ye providence of God) deprived
2 CONT of ye use of one of his arms, wch five pounds is to be paid as is above specified
2 CONT “Item I do give to my son in Law Daniell Wood That hundred acres of Land which is above excepted to my son William & is
2 CONT already in part possessed by my son in [law] Daniel Wood which said Land I do give to him & his Heirs for ever it be[ing] in
2 CONT consideration of what I was oblidged to do for him when come of age & pro[vided] yt he shall be satisfyed therewith on yt
2 CONT account & give a discharge thereof to such [...] shall concern Which Land I have already promised & do purpose forthwith to
2 CONT [give] him a deed of in a way of firm conveyance in which deed I shall bound....Limit ye aforesaid hundred acres accordingly
2 CONT “Item I go give to my son Isaac Pebody all the land yt I do now live upon which I bou[ght] of Mr. Simons & my will is yt my
2 CONT son Isaac shall have all ye said Land which lyeth on [ye] south side of ye brook running through the said farm, both upland
2 CONT and meadow so bounded. I give to my son Isaac Pebody, together wth my dwelling house and housing, orchard, mill, and mill
2 CONT yard, wth all yt I bought of William Evens; and moreover I give to my son Isaac from the bridge all ye meadow downward on
2 CONT ye northeast side of ye brook wch runneth through Tho. Dorman's meadow; as also I do give to my son Isaac a rod and half
2 CONT of upland adjoining to ye aforesaid meadow all along for ye bringing of his hay from time to time, wch aforesaid land I do give
2 CONT to my son Isaac and his heirs forever, together wth Twenty acres of land on ye south side of ye river, neer to ye dwelling of
2 CONT Joseph Toun junr. Also I give to my son Isaac that bed with the furniture thereunto belonging which he now hath ye
2 CONT improvement of, and this I would have noted, that I have given the more to my son Isaac on consideration of ye providenceof
2 CONT God disinabling him by ye loss of one of his leggs.
2 CONT “Item. I do give to mygrandchild Jacob Pebody (ye son of myson Jacob deceased) ye house which his father dwelt in,
2 CONT together with all ye upland on yt south side of ye brook yt is on ye north side of ye abovesaid brook, as also all the meadow
2 CONT on ye same side of ye brook from ye bridge and so upward; my will is yt in case my said grandchild Jacob Pebody do live to
2 CONT ye age of twenty one years, yt then he shall have as is abovesaid to injoy himself and his heirs forever. But in case ye said
2 CONT Jacob live not to yt age, yt then any of my other sons shall have liberty to have the land and house abovesaid, provided yt he
2 CONT or they shall pay unto my grandchildren Kesia and Mercy Pebody, ye children of my son Jacob deceased, an hundred and
2 CONT twenty pounds in common current money (not silver) notwithstanding what is above said in case ye said Jacob should have
2 CONT issue before he should arrive at ye aforesaid age, yt ye said land shall be at ye dispose of ye said Jacob Pebody, together wth
2 CONT ye house aforesaid. Also I do give to my grandchildren Kesia and Mary Pebody, ye children of my son Jacob deceased, I do
2 CONT give to each of them thirty acres of land apiece, provided they shall shall live to ye age of eighteen years; which three score
2 CONT acres of land lyeth on ye south side of ye river, in ye southwest division beyond Mr. Endicott's farm, in ye place called the
2 CONT stickey meadow, which land abovesaid I bought part of deacon Tho. Perkins about thirty acres, and about thirty more which I
2 CONT bought of Daniell Dorman; but in case yt neither of ye children Kesia or Mercy shall live to the age of eighteen, yt the ye
2 CONT abovesaid thirty acres apiece shall return to my next and immediate children to be equally divided amongst themp; and in case
2 CONT one of the said grandchildren live to yt age and not ye other, that then ye whole threescore acres shall fall to ye survivor of
2 CONT them.
2 CONT “Item. I give to my son Nathaniell Pebody together with my Grandchild Samson How a[ll] that four hundred acres which I
2 CONT bought of mr Stephen Sewall Lying in Rowly village called Boxford which land lyeth near Bradford & was formerly mr
2 CONT Nelsons of Rowly. My will is yt my son Nathaniell shall have three hundred acres & Samson How ye other hundred acres
2 CONT which for quantity and quality ye aforesaid Samson How shall have ye said Hundred acres provided yt yt said Samson How
2 CONT shall be at my despose till ye age of twenty[one] years But in Case my son Nathaniell shall dye without Lawfull Issue that then
2 CONT ye above said three hundred acres shall fall to my other children by equal devision, his widdow notwithstanding injoying ye
2 CONT benefit thereof during life & as to his moveable estate which he is already in possession of I leave it all to be at his yt is my son
2 CONT Nathaniells despose here is to be understood yt what shall be left undesposed of by my son Nathaniell at his death of his three
2 CONT hundred acres shall be for ye use of his widdow during her life as abovesaid yt is ye life of her widdowhood.
2 CONT “I do reserve for Mary my wife ye South End of my house for her Use to live in as also ye New Cellar as also ye Use of two
2 CONT milch Cows which she shall choose out of my milch kine, Also my will is yt my son Isaac shall pay to my wife Mary yearly
2 CONT twenty bushells of Indian Corne, four bushells of wheat, four of rye & six of malt, also yt my wife have liberty to keep two or
2 CONT three swine, as also yearly half a dozen pounds of wool, also my will is yt my wife shal have pasture for her cowes with my
2 CONT son Isaacs as also my son Isaac shall provide fodder for them in ye winter. As also my wife shall have Liberty for an horse to
2 CONT ride on as she shall have occasion. Also my will is yt in Case my wife shall marry again yt then all ye privilidges abovesaid shall
2 CONT cease, but during her widowhood she shal also have (as benefit by my orchard) yearly a barrell of Cidar as also som apples
2 CONT as her occasions either in the summer or winter shall require, Moreover my wife shall have ye use & despose of two beds
2 CONT together with needful firewood provided for her for which end she shall have ye use of such of my oxen as shall be needful.
2 CONT Also I do order yt Samson How shall live with my wife till he shall come to ye age of twenty one years & be at her Command
2 CONT to be helpfull to her on all accounts as she shall have occasion & in case Samson How shall be taken away by his father
2 CONT before he shall have served as abovesaid yt then my wife shall have that hundred acres of land abovesaid (given Conditionally
2 CONT to ye said Samson) to provide for herself such help as shall be necessary. & in Case my wife shall dy before ye said Samson
2 CONT How shall arrive at yt aforesaid term of years yt then he shall be at my wives despose to whom she shall see good & in case
2 CONT yt said Samson will comply with such despose yt then ye said hundred acres of land intended for him shall be at my wives
2 CONT despose
2 CONT “Item I give to my daughter Lydia Perley five pounds besides what she hath already had of me
2 CONT “I do give to my daughter Mary Death five pounds besides what she hath had already
2 CONT “I do give to my daughter Sarah How five pounds besides what she hath had already
2 CONT “I do give to my daughter Hephzibah Ray five pounds besides what she hath had already all which Legacies ordered to my
2 CONT Children I do appoint to be paid in common currant pay as is before specified to others of my children
2 CONT “Note yt what Legacies I do here give in my will shall be paid by my executors out of my estate which I do leave in my son
2 CONT Isaac hands & to my wives, as corn or Cattell &c my debts & funerall expences being discharged.
2 CONT “And finaly. I do appoint, Constitute & ordain my wife Mary to be an executrix together with my son John Pebody & Isaac
2 CONT Pebody as executors of this my last will & testament & in Case after Legacies paid there be any estate left to be devided yt it
2 CONT shall be desposed of in a way of devision as my executrix together with executors shall see good.
2 CONT “That what is here above written is ye last will & Testament of ye abovesaid Francis Pebody appears by his own hand & seal
2 CONT ye day & Date here mentioned as also by ye testimony of ye witnesses hereunto subscribed
2 CONT “It is to be noted yt notwithstanding what is abovesaid concerning my son Nathaniells three hundred acres returning to his
2 CONT brethren in Case of his dying wtout lawfull Issue, It is to be understood by ye three hundred acres what he shall not see Cause
2 CONT to despose of before his death I hereby notwithstanding what hath been said giving him full power in Case he see good to
2 CONT despose of it either in part or whole not knowing but divine providence may necessitate him thereunto, otherwise what is
2 CONT above written to be of full force as is expressed.
2 CONT ffrancis Pabody
2 CONT “The above said premises were signed & sealed & declared to be ye last will & Testament of ye said Francis Pebody ye
2 CONT twentieth day of January in ye year of our Lord one thousand six hundred Ninety & five or six, in presence of us
2 CONT Joseph Capen
2 CONT Thomas Baker
2 CONT Ephraim Dorman Senr”
2 CONT
2 CONT The will was proved Aug. 7, 1698. An inventory of his estate made May 20, 1698, found the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT Two oxen, three cows and three young cattle, valued at 23 pounds;
2 CONT Sheep, one mare and colt, 5 pounds, 10 shillings;
2 CONT Chains, axes, wedges, chisels and other iron tools, 2 pounds, 4 shillings;
2 CONT Wearing clothes, 2 pounds, 4 shillings;
2 CONT Beds and bedding, napkins, table cloths, other linens, 17 pounds, 9 shillings;
2 CONT Chests, tables, chairs, other lumber, 5 pounds, 15 shillings, 6 pence;
2 CONT Pewter and brass ware, 5 pounds, 12 shillings;
2 CONT Iron ware, as pots, kettles, “tramells”, etc., 2 pounds, 5 shillings;
2 CONT New cloth home made, 2 pounds, 11 shillings;
2 CONT Ten bushells of malt, 8 bushells of Indian corn, 6 bushells of barley, 4 pounds;
2 CONT Four swine, 2 pounds;
2 CONT Land given to his son John, 200 pounds;
2 CONT Land to Joseph Peabody, 100 pounds;
2 CONT Land to William Peabody, 100 pounds;
2 CONT Land to Nathaniel Peabody, 150 pounds;
2 CONT Land to Samson Howe, 50 pounds;
2 CONT Land to Keziah and Marcie Peabody, 50 pounds;
2 CONT Land and house to Jacob Peabody, 160 pounds;
2 CONT Homestead, upland, meadow, dwelling house, barn and mill to Isaac Peabody, 400 pounds;
2 CONT Silver money, 44 pounds, 15 shillings;
2 CONT Total: 1327 pounds, 5 shillings, 6 pence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Additional items inventoried Sept. 30, 1698:
2 CONT
2 CONT Two oxen, 9 pounds;
2 CONT Nine books, 13 shillings;
2 CONT Two cows, 5 pounds;
2 CONT Two oxen, 8 pounds;
2 CONT Two linen spinning wheels, 6 shillings;
2 CONT Two woolen spinning wheels, 6 shillings;
2 CONT Two beds with furniture given to widow, 19 pounds;
2 CONT Eight yards of wool cloth, 1 pound, 12 shillings;
2 CONT Six cushions, 6 shillings;
2 CONT Bed and bedding given to Isaac, 3 pounds, 10 shillings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children by Lydia:
2 CONT
2 CONT i. Lydia,3 bapt. Aug. 30, 1640, in Hampton; m. (1) by 1663, Thomas Howlett, b. about 1638, son of Thomas and Alice
2 CONT (French) Howlett. He d. Dec. 22, 1667 (probably an error for 1666) in Ipswich and she m. (2) July 8, 1667, in Rowley,
2 CONT Mass., Thomas Perley, b. 1641 in Ipswich, son of Allan and Susanna (Bokeson) Perley; ; he d. Sept. 24, 1709, in Boxford,
2 CONT Mass.; she d. Apr. 30, 1715, in Boxford; children by Lydia: Mary Howlett, Thomas Perley, Jacob Perley, Lydia Perley,
2 CONT Mary Perley, Hephzibah Perley, Sarah Perley.
2 CONT
2 CONT #4 ii. John, b. about 1642.
2 CONT
2 CONT #5 iii. Joseph, b. about 1644.
2 CONT
2 CONT #6 iv. William, b. March 3, 1646.
2 CONT
2 CONT #7 v. Isaac, b. about 1648.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children by Mary:
2 CONT
2 CONT vi. Sarah, b. about 1650; m. March 20 or 26, 1678, at Ipswich, Mass., Abraham Howe, b. about 1649, in Ipswich, son of
2 CONT James and Elizabeth (Dane) Howe of Ipswich; settled in Ipswich; he d. Jan. 21, 1717/8; she, Sept. 29, 1732; children: Love
2 CONT Howe, Increase Howe, Samson Howe, Abraham Howe, Abijah Howe, Israel Howe, Mark Howe.
2 CONT
2 CONT vii. Hepsibah, b. about 1652; m. Apr. 10, 1678, Daniel Rea of Salem Village, son of Joshua and Sarah (Waters) Rea; he m.
2 CONT (2) Mary (Read) Tompkins, widow of John Tompkins.
2 CONT
2 CONT viii. Mary, b. about 1656; m. (1) about 1669, John Death of Framingham, Mass., son of John and Abigail Death; it is not
2 CONT clear whether Mary m. (2) Samuel Eames or whether it was John's sister, Mary, who married him; children: Oliver Death,
2 CONT Thomas Death, John Death, Hepzibah Death, Lydia Death, Samuel Death, Ruth Death. Porter Pedigree says this family
2 CONT changed name from Death to How.
2 CONT
2 CONT ix. Ruth, b. May 22, 1658; not mentioned in father's will.
2 CONT
2 CONT x. Damaris, b. Jan. 21 or June 21, 1660; d. Dec. 19, 1660, in Topsfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT xi. Samuel, b. June 4, 1662; d. Sept. 13, 1667.
2 CONT
2 CONT #8 xii. Jacob, b. July 28, 1664.
2 CONT
2 CONT xiii. Hannah, b. May 8, 1668; m. Daniel Andrew, b. Sept. 2, 1677, in Salem, son of Daniel and Sarah (Porter) Andrew; lived
2 CONT Salem Village; she d. before Feb. 12, 1698, and he m. (2) Feb. 12, 1701/2, Elizabeth Peabody [#4]; he d. Feb. 6, 1717/8;
2 CONT children born to Daniel and Elizabeth: Hannah Andrew, Daniel Andrew, Mary Andrew, Thomas Andrew, Israel Andrew,
2 CONT Israel Andrew, John Andrew, Samuel Andrew.
2 CONT
2 CONT #9 xiv. Nathaniel, b. July 20, 1669.
