1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *''''''The Wentworth genealogy: English and American (1878) Vol. 1
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/wentworthgeneal02wentgoog#page/n78/mode/1up/search/Nicholas
2 CONT *(15) Henry Wentworth, of Codham Hall, in Wethersfield, co. Essex, married, first, Elizabeth, daughter and sole heir of Henry Howard, second son of Sir John Howard; and, secondly, Jane, daughter and heir of Henry Fitz Simon. She s
2 CONC urvived her husband and remarried Sir Richard Fitz Lewes, Kt., of West Horndon, do. Essex, whose Will she proved 24 November 1529. By his second wife Henry Wentworth had issue only one son, Nicholas, ancestor of the Wentworths of 
2 CONC counties Oxford, Bucks, Northampton, Warwick, and Lincoln, of whom hereafter. The said Henry Wentworth died 22 March 1482-3, having had issue by his first wife as follows: --
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/wentworthgeneal02wentgoog#page/n79/mode/1up/search/Nicholas
2 CONT *Before proceeding with the main line, we give the descendants of the son by the second wife, just mentioned, viz.--
2 CONT **''''''(16) Nicholas Wentworth, who became Sir Nicholas, being knighted by King Henry VIII in person during his expedition to Boulogne, in August 1544. For many years he was "Chief Porter of Calais," then an important government 
2 CONC post. He possessed numerous estates in the counties of Oxford, Bucks, Northampton, Warwick, and Lincoln, but his chief residence was at Lillingstone Lovell, co. Oxford, which manor was given to him by the king in 1546, in exchange
2 CONC  for certain lands in Northamptonshire. His Will, dated 7 February 1551-2, was proved 24 June 1557, and he probably died not long before the latter date. He married, before 1525, Jane, daughter of John Josselyn, Esq., of Newhall-J
2 CONC osselyn, co. Essex, and of Sabridgeworth, co. Herts, and sister of Sir Thomas Josselyn, Kt. She survived him, and was buried at Burnham, co. Bucks, 26 August 1659. Their issue were are follows: --
2 CONT ***1. Peter, of whom hereafter.
2 CONT ***2. Henry, who became a counsellor at law in London, and died in St. Sepulchre's parish 1 January 1613-14. By a first wife, whose name has not been ascertained, he had one daughter, Cicely, who married Francis Michell, of Theydo
2 CONC n Garnon, co. Essex, and had issue. Henry Wentworth married, secondly, Anastacy, daughter and heir of William Hale, Esq., of Maldon, co. Essex. She survived her husband, dying 4 June 1634, without issue and was buried at Maldon af
2 CONC oresaid.
2 CONT ***3. Paul, who was of Burnham, co. Bucks. He was chosen M. P. 1571, and was leader of the opposition party in the time of Queen Elizabeth. Hallam calls him "the most undaunted asserter of civil liberty in this reign." He and his 
2 CONC brother Peter were repeatedly sent to the Tower. He died 13 January 1593-4, in his 60th year, and was buried at Burnham. He married there, 26 November 1653, Helen, daughter of Richard Agmondesham, Esq., of Heston, co. Middlesex, a
2 CONC nd widow of William Tyllesley, Esq., of Burnham aforesaid. Her Will, dated 24 August, was proved 8 November 1615. They had issue:-
2 CONT ***http://www.archive.org/stream/wentworthgeneal02wentgoog#page/n80/mode/1up/search/Nicholas
2 CONT ***4. Francis, whom his father in his Will directed should become a priest. There is no evidence that he did so; and he was buried at Burnham 4 September 1564.
2 CONT ***5. Clare, only daughter, who married, before her father's death, Edward Boys, Esq., of Fredville, in Nonington, co. Kent, sheriff of that county in 1577. She died before 1586, and he remarried twice, dying 15 February 1598-9, a
2 CONC ged 71.
2 CONT *______________
2 CONT *''''''The Wentworth genealogy, comprising the origin of the name, the family in England, and a particular account of Elder William Wentworth, the emigrant, and of his descendants (1870)
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/wentworthgenealo01inwent#page/n115/mode/2up
2 CONT *CHART - Henry Wentworth, of Codham Hall, Co. Essex; son of Sir Roger, as in Table G. = Jane Fitz Lewes; had only son, ''''''Nicholas''''''
2 CONT **http://www.archive.org/stream/wentworthgenealo01inwent#page/n117/mode/2up
2 CONT **CHART - ''''''Sir Nicholas Wentworth, of Lillingston Lovel, Co. Oxford Will proved 1557 = Jane, daughter of John Josselyne. children: Peter, Three sons and one daughter.
2 CONT *<nowiki>------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT *''''''Nicholas WENTWORTH (Sir Knight)
2 CONT *Born: 1482 / ABT 1500, Lillingstone Lovell, Oxfordshire, England
2 CONT *Died: ABT 1553 / 24 Jun 1557
2 CONT *''''''Notes: held the office of chief porter of Calais. He is variously styled chief porter, master porter, or knight porter. He was knighted by Henry VIII at the siege of Boulogne, 1544, and died in 1557. He married the sister o
2 CONC f Sir Thomas Josselyn, K.B., and lived at Lillingstone Lovell, then a detached bit of Oxfordshire surrounded by Buckinghamshire. Lady Wentworth survived to live with her younger son, Paul Wentworth, at Burnham Abbey, and was burle
2 CONC d in Burnham church. 
2 CONT *Father: Henry WENTWORTH of Codham (Esq.)
2 CONT *Mother: Joan FITZSIMON
2 CONT *''''''Married: Jane JOSSELYN (b. ABT 1482) (dau. of John Josselyn of Hyde Hall) 1525, Hertfordshire, England
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Peter WENTWORTH (Sir Knight)
2 CONT **2. Henry WENTWORTH
2 CONT **3. Joan WENTWORTH
2 CONT **4. Paul WENTWORTH
2 CONT **5. Francis WENTWORTH
2 CONT **6. Clara WENTWORTH
2 CONT *http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/WENTWORTH.htm#Nicholas WENTWORTH (Sir Knight)1
2 CONT *__________
2 CONT *''''''The New England historical and genealogical register, Volume 71     By Henry Fitz-Gilbert Waters, New England Historic Genealogical Society
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=f0plVwLW5zQC&pg=PA238&lpg=PA238&dq=Maude+Sutton+1312&source=bl&ots=lh0yOyUmUy&sig=BSnsLrHC_vkN8ZpaRgOqjy_vjUM&hl=en&ei=sWbmTtWKOaavsQLjx9D5BQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=6&ved=0CD8Q6AEwBQ
2 CONC #v=onepage&q=Maude%20Sutton%201312&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 241
2 CONT *   17. GEORGE16 JOSSELYN (Thomas15, Jeffrey14), of Hide Hall in Sawgridgeworth, co. Herts, and of Sheering, co Essex, born in 1428, died after 1480, the year in which he settled Hide Hall on his son Ralph. He married MAUD BARDOLL
2 CONC PH, daugher and heir of Edmund. He is said to have been called "Jocelyn the Courtier." He was heir of his uncle, Sir Ralph (14, vi), and at the death of the latter in 1478 was said to be fifty years and more of age.
2 CONT *   Children:
2 CONT **   19. JOHN17 JOSSELYN (George,16 Thomas,15 Jeffrey14), of Newhall Josselyn in High Roding, co. Essex, and of Hide Hall in Sawbridgeworth, co. Herts. the testator of 1524, born about 1640, died 14 July 1525. he married first, be
2 CONC fore 1492/3 CICELY (MOLINEUX) FITZHERBERT, who died 7 July 1502, the inquisition post mortem being taken 12 Feb. 1503/4, daughter of John Molineux of Chipping Campden, co. Gloucester, and widow of Henry FitzHerbert; and secondly P
2 CONC HILIPPA (BRADBURY) BARLEY, the testatrix of 1530, who died after 14 Oct.
2 CONT **Pg. 242
2 CONT **1530, daughter of William and Jane (FitzWilliams) Bradbury of Littlebury and Wicken-Bonhunt, co. Essex, and widow of John Barley of Stapleford-Abbots, co. Essex.
2 CONT **   John Joseelyn bought of his nephew, George Josselyn (17, i, 1), the ancestral estate of Hide Hall in 1513. ....  He and his wife were buried in the priory of King's Hatfield, and in the chancel of the ancestral church in Sawb
2 CONC ridgeworth was erected a stately tomb, .... It seems probable that, when the priory at King's Hatfield was destroyed at the dissolution of the monasteries in 1538, their bodies were removed to Sawbridgeworth.
2 CONT **   Children by first wife:
2 CONT ***	i. PHILIP,18 d. young.
2 CONT ***	ii. NICHOLAS, b. in 1498; d. between Feb. 1503/4 and 1524.
2 CONT **   Children by second wife:
2 CONT ***	21. iii. THOMAS, b. abt. 1507.
2 CONT ***''''''	iv. JANE or JOAN, d. in 1569; bur. in the church at Burnham co. Bucks; m. SIR NICHOLAS WENTWORTH of Lillingstone-Lovell, co. Bucks, Knight, s. of Henry and Joan FitzSymonds). Children: 1. Peter, b. before 1530. 2. Henry,
2 CONC  b. before 1520. 3. Clare, b. in or before 1530. 4. Paul.
2 CONT ***	v. ANNE, m. after 1530 JOHN BAGOTT or BAGSHOTT of Blofield, co. Norfolk, Esq.
2 CONT *____________
2 CONT *''''''Powers-Banks ancestry: traced in all lines to the remotest date obtainable ...   By William Howard Powers
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=TbBIAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA163&lpg=PA163&dq=Ralph+Wentworth+1383&source=bl&ots=uZFIr5LCxO&sig=9gF8u_OtWiZ0ZmTfAFreklKdRGY&hl=en&ei=9GTNTsiTBqqWiAKUvpmPDA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=3&ved=0CC4Q6AE
2 CONC wAjgK#v=onepage&q=Ralph%20Wentworth%201383&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 151-163
2 CONT *Pg. 158
2 CONT *   The Wentworth connection although it occurred before the settlement of the colonies, is yet of sufficient interest to justify an account. Geoffrey Gates, the great-grandfather of the immigrant, Stephen Gates, wa married to Joa
2 CONC n Wentworth; no dates are given, but as Stephen was born about 1605, the marriage of his great-grandfather must have occurred about 1550. The parentage of his wife, Joan Wentworth, is not given in any book accessible in the librar
2 CONC y of the New England Historical and Genealogical Society. However, certain intermarriages of the families of Gates, Wentworth, and Josselyn are known and from them can be inferred the relationship of Joan.  .....
2 CONT *Pg. 159
2 CONT *   .... Sir Thomas's sister, ''''''Joan Josselyn, was maried to Sir Nicholas Wentworth about 1524; the sons of this marriage are listed in the Wentworth genealogy, and one daughter --- no Joan; but the list may be incomplete. The
2 CONC  eldest son was Peter, born 1524; a third son, Paul, born 1534; obviously there were daughter between; the eldest would be likely to bear the name of her mother Joan and would have been born in  or about the year 1530'''''', a dat
2 CONC e which exactly fits the wife of Geoffrey Gates.  .....
2 CONT *Pg. 160
2 CONT *________________
