1 OCCU held the office of Lord Mayor of London in 1559.
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William Hewett was born in 1496, in Wales, then a hamlet of Laughton-en-le-Morthen. He was the son of Edmund Hewett, of the same place, and grandson of Nicholas.
2 CONT
2 CONT He followed the trade of a clothworker, and after serving his apprenticeship was admitted to the freedom of the Clothworkers' Company of London before 1529, in which year he himself took an apprentice as a freeman. Hewett became M
2 CONC aster of the Clothworkers' Company in 1543-44. He succeeded well in commerce and was joined by many of his relatives and friends from Yorkshire. He employed his brother Thomas and Henry, Thomas' son to help with his business, at t
2 CONC he Three Cranes in Candlewick Street, which he bequeathed to his nephew Henry, with remainder to Thomas Hewett.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served the Office of Sheriff in 1553, and was charged with carrying out the sentence of execution upon Lady Jane Grey and her husband. In the same year he countersigned, with other principal citizens, the letters patent of Edwa
2 CONC rd VI leaving the crown to Lady Jane Grey.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1559 he became Lord Mayor, being the first member of the Clothworkers' Company to reach that status.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was knighted at Greenwich on 21st January. 1559-60.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hewett lived in Philpot Lane. He had also a country house at Highgate, and was chosen by Sir Roger Cholmeley in 1565, as one of the six governors of his newly established grammar school.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hewett also possessed Manors in Dagenham, Essex, which were passed to his daughter on her marriage;and various other manors and estates in Yorkshire, Derbyshire and Nottinghamshire, including Shireoaks, Harthill and Woodall, Treet
2 CONC on and Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hewett inherited through his wife the greater portion of her father's estates
2 CONT
2 CONT Hewett died on 25th January 1567, and was buried beside his wife in St. Martin Orgar, Cannon Street, London. His monument perished in the destruction of the church at the great fire of 1666. His will dated 3rd January. 1566-7, sta
2 CONC ted that a portrait of Hewett in his robes as Lord Mayor was preserved at Kiveton House, Yorkshire, the seat of the Duke of Leeds; it was later moved to Hornby Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hewett married Elizabeth(d1561), third daughter of Nicholas Leveson of Halling in Kent, a rich mercer of London and Sheriff in 1534 his wife Dinoysia Bodley . Alice died on 8 April 1561, and was buried on 17th April at St. Martin 
2 CONC Orgar. By this marriage Hewett is said to have had several children, all of whom died in infancy except Anne, who was born in 1543, and was twenty-three years old at her father's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Hewett, as a child, while playing at one of the windows of her father's house on London Bridge, was dropped by a careless maid into the river, and was rescued by Edward Osborne her father's apprentice. Osborne married her lat
2 CONC er, being preferred by Sir William above many other suitors, among them George Talbot, 6th Earl of Shrewsbury, who was a member of the Clothworkers' Company, and a good friend of Hewett.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.rotherhamweb.co.uk/h/whewett.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT William Hewett, Knight UK, Extracted Probate Records, 1269-1975
2 CONT
2 CONT Name:	Thomas Hewett Henry Hewett Place:	London, England Book:	Burials. (Burial) Collection:	England: London - Inquisitiones Post Mortem for the City of London, 1561-1577 Volume:	Abstracts of the Inquisitiones Post Mortem Relating 
2 CONC to the City of London, Returned into the Court of Chancery. Chapter:	William Hewett, Knight. Text:	I give to my brother Thomas Hewett my tenement lying at the end of St. Clements Lane, to hold for his natural life; after his decea
2 CONC se, I give the same to the said Henry Hewett and his heirs.
