1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Samuel Foxe (1562-1629) was the son of John Foxe, the author of Foxes Book of Martyrs. After some years at Oxford and travelling around Europe Samuel was appointed in 1587 to be in charge of the Royal Palace of Hav
2 CONC ering.  
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1594 he had obtained possession of Warlies. The house and grounds were held by "copyhold of tenure" of the Lord of the Manor of Waltham.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel died in 1629 and left Warlies to his wife Anne, who died four months later and so Warlies was passed to their son Dr. Thomas Foxe (1591-1662).
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas went to Magdalen College Oxford and became a surgeon. On succession to Warlies he started to expand the estate. Land and cottages in Upshire were bought and added to his father's copyhold.  He and his wife Anne, had an only
2 CONC  daughter, Alice.  When he died in 1662, Warlies passed to Alice's husband, Sir Richard Willys (1615-1690).
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Warlies Park House
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.warliesparkhouse.com/Warlies-Park-House-History.pdf
