1 NAME William /Newby/
2 GIVN William
2 SURN Newby
2 _MARNM Newby
1 NAME William "the Quaker" Newby
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Perquimans District
3 STAE North Carolina
3 CTRY British Colonial America
1 BIRT
2 DATE AUG 1637
2 PLAC Easington, Durham, England
2 ADDR
1 BAPM
2 DATE 14 AUG 1637
2 PLAC Easington, Durham, England
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 2 JUN 1704
2 ADDR
3 CITY Perquimans District
3 STAE North Carolina
1 OCCU immigrated to Nansemond, Virginia
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William immigrated to Virginia sometime prior to May 10, 1683 because he signed as a witness to a wedding in Nansemond County.  One account says that he came from London to New England on the Mary & John on March 2
2 CONC 4, 1633 at age 24.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time, they were settled in the area now known as Norfolk.  The "Friends" aka Quakers received a number of acres of land for every "Friend" they sponsored into the U.S.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Quaker.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known when William Newby went to Virginia, but he was there on May 10, 1683 when he signed as a witness to a wedding in Nansemond County.
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Isabel Newby were active in Chuckatuck Monthly Meeting in Virginia in the 1680's. Both signed marriage certificate there on 10-13-1684. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William must have moved to NC after the death of his wife Isabel.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1704 William Newby purchased an acre of land from William Bogue for use of the Quakers -- later known as Wells Monthly Meeting. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://perquimans.lostsoulsgenealogy.com/family/henrynewby.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-40001-1089911643/william-d-newby-in-familysearch-family-tree FamilySearch Family Tree] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Nov 7 2017, 6:53:56 UTC
2 CONC ''
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT William was the son of Ralph and Dorothy Hinchley Newby, one of the early birthright Quakers. His brother John was married to Grace Edmundson, sister of the famous Quaker minister and missionary to the Americas, William Edmundson.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Newby was the first member of the Newby line to live in America and one of the first Quakers to settle in Virginia, being active in the Chuckatuck Monthly Meeting and later the Somerton Monthly Meeting of Nansemond County.
2 CONC  After persecution of Quakers began in Virginia following the restoration of the British monarchy, he moved to Perquimans County, North Carolina, where religious freedom was guaranteed.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was married to Isabel Turner in 1660.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following known children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathan Newby (1662-1735) Gabriel Newby (1665-1735) Thomas (remained in Virginia) Dorothy (abt 1668 - 1701)
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel died before William left Virginia and moved to North Carolina with his sons Nathan and Gabriel.
2 CONT
2 CONT William married Jane Byar in Perquimans County, North Carolina on July 3, 1701.
2 CONT
2 CONT William is mentioned in the Minutes of the Paquotank, Welles, and Perquimans Monthly Meetings. However, he settled near his son Nathan on the east side of the Perquimans river, and the last record we have of him is the purchase of
2 CONC  an acre of land from William Bogue for use of the Quaker community at the Welles Monthly Meeting. This monthly meeting was on the eastern side of Hertford, and near the town of Winfall where William lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Because Quaker headstones do not have individual names on them (See the Cemetery Introduction), PLEASE DO NOT REQUEST INDIVIDUAL PHOTOS].
2 CONT
2 CONT .∼According to "The Newby Book" From England to America 1612-1996 by Margaret Newby Swanzy. With her written permission.
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears that, William Newby is the son of Ralph and Dorothy (Hinchley) Newby of England. He is the earliest proven Newby ancestor in America. He was born, ca 1640 in England. He died, after 1704, probably in Perquimans county, 
2 CONC North Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT William first settled in Nansemond county, Virginia. He was an active member in the 1680's, of Chuckatuck Monthly meeting, of the society of Friends, called Quakers.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Newby married Isabel _____. She died before 1701, probably in Nansemond county, Virginia. He married 2nd, 3rd June 1701 in Perquimans Precinct, Albermarle County, North Carolina, to Jane Byar, a widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Newby and Isabel had at least 6 know children. They are as follows: John Newby Edward Newby Nathan Newby Dorothy Newby Gabriel Newby Thomas Newby
2 CONT
2 CONT  Find a Grave, database and images (https://www.findagrave.com : accessed 20 April 2021), memorial page for William Newby (1636–1704), Find a Grave Memorial no. 95345028, citing Welles Monthly Meeting, Perquimans County, North Car
2 CONC olina, USA ; Maintained by Jan (contributor 47388195) . 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------
2 CONT '''https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree/person/about/LB14-3TZ
2 CONT Brief Life History of William'''
2 CONT
2 CONT When William Newby was born on 14 August 1630, in Durham, Durham, England, his father, Ralph Newby, was 18 and his mother, Dorothy Hinchley, was 20. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Isabel Turner in 1649, in Dublin Castle, County Dublin, Ireland. They were the parents of at least 6 sons and 1 daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 11 January 1704, in Durant, Perquimans, North Carolina, United States, at the age of 73, and was buried in Welles Monthly Meeting, Perquimans, North Carolina, United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
