1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Colonel William Fauntleroy
2 CONT * Birth:	Mar 31 1684 - Naylor's Hole Plantation, Richmond, Virginia
2 CONT * Death:	Nov 15 1757 - Nalors Hole, Richmond, Virginia
2 CONT * Parents:	Col. William Fauntleroy,  Katherine Griffin
2 CONT * Wife:	Apphia Bushrod
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT ”The Fauntleroy Family,” Mary Emily Fauntleroy. Page 213. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. William Fauntleroy (third) born on March 31, 1684, lived at Naylor’s Hole, Richmond County, Virginia. He was married in 1712 to Apphia Bushrod, daughter of Col. John Bushrod, of Bushfield, Westmoreland County, Virginia. He wa
2 CONC s a member of the House of Burgesses from Richmond County, 1736-1749. His will was dated December 5, 1757, at Naylor’s Hole. He died in 1757.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Col. William and Apphia (Bushrod) Fauntleroy :
2 CONT #William (Lieut. Co1.)-b. 1713; d. 1793; m. (1) Elizabeth children. (one child) ;3 m. (2) Margaret Murdock; 12
2 CONT #Moore (Capt.)-b. 1716; d. 1791; m. (1) Ann Neal; (2) Elizabeth Mitchel.
2 CONT #John-b. 1724; d. 1766; m. (1) Judith Littlepage; (2) Elizabeth Waring, daughter of Thomas Waring.
2 CONT #Hannah-m.  Capt. Maxmillian Robinson.
2 CONT #Apphia-m. Col. William Dangerfield.
2 CONT #Katherine-m. John Lewis. 
2 CONT #Mary-m. Robert Brooks.
2 CONT #Ann-m. John Pettet. 
2 CONT #Sarah-m. James Gray.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://digitalarchives.usi.edu/digital/collection/D_Blair/id/4881/ Naylor's Hold/Naylor's Hole in Richmond County, Virginia]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Description
2 CONT
2 CONT Painting of a house entitled Naylor's Hold in Richmond County, Virginia. "Moore Fauntleroy moved to the Northern Neck of the Virginia Tidewater in 1648, and settled on a tract of land River, extending from the Rappahannock to the 
2 CONC Potomac River in Lancaster County, Virginia. Of this large tract of land, five hundred acres was granted and confirmed by Act I, Grand Assembly of Virginia, March 23, 1660, in the twelfth year (reckoning from the execution of Char
2 CONC les on January 30, 1649) of the rein of King Charles II. This property was successively a part of Lancaster, Old Rappahannock, and Richmond counties. Moore Fauntleroy was justice and Burgher from Lancaster 1651, 1652, 1656, and fr
2 CONC om Rappahannock, 1656. He owned large tracts of land, many slaves, and several vessels, and transported many settlers from England. He died in 1667 at Crondall, a part of Naylors Hole. He was buried there." (“The Fauntleroy Family
2 CONC ,” Mary Emily Fauntleroy.  Page 213.  [https://scholarworks.iu.edu/journals/index.php/imh/article/viewFile/7141/7996 link].  Another source says he built a large brick house, made from bricks made in England and brought by ship, a
2 CONC nd named it Naylor's Hold.  (St. Louis, the Fourth City, 1764-1909, Volume 2.  By Walter Barlow Stevens.  Page 569.  [https://books.google.com/books?id=KgI2AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA569&lpg=PA569&dq=Naylor's+Hold+in+Richmond+County,+Virginia&
2 CONC source=bl&ots=oJK55qup_w&sig=og9GvTaGP2hidASXhbxPA7dYEq4&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjxwOKyxO7YAhUEX60KHQz1C14Q6AEIUzAH#v=onepage&q=Naylor's%20Hold%20in%20Richmond%20County%2C%20Virginia&f=false GoogleBooks].  
2 CONT
2 CONT A more contemporary, travel report about the area, now called Naylor's Beach, says, "Written records about the breezy beach begin with its purchase by Col. Moore Fauntleroy from the Rappahannock Indians on April 4, 1651. Fauntlero
2 CONC y was born in 1610 in Southampton County, England, and established a series of plantations and mansions along the Rappahannock River. The property there was called Naylors Hole, possibly after John Naylor, a gentleman of Richmond 
2 CONC and King George counties." [http://www.ssentinel.com/index.php/rivah/article/a_quiet_beach_getaway_out_of_history_at_naylors_beach dead link]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT
2 CONT * “ Married Well and Often: Marriages of the Northern Neck of Virginia, 1649-1800.” Page 130.  [https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/5063/images/gpc_marriedwellandoften-0144?usePUB=true&_phsrc=XMj2&_phstart=successSour
2 CONC ce&usePUBJs=true&pId=12291 AncestryImage] 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Virginia Vital Records; Indexed by: Judith McGhan.  Page 523.  [https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/61462/images/vavitalrecords-000756-523?treeid=&personid=&hintid=&usePUB=true&usePUBJs=true&_ga=2.45366118.375339329
2 CONC .1611948395-1066166009.1611948395&pId=30011729 AncestryImage] 
2 CONT
2 CONT *”The Fauntleroy Family,” Mary Emily Fauntleroy.  Page 213.  [https://scholarworks.iu.edu/journals/index.php/imh/article/viewFile/7141/7996 link]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Lewis of Warner Hall: The History of a Family Including The Genealogy of Descendants in Both the Male and Female Lines page 814.  [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-67744-837/lewis-of-warner-hall-the-history-of-a-family
2 CONC -including#fullscreen MyHeritage]
2 CONT * Naylors Hole (historical) in Richmond County VA.  [https://virginia.hometownlocator.com/maps/feature-map,ftc,2,fid,1782071,n,naylors%20hole%20(historical).cfm link]  This feature was located at the present site of the community 
2 CONC of Naylors Beach. History:	Colonel Moore Fauntleroy settled here in 1651 and est. a shipping point to expand his family's export business, which operated in England. Elizabeth Fauntleroy lived here in 1752 and was proposed to by y
2 CONC oung Geo. Washington, who was turned down because 'she couldn't stand the sight of smallpox scars on his face.’ (US-T131/1986/Heritage of VA/p171
2 CONT * https://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I023427&tree=Tree1
