2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 10 OCT 1771
2 PLAC Brunswick,, Virginia, United States
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Isaac Collier, son of Charles and Judith (Myhill) Collier, is named as husband of Ann Vines in her father's will and names Myhill as son in his own will, proved 28 Oct 1771 in Brunswick Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann Vines was the daughter of Thomas Vines and Mary Hill. Thomas Vines died at Poto Bello on the York River. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===family 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Collier-988
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Collier's will, made July 8, 1771 and proved October 28, 1771,[3][4] names
2 CONT * his wife Ann[3]
2 CONT * son Myhill Collier[3]
2 CONT * son Vines Collier[3]
2 CONT * son Thomas Collier[3]
2 CONT * son Charles Collier[3]
2 CONT * son Isaac Collier[3]
2 CONT * daughter Elizabeth Smith[3]
2 CONT * daughter Judith Hicks[3]
2 CONT * daughter Ann Collier[3][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Isaac Collier
2 CONT
2 CONT WILL OF ISAAC COLLIER In the name of God Amen, I Isaac Collier of the County o f Brunswick in Virginia, Planter, being of sound mind and memory do make this my last Will and Testament in manner and form following - first I give my
2 CONC  soul to God my maker in hopes of a Joyfull Ressurection through the Merits of his Son my Saviour, and my Body to be intered in a decent manner at the discretion of my Executors hereafter named, and for all my Real and Personal Es
2 CONC tate with which it is plea sed Almighty God to bless one with after funeral charges and just debts are paid then I give in manner and form following.
2 CONT *ITEM I lend to my loving wife Ann Collier during her life and to my daughter Ann Collier while she lives single my Maner Plantation and to lend any part of my land as they shall think fit.
2 CONT *ITEM I give to my daughter Ann Collier four Negro's named Phibby and Annshay and Delsey and Pompy, and their Increases and a feather bed and furniture and four pewter dishes and a dozen of pewter plates and a iron pott and a pan 
2 CONC and a case of knives and forks and a box iron and hosters and a good horse and after my wife's death I give to her my dash and riding chair and two cows and calves to her and her heirs forever.
2 CONT *ITEM I give to my son Myhill Collier two Negro's named James and Daniel two cows and calves and---
2 CONT *ITEM I give to my son Vines Collier a Negro Boy named Dave that he is already got to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT *ITEM I give to my daughter Elizabeth Smith a Negro Woman named Emly that she has managed and all her other increases forever.
2 CONT *ITEM I give to my daughter Judith Hicks one Negro Girlnamed Eady and her increases forever.
2 CONT *ITEM I give to my son Thomas Collier the land I had upon the southeastside of Tuck's branch being about forty five acres more or less and two Cows and Calves to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT *ITEM I lend my loving wife Ann Collier six Negro's named Nick and Harry and Frank and Patt and Milly and Daphny and their Increases and all my household goods and stock of all kinds that I have not already disposed ofduring her n
2 CONC atural life.
2 CONT *ITEM after my wife's death I give the six Negro's lent to my wife named Nick and Harry and Frank and Patt and Milly and Daphny and their Increase to be equally divided between my son Vines Collier and my son Charles Collier and m
2 CONC y son Isaac Collier and my daughter Elizabeth Smith to them and their Heirs forever.
2 CONT *ITEM and after my wife's death I give all my household goods and stock of all kinds that I have not already disposed of to be equally divided between my son Vines Collier and my son Charles Collier and my son Isaac Collier and my
2 CONC  son Myhill Collier and my daughter Elizabeth Smith.
2 CONT *ITEM after my wife's death if my Daughter Ann Collier marries or dead I give the land I bought of Harrison to be equally divided between mysSon Isaac Collier and my son Myhill Collier and also the land where I now live that I bou
2 CONC ght of William Smith and the land I took up on the Norwest side of Tack's branch to be equally divided between them and their Heirs forever in all being six hundred and thirty acres more or less.
2 CONT *LASTLY I do hereby appoint my Son Vines Collier and my son Charles Collier and my son Isaac Collier Executors of thi s my last Will and Testament. 
2 CONT *IN WITNESS whereof I have he r unto set my hand and Seal this eighth day of July one thou sand seven hundred and seventy one.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Collier (seal) Signed Sealed published and Delivered to be the last will and testament of the above named Isaac Collier in Presence of us James Trotter William Hamilton James Hamilton.
2 CONT
2 CONT At a Court held for Brunswick County the 28th day of Octobe r 1771 this will was proved according to law by the Oath's of James Trotter and William Hamilton Witnesses thereto and directed to be recorded, and on the motion of Vines
2 CONC  Collier, Charles Collier and Isaac Collier the Executors therein named w ho made oath thereto and together with Benjamin -, Isham Winfield and Josiah Smith their Securities entered into and acknowledged Bond as the Law directs, C
2 CONC ertificate was granted them for obtaining a - thereof in pure form.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT *3.  “Collier Family," The William and Mary Quarterly, Vol. 9, No. 3 (Jan., 1901), pp. 183-185 (Omohundro Institute of Early American History and Culture; DOI: 10.2307/1920292). Stable URL: http://www.jstor.org/stable/1920292 [htt
2 CONC ps://books.google.com/books?id=NDsSAAAAYAAJ&lpg=PA185&ots=yT4lvfVttp&dq=Mary%20(Gaines)%20Collier&pg=PA184#v=onepage&q&f=true GoogleBooks]
2 CONT
