1 NAME John /Darling/
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Darling
2 _MARNM Darling
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Old Chestnut Hill Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Millville
3 STAE Massachusetts
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 12 JUL 1664
2 ADDR
3 CITY Braintree
3 STAE Massachusetts Bay Colony
1 DEAT
2 DATE 29 MAY 1753
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bellingham
3 STAE Province of Massachusetts
1 OCCU Farmer/ Weaver
1 TITL Capt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The old Cemetery Chestnut Hill, Millville Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Cornelius Darling were sons of Dennis Darling of Braintree. 
2 CONT '''Captain John was born before 1667 and had three wives and thirteen children.''' 
2 CONT His will in 1753 speaks of being "grown antient", and leaves twelve equal shares to 12 children.  
2 CONT He names sons John, Samuel and Ebenezer.  Daughter Anna Brown.  Children of daughter Elizabeth Brown deceased.  Children of daughter Ruth Thayer deceased.  Daughter Hannah Thompson.  Children of daughter Margaret French deceased. 
2 CONC  Daughter Mary Draper.  Daughter Martha Thompson.  Daughter Abigail Verry.  Children of daughter of Deborah Bosworth deceased.  
2 CONT He died at age 90 [Suffolk County Probate Records xlviii, p. 196].
2 CONT
2 CONT # His first wife was Elizabeth Thompson, probably daughter of John Thompson of Mendon.  
2 CONT # His second wife was Anne Rockwood, daughter of John and Joanna (Ford) Rockwood, whom he married in Boston on 1/2/1687-8.  She died in Mendon 4/30/1690.  
2 CONT # Elizabeth Morse was his 3rd wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John Darling was a great businessman and a landed proprietor.  
2 CONT He was designated with 20 others in the setting off of Bellingham from Mendon in 1719.  
2 CONT He had 13 children!
2 CONT
2 CONT The brothers, John and Cornelius, were both weavers as well as farmers. 
2 CONT Samuel, the third signer of the Bellingham petition of this family, was John's son. 
2 CONT Fifteen Darlings were born in town before 1750, and 115 before 1850.
2 CONT
2 CONT "May ye 21th 1718. being a publick Meeting of the propriators of that Tract of Land belonging to Dedham Westward of Wrentham in order to choose a Committe To make their adress to ye Town of Dedham relateing the Church lott sold by
2 CONC  some of ye church of Dedham, to Josiah Thayer, ye propriators then Mett at ye hous of Ebenezer Thayer on sd land & chose Leut John Darling Moderator and so proceded to choose a committee which were Leut. John Darling, Ensn Jonas 
2 CONC Draper & Ensn John Tompson and Impowere them to address ye Town of Dedham for Releaf with referance to sd Church Lott & also get the Selectmen take care that some persons be appointed to Lay out highways in sd tract of land to acc
2 CONC omodate ye Inhabitance & Likewise to preambulate Line between them & Wrentham."
2 CONT
2 CONT The distance from Dedham, the lack of help in laying roads, the futility of paying toward a government that was distant and spending its money in ways not benefitting the landowners, led to the inevitable conclusion that a new tow
2 CONC n should be formed. A petition went first to Dedham. The debate within the town government was not long. The area was not crucial to the town nor was it particularly desirable. Governing these remote lands may not have been any mo
2 CONC re attractive to the governing than to the governed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The reply came May 11, 1719: "This day the Inhabitants of this town in that track of land lying between Mendon and Wrentham presented a petition to this town praying that they may be set off from this town in order to a township t
2 CONC he town have granted it provided they can unite and encorporat together with the farms adjacent and some Assistance and Inlargement from the towns of Mendon and Wrentham so as to capassatate them to manage the affairs of a town an
2 CONC d have the approbation of the General Court."
2 CONT
2 CONT The area to be incorporated included The Rawson Farms located north of the Charles River and portions of Mendon located east of the Charles River and west of the Farms. The area belonging to Dedham was not clearly defined. A map i
2 CONC ncluded with the petition showed a town approximately seven miles long bordered to the south by Attleborough. The latter may have been correct, but the homes of many of the signers were actually in what is now Woonsocket, making t
2 CONC he Town closer to fifteen miles in length at that time. With the exception of a few minor changes and a clear definition of the southern boundary in 1745, Bellingham's borders have been unchanged since that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once that hurdle was crossed, the consent of the colonial legislature was required. The settlers met again and prepared their petition. This was not a step taken lightly, and even though the path was determined, it was several mon
2 CONC ths before the petition reached the General Court. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 26, 1719 "A Petition of John Darling, Nicholas Cook and 30 others, Inhabitants of a remote part of Dedham, some Farms, and 4 Inhabitants of Mendon", was presented to the House and Read, praying, that they may be consti
2 CONC tuted a Township. No name was requested within the petition.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the tradition of the time the petition went first to the House. The record does not show whether there was any debate or discussion. With the approval of the ruling town body already given, it was unlikely that there was a grea
2 CONC t deal of debate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In any case a vote was taken and it was: "Ordered, That the prayer of the Petition be Granted, and that a Township be Erected and Constituted accordingly thereunto, and the Plat therewith exhibited, with all Powers and Priveleges 
2 CONC belonging to a Town. Provided, They procure and settle a Learned Orthodox Minister, within the space of three Years next coming, and that John Darling, John Tompson and John Marsh be Impowered, to call a Town Meeting any time in M
2 CONC arch next, to choose Town Officers and manage the other prudential Affairs of the Town; the name of the Town to be called Westham. Sent up for Concurrence."
2 CONT
2 CONT The General Court was a two bodied legislature. The House action had to be followed by passage by the Council (closer to the present Senate than the Governors Council of today). The Town of Westham had only this final hurdle to ma
2 CONC ke in order to become an independent town.
2 CONT
2 CONT Westham's final vote came the next day. The Council did not grant Westham the independence requested. Less than twenty four hours after its birth, Westham went down to defeat. The reason for its death is not in the records. A sear
2 CONC ch of the Massachusetts Archives does not show the whys and the wherefores, only a simple statement of the Council's action.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 27, 1719: "The Vote of this House on the Petition of John Darling &c. Pass'd the 26. Currant, Sent down fron the Board pass'd on there, viz. In Council Nov. 27th 1719. Read, and Concurr'd with this Vote excepting the N
2 CONC ame to be Bellingham. Read and Concurr'd."
2 CONT
2 CONT Chapter 96 of the Province Laws thus created the Town of Bellingham in answer to the petition of its settlers. Approved on November 27, 1719, the Town now had to go forward with the chores of independence.
2 CONT
2 CONT The petition presented to the General Court as preserved in the Massachusetts Archives is as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT "November 17, 1719
2 CONT
2 CONT "To his Exelency Samuel Shute Esqer, Capt General & Governer in Chief in & over his majesties Province of ye Massachusits Bay in New england and to ye Honourable Council & house of Representatives in General Court conveined at Bos
2 CONC ton. The Petition of the Inhabitance of a Tract of Land belonging to Dedham westward of Wrentham and ye Inhabitance of a Considerable Farm ajdoyning Thereto And ye Inhabitance of a Small Corner of ye Townshipe of Mendon ajdacent T
2 CONC hereto (to ye number of four families) Humbly Sheweth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT That Whereas ye Above Sd Inhabitance are scituated at a Remoat Distance from ye Respective Towns where They at Present belong: (viz) The inhabitance of the Town of Dedham to ye number of three & twenty families are about Twenty mi
2 CONC les Distance from the Town where they belong & Do douty & being very Remoate from ye Publick worshipe of God. & The inhabitance of the number of 13 families of ye above sd Farme being six or seven Miles Distance from ye place of p
2 CONC ublick worshipe and ye inhabitance of Mendon aforesd being about four miles Distance and Considering our remoateness & ye inconvenianceys we Labour under by reason of the Same: and that ye uniteing & Incorporating of ye above Sd T
2 CONC racts togeather & makeing of them a Town may put us into a way in Some Conveniant Time to obtain ye Settlement of ye Gospel among us; & (the Uniteing of ye above sd Tracts of Land togeather will make a Town of about Seven Miles Lo
2 CONC ng and Three miles & half wide) and Further Considering yt the Inhabitance of ye Sd Tract of Dedham Land & the Farms are alredy incoporated into a Training Companie & that they have Little or no benefit of Town prevelidges or by h
2 CONC aveing benefit of ye Schools we Do Respectively pay To. The whole number of Families belonging to ye above Sd tracts being forty & Lands enough alredy Laid out to accomodate 20 or 30 more. The Inhabitance of Dedham Land being Voat
2 CONC ed off by ye Town for that end.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our Prayer Therefore is That Your Honours would Graciously plase to Consider our Diffecult Circumstances and grant us our petition which is That ye above Mentioned Tracts of Land (as by one plate hereto afixed & described) may be 
2 CONC Incorporated togeather & Made a Town, & Invested with Town prevelidges. That so we may be Inabled in Conveniant Time to obtain ye Gosple & publick worshipe of God Settled, & our Inconveniancies by Reason of our Remoatness be Remov
2 CONC ed: granting us such Time of Dispence from publick Taxes as in Wisdom you shall Think Conveniant, & in your so doing you will Greatly oblige us who am your Humble petitioners and for your Honours, as in Consciance we are Bound, Sh
2 CONC all forever Pray. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dated ye 17th Day of November: 1719."
2 CONT
2 CONT John Darling            Isaac Thayer	          Samll Tompson	
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolas Cook           Ebenezer Tompson   Samll Darling	
2 CONT
2 CONT Palatiah Smith         Richard Blood             Joseph Tompson	
2 CONT
2 CONT Thot Burch              Joseph Holbrook         Nathaniel Weatherly	
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tompson        Zuriel Hall                    Samll Smith	
2 CONT
2 CONT Ebenezer Thayer    Daniel Corbet		
2 CONT
2 CONT Cornelius Darling   William Hayward         The Inhabitance of Mendon	
2 CONT
2 CONT Samll Hayward        James Smith	           John Holbrook	
2 CONT
2 CONT Samll Rich	         Nicolas Cooke Jur        John Corbit	
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tompson Jur  Jonathan Hayward       Peter Holbrook	
2 CONT
2 CONT Seth Cook	         Eliphelet Holbrook	
2 CONT
2 CONT Accompanying the petition was "A Rough Plat of the Several Tracts of Land herewith petition for To Make a Town as Scituate & Joyning Each to other with a Demonstration of what Land They Bound upon as Taken by the scale of a mile t
2 CONC o an Inch."
2 CONT
2 CONT Added to the bottom of the page with the map was the following reply:
2 CONT
2 CONT "In the House of Representatives Nover 26th 1719.  Read & Ordered that the Prayer of this Petition be Granted & That a Township be Erected & Constituted according thereunto & the Plat above. Provided They Procure & Settle a learne
2 CONC d orthodox minister within the space of three years next coming.  And that John Darling, John Thomson, & John Marsh be Impowered to Call a Town Meeting anytime in March next to choose Town Officers & manage the other prudential af
2 CONC fairs of the Town. The name of the Town to be called Westham.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sent up for Concurrence
2 CONT
2 CONT In Council Nov 27, 1719 John Burrill, Speaker
2 CONT
2 CONT Read & Concur'd with this Vote accepting the Name to be Bellingham" [History of the Town of Bellingham 1660-1780].
2 CONT --------------------
