1 TITL Captain
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for MASSACHUSETTS with the rank of CAPTAIN.  [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A041858  DAR Ancestor # A041858]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT English Noble House of Forester
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Timothy and family were the first settlers of the interior of Maine - at Pondtown, later known as Winthrop.
2 CONT Timothy and all of his living sons served in the American Revolution. Four sons went "privateering" and were never heard from again.
2 CONT The home of Timothy's wife, Sible, is standing and occupied in Winthrop. A memorial brass plaque was placed on a boulder in the front yard of the home by the local DAR chapter. This memorial was photographed in 1987 by Gayle and R
2 CONC on Foster, and has been shared on many internet sites.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Foster-800
2 CONT
2 CONT Timothy Foster 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Timothy Foster (1720 - 1785)===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Timothy Foster===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 14 May 1720 in Town of Attleborough, Bristol, Massachusetts Bay
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of John Foster III and Margaret (Ware) Foster
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of John Foster, Robert Foster, Robert Foster, Ebenezer Foster, Margaret Foster, Benjamin Foster, Jonathan Foster, Sarah Foster, Dorothy Foster, Nathan Foster Sr., Esther Foster, Michael Foster, Michael Foster and Mary (Fos
2 CONC ter) Walcott
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Sible Sibula (Freeman) Foster — married 22 May 1745 in Attleboro, Bristol County, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Timothy Foster Jr., Bela Foster, Eliphalet Foster, Susannah Foster, David Foster, Thomas Foster, Steuart Foster, John Foster, Oliver Foster, Sybilla (Foster) Stevens and Stephen Foster
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 3 Apr 1785 at age 64 in Winthrop, Kennebec, Maine, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Foster-800 created 1 Mar 2011 | Last modified 11 Feb 2023
2 CONT
2 CONT Source
2 CONT
2 CONT FOSTER GENEALOGY by Frederick Clifton Pierce, pub 1899 - see attached PDF;
2 CONT HISTORY OF WINTHROP MAINE - by Stackpole;
2 CONT "Massachusetts Births and Christenings, 1639-1915," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:VQDB-ZVY : 4 December 2014), Timothy Foster in entry for Timothy Foster, 21 Mar 1745; citing ATTLEBORO,BRISTOL,MAS
2 CONC SACHUSETTS, ; FHL microfilm 0982348 IT 1.
2 CONT "Maine, Veterans Cemetery Records, 1676-1918," database with images, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:KXQ8-SJ8 : 4 December 2014), Timothy Foster, ; citing Death, , State Archives, Augusta; FHL microfilm 1,001
2 CONC ,838.
2 CONT Find-a-Grave Memorial - Metcalf Cemetery - https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/215647297/timothy-foster
2 CONT Wikipedia Article - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timothy_Foster_(settler)
2 CONT DAR Patriot Listing - https://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A041858
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT PROBABLY NO GRAVESTONE -  Information on place of burial for Captain Timothy Foster is from MAINE VETERANS CEMETERY RECORDS 1767-1918 available on familysearch.org.  Image attached.   Timothy Foster was the son of John Foster and 
2 CONC Margaret (Ware) Foster.  The Foster family was included in Stackpole's History of Winthrop and in FOSTER GENEALOGY by F. C. Pierce, pub in 1899.   Their immigrant ancestor, John Foster, arrived in 1635 from England and was a resid
2 CONC ent of Salem, Massachusetts.   FIRST SETTLERS - Timothy Foster and his family were the first settlers of Winthrop. REVOLUTIONARY WAR - Captain Timothy Foster was a Revolutionary War Veteran, and has been recognized as a patriot by
2 CONC  the Daughters of the American Revolution.   Eight sons of Timothy and Sible served in the American Revolution. MORE INFORMATION - See Wikipedia article "Timothy Foster - Settler"  and Wikitree profile Foster-800
2 CONT --------
2 CONT Early Cemetery  Locations from Thurston's History of Winthrop, pg 27 BURYING PLACES. At a town meeting, May 27, 1771, the Selectmen, according to instructions, " reported a burying place." The spot which they judged would best " c
2 CONC ommode the present inhabitants, lies upon the highway between Mr. Bishop's and Mr. Cha- [record torn off,] - on Mr. Pullen's lot, bounded  southerly on said highway. The land contains one acre, lying in a square form. Timothy Fost
2 CONC er, Ichabod How, Jonathan Whiting, Selectmen."   The next cemetery was laid out March 10, 1777. Micajah Dudley, John Chandler, Timothy Foster, James Craig, and Ransford Smith, were appointed a committee to select suitable places f
2 CONC or burying the dead.  " March 18, 1780, Voted to accept one acre of land for a burying place, near Mr. Chandler's, part Mr. Chandler's, and part Mr. Lambert's, and part common land, as it is bounded by the committee." This is the 
2 CONC cemetery at the village.   June, 1795, a committee of nine were appointed to select places for burying the dead, and to see on what terms suitable places could be had. The town records contain no report of this committee. In Augus
2 CONC t, the same year, the town chose a committee of three to see on what conditions the town can have burying places. They were instructed to purchase three, one of Mr. Stephen Pullen, one of Mr. John Chandler, and one of Mr. Benjamin
2 CONC  Fairbanks. There are now five places for the interment of the dead,  in the town, one in East Winthrop, one in the south-east part of the town, another in the METCALF NEIGHBORHOOD, which was the FIRST, one at the village, and ano
2 CONC ther west of the village, near Dea. Stanley's.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/215647297/timothy-foster Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 17 2021, 22:34:18 U
2 CONC TC''
