1 NOTE {geni:about_me} At 14 years of age became soldier in the Revolutionary War.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen Foster was the son of Captain Timothy Foster. He was the first white child born in Winthrop. From a young age, he had a love of hunting and fishing. He had a robust constitution, and so at the age of 14 was accepted into t
2 CONC he service as a soldier of the Revolutionary War.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married first to Sally Streeter, and second to Diadema (Heton) Johnson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1785 Winthrop became too thickly settled for him, so Stephen relocated to Leeds. He settled near in Leeds on the south east side of Androscoggin Lake, today near Leeds Center, near where the railroad track crosses Bishop Hill r
2 CONC oad. He built a cabin and moved in his wife and children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though Stephen was a farmer, he devoted a great deal of time to hunting and fishing. Due to his well-known expertise, he was called "Hunter" Foster. In 1842 he made a trip to Augusta to collect his Revolutionary War pension. On th
2 CONC e way he met Zadoc Bishop, who asked him, as an expert in woodcraft, to tell him the name of a suspicious looking shrub. Stephen assured him the plant was harmless and to prove it, he ate some. Shortly after, Stephen became violen
2 CONC tly ill and was taken to the house of his uncle Enos Fairbanks in Winthrop. Stephen died there on April 2.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/83760204/stephen-foster Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/pro
2 CONC jects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 17 2021, 22:34:18 UTC''
2 CONT
