1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===William Moulton, Jr.
2 CONT * Born 25 May 1664 in Hampton, Norfolk, Massachusetts Bay Colony
2 CONT * Died Oct 1732 at age 68 in Newbury, Essex, Province of Massachusetts Bay
2 CONT * Son of William Moulton and Margaret Page
2 CONT *Husband of Abigail Webster  — married 27 May 1685 in Newbury, Essex, Massachusetts Bay Colony.  Daughter of John Webster Jr and Anne Batt.  
2 CONT * Husband of Sarah (Clark) Ordway — married 1730 in Newbury, Essex, Province of Massachusetts Bay.  Daughter of John Clarke and Mary Poore.   Widow of James Ordway.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT Founder of the Moulton family of silversmiths; example [http://i.ebayimg.com/t/18th-CENTURY-AMERICAN-COIN-SILVER-SHOE-KNEE-BUCKLE-/00/s/NTM1WDcyMA==/$(KGrHqN,!okE63(r1M7FBO53+pcESw~~60_3.JPG Colonial shoe or silver knee buckle (ar
2 CONC tist unknown)]. "Mr. Towle informed the writer that many a time when he worked with the Moultons the old silver knee buckles made by their ancestors had been brought to their store for sale.  They were relics of a vanished age ; t
2 CONC he fashion of wearing them had passed away, and they were bought as old silver and melted up. Had they been spared till now, they would have brought their weight in gold, for the work of the ancient Moulton silversmiths is in grea
2 CONC t demand now, as a souvenir of colonial times." (Moulton Annals)
2 CONT
2 CONT from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Towle_Silversmiths Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT >Although [http://www.towlesilver.com/ Towle Silversmiths] was not founded until 1857 (as Towle & Jones) and then 1873 (as A.F. Towle & Son); its progenitors included several members of the Moulton family, whose silversmiths dynas
2 CONC ty is claimed to have the longest continuous span of silversmithing of any American family. From father to son, this family produced silversmiths for two hundred years.
2 CONT
2 CONT >In 1637, William Moulton (1615-1664) came together with his two brothers John and Thomas from Ormesby, Norfolk, England, and settled on Winnacunnet Road in Hampton, New Hampshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT >>In 1664 his son '''William Moulton''' II (1664-1732) was born. In 1682 at the age of 18, William Moulton II left the family farm in Hampton and settled near the Merrimack River in a section of Newbury, Massachusetts that would l
2 CONC ater become Newburyport. By some accounts, he was the first in six generations of silversmiths. While he did buy and sell silver goods, he was basically a general trader.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Abigail had nine children:
2 CONT * 1) Abigail - born June 13, 1686 Newbury, married Samuel Bartlett of Newbury
2 CONT * 2) Batt - born July 4, 1688, married 1st Hannah Sibly/Sible of Salem December 4, 1712 Newbury, 2nd Jemina George of Amesbury May 29,      1730, he died 1750
2 CONT * 3) William - born 1690, 1st married Sarah____,2nd Ruth Emery April 24, 1716 Newbury, he died 1762
2 CONT * 4) Jonathan - born September 7, 1692, married Rebecca Chase December 5, 1716, he died January 26, 1717
2 CONT * 5) Joseph - born November 25, 1694 Newbury, married Mary Noyes July 25, 1717
2 CONT * 6) Stephen - born February 14, 1697/8, married Rebecca Chase December 14, 1721, moved to Rehoboth, MA
2 CONT * 7) Margaret - born February 21, 1698/9, she died September 25, 1701
2 CONT * 8) Sarah - born July 4, 1701, married Ezekiel Moulton of Hampton July 4, 1727, she died August 7, 1783
2 CONT * 9) Mary - born August 2, 1705, married  _______ Morse
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT from [http://books.google.com/books?id=_c4UAAAAYAAJ&lpg=PA919&ots=GRzD1a6uss&dq=William%20Moulton%20Abigail%20Webster&pg=PA919#v=onepage&q=William%20Moulton%20Abigail%20Webster&f=false New England Families, Genealogical and Memori
2 CONC al: A Record of the Achievements of Her People in the Making of Commonwealths and the Founding of a Nation edited by William Richard Cutter Published by Lewis historical publishing company, 1915]  pg. 919
2 CONT
2 CONT II) William (2), son of William (i) Moulton, was born May 25, 1664, died in 1732. His will was dated October 12, 1732, and proved October 30, 1732. He owned land in Amesbury and Salisbury, MA, and his estate was valued at one thou
2 CONC sand four hundred and thirty-three pounds, seven shillings. In various deeds on record he was called a weaver, inn holder, trader and merchant. He was the first of the famous Moulton silversmiths. The "Towle Manufacturing Company,
2 CONC " one of the finest silverware manufactories in the world, has grown up from his small shop. Mr. Towle learned the trade from William's son Joseph, and his son William took the business after his death. William Moulton lived at Ne
2 CONC wbury and here began the manufacture of silver shoe buckles and some silver spoons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the church records the following entry is found : "Dinah, Indian, Servant to William Moulton, joined 1698." He married (first) May 27, 1685, Abigail, daughter of John Webster Jr., son of John Webster, of Ipswich. John Webster J
2 CONC r. was the ancestor of the famous Daniel Webster, and Abigail Webster was a cousin of Hannah Dustin, who was held in captivity by the Indians. Abigail Moulton died July 24, 1723. He married (second) Sarah , who survived him.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT from [http://www.ebooksread.com/authors-eng/henry-william-moulton/moulton-annals-luo/page-25-moulton-annals-luo.shtml Moulton Annals. By Henry William Moulton, Claribel Moulton.  Published by Edward A. Claypool, 1906.  Moulton Ann
2 CONC als.] page 329
2 CONT
2 CONT The primitive Colonial silversmith of New England was 
2 CONT William Moulton 2 , whose first silver shoe buckles appeared 
2 CONT about 1690, and his son Joseph Moulton was the first goldsmith  of New England whose successors have continued the business  down to the present time. This enterprising father and son  were forerunners of the great army of craftsm
2 CONC en, who, two  centuries later, offer magnificent products in the precious  metals, turning out an infinite variety of spoons, cutlery, hol-  low-ware and jewelry, in all the splendor of the modern art. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Yea, more. Out of a rude "Black-smith & White-smith  shop" of about 1690 has grown through eight generations, in  the direct line, one of the most superb solid silver-ware fac-  tories in the world. The stately and extensive works
2 CONC  of the  "Towle Manufacturing Company," located in Newburyport,  business. We, therefore, claim this large and successful manu-  factory as the legitimate successor of "Little William," who  founded the Moulton family and the silv
2 CONC er and goldsmiths  business in Newbury, away back in the far past, when the  Indian still sped over its waters in his light canoe. I cannot  forbear to sketch this ancestor, whose courage and enterprise  were conspicuous in days w
2 CONC hen life was made up of a continual  battle with a dreary climate, a barren land in a wilderness of  savage beasts and savage men, environed by poverty! Yet  cheerful and brave was this first silversmith born in America. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT William MOULTON. Born 25 MAY 1664. He married (1) 27 MAY 1685 to Abigail WEBSTER, daughter of John WEBSTER, Jr. of Ipswich, Massachusetts. She died 24 JUL 1723. He married (2) Sarah. He owned land in Amesbury and Salisbury. He is 
2 CONC called in various records, Weaver, Inn Holder, Trader, Merchant. He had a shop Near Moulton Hill in Newbury, and he made silver buckles and ornaments. His will was dated 12 OCT 1732, and was proved on 30 OCT 1732.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT WILLIAM MOULTON JR. (1664–1732) was born on May 25, 1664 in Hampton, Rockingham, New Hampshire, and married Abigail Webster on May 27, 1685. There may have been a second marriage to Sarah Clark in 1728. He died in October of 1732 
2 CONC in Newbury, Essex, Massachusetts.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT William Moulton, married Abigail Webster on May 27, 1685.  She died July 24, 1723.  He was a young man of energy and returned to Moulton Hill (as it was called) in Newbury in 1683.  He was a weaver, inn holder, trader and merchant
2 CONC . He built a house at the foot of the hill for him and his family and opened a "Blacksmith & Whitesmith" shop, making silver buckles and ornaments.  On a corner timber of the house was the Moulton Coat of Arms. His home was occupi
2 CONC ed by seven generations of Moulton's for two hundred years.   See Moulton Silver.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://moultonfamilysearch.weebly.com/newbury-ma.html
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
