2 CONT
2 CONT Obadiah Bruen (son of John Bruen)33, 34, 35 was born 1606 in Bruen Stapleford, Cheshire, England, and died 1681 in Newark, N.J..
2 CONT  Includes NotesNotes for Obadiah Bruen: Second son represented the town at the General Court 1647 - 1651 He was the second son of John Bruen, Esq. of Bruen Stapleford, Cheshire, England.He is a descendant of Robert Le Brun of Stap
2 CONC leford 1230. He came to America to the Plymouth jurisdiction from England with his wife, Sarah, in 1640 (between 1630 - 1642 some 16,000 settlers arrived from England.Known as the Great Migration). In 1642 he removed to Glouster a
2 CONC nd was made a freeman and a selectman in the following year and represented the town at the General Court in 1647 - 51/2.He removed to Pequot (New London, Conn) and was Clerk of the town for fifteen years.He was also elected to th
2 CONC e General Court, and is named in the Charter of Connecticut Colony in 1662.In 1664 Duke of York gave grant of New Netherland (from Connecticut to the Delaware) to Sir George Carteret.After his arrival the governor despatched messe
2 CONC ngers into New England to invite emigrants.Obadiah came to Newark, N.J. with the Milford colonists, in 1666 and his name is second on the list of the Fundamental Agreement, signed June 1667.His "Home Lott" and residence was on Mar
2 CONC ket Street (a continuation of the Old Ferry Road to New York), not far from the present Pennsylvania Railroad depot.The town was named Newark in honor of the Rev. Abraham Pierson, the pastor of Milford, who had been ordained at Ne
2 CONC wark, England. "Ormerod's Cheshire" provides pedigree reaching back in an unbroken line to Robert Le Brun of Stapleford 1230. English Origins of new England Families from the New England Historical and Genealogical Register:First 
2 CONC Series , Volume I "Obadiah Bruen of Cape Anne, Alias Gloucester in New England.Obadiah Bruen, youngest son of John Bruen of Bruen Staple county of Chester, Eng., bap. Dec. 22, 1606, came to Plymouth with Rev. Richard Blynman --wen
2 CONC t afterward to Gloucester-- was made freeman in 1643; clerk of the writs, and commissioner to and small causes, in 1643; was selectman and representative -- went to new London, 1650, and was there recorder many years, and one of t
2 CONC he patentees of the Colony of Connecticut.He emigrated to Newark, N.J. in company with about fifty families and bought the place of the Indians in 1667.He had a wife, Sarah, and two children born in Gloucester; Hannah in 1643 and 
2 CONC John in 1646." (this is the same time William Miller emigrated.England was in turmoil due to King Charles I desire for absolute power over both state and church.He was supported by Archbishop Laud.The on-going clash between Cathol
2 CONC ic and Protestant religious views was at issue as it had been for more than 200 years) More About Obadiah Bruen: Baptism: 22 Dec 1606, Bruen Stapleford, Cheshire, Eng.. Election: 1643, Glouster, Mass.. Immigration: 1639, Plymouth 
2 CONC Jurisdiction. Children of Obadiah Bruen are:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hannah Bruen, b. 1643, Glouster, Mass., d. date unknown.
2 CONT     +John Bruen, b. 02 Jun 1646, Glouster, Mass.36, d. 1695, Newark, N.J..
2 CONT     Rebecca Bruen, b. Abt. 1648, Glouster, Mass., d. date unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT _________________
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral certificate of "John Bruen of Bruen Stableford, in the countyof Chester, Esquier, departed this mortall life the 18 January 1625 [-6],at his hall of Bruen Stableford, and was buried in Tarvin Church, in thecounty afors
2 CONC ayd" states he married 3 wives. The sayd defunct married tohis 2 wife, Anne, daughter to William Fox of Rodes, in the county ofLancaster, and hath yssue Samuell, Obadiah, and Kathrine Bruen, allunmarried [The Record Society of Lon
2 CONC don, Volume VI, pages 50,51].
2 CONT
2 CONT Obadiah became a draper at Shrewsbury, County Salop, England. "30th dayof September, in the 9th year of the reign of King Charles of England..... Obadiah Bruen of the Vill of Salop draper, son of John Bruen ofStapleford in the cou
2 CONC nty palatine of Chester is admitted a free burgessof the Vill of Salop aforesaid ..... with full assent and consent to haveenjoy and use all liberties and privileges of the same Vill, who saysupon his oath that he has no issue bef
2 CONC ore this session lawfully begottenand thereupon he is sworn in the manner of a burgess and pays for fine Vli together with the due and accustomed fee ....." [Roll of Freeman ofShrewsbury, Shropshire, England, translated from the L
2 CONC atin, Town Recordsof Shrewsbury, 1633].
2 CONT
2 CONT Obadiah emigrated to New England in 1639. He enlisted as a freeman of Plymouth Colony and settled in Marshfield, Massachusetts, 1640. He moved to Gloucester, Massachusetts, where he was made a freeman on 19 May 1642.He was clerk o
2 CONC f the writs, and commissioner to end small causes in 1643.He was selectman in 1642 and several following years, and representativeat the General Court, 1647 to 1651.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Reverend Mr. Benjamin Blinman removed from Gloucester, Massachusetts,to New London, Connecticut, then called Pequot Harbor, in 1650. "Heseems to have been accompanied on his first visit by Obadiah Bruen, a manof unusual intell
2 CONC igence and education, and sound mind and judgment."[History of New London, Connecticut, Frances Manwaring Caulkins, NewLondon, 1895, page iv.]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1651, Obadiah went to New London, Connecticut, and lived there sixteenyears. For fifteen years, he was Town Clerk of New London, Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT Obadiah Bruen held the following offices by colonial authority inConnecticut and New Haven Colonies; Patentee, Royal Charter, 1662; DeputyJudge (New London town), April 1660, May 1660; Judge (New London town),May 1662, May 1663, O
2 CONC ctober 1663, May 1664, May 1665; Deputy (New London)to Connecticut Legislature, October 1665.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 24 June 1667, Obadiah Bruen signed the Fundamental Agreement of 30October 1666, an agreement at Branford, Connecticut, to move to Newark,New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 11 July 1667, Obadiah and four others signed the purchase agreement on behalf of the settlers for the land in New Jersey, now the City of Newark, acquired from the Hackensack Indians.
2 CONT
2 CONT He moved there as one of the founders. His "Home Lott" and residence inNew Jersey was on Market Street, not far from the present Pennsylvania Railroad depot in Newark. The second common Gate next to the GreatRiver, was disposed of
2 CONC  to make and maintain as his division of fences toMr. Bruen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Facts about this person:
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptism December 25, 1606 Tarvin, England
