1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Margaret Mainwaring1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8
2 CONT *'''F, #10992, b. circa 1521, d. before 2 September 1590
2 CONT *Father      Sir Randall Mainwaring2,3,4,5,6,7,8 b. c 1495, d. 6 Sep 1557
2 CONT *Mother     Elizabeth Brereton2,3,4,5,7,8 d. bt 30 Nov 1545 - 1551
2 CONT
2 CONT *''' Margaret Mainwaring was born circa 1521 at of Over Peover, Cheshire, England. She married Sir Arthur Mainwaring, Sheriff of Shropshire, Justice of the Peace for Shropshire, son of Sir Richard Mainwaring, Sheriff of Shropshire
2 CONC  and Dorothy Corbet, circa 1540; 
2 CONT
2 CONT They had 1 son
2 CONT *  (Sir George)
2 CONT  and 3 daughters
2 CONT *  (Elizabeth, wife of Sir Thomas Aston; 
2 CONT * Margery; &
2 CONT *  Mary, wife of Richard Cotton, Esq.).2,3,4,5,6,7,8 Margaret Mainwaring died before 2 September 1590 at of Ightfield, Shropshire, England.2,4,7
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Family      Sir Arthur Mainwaring, Sheriff of Shropshire, Justice of the Peace for Shropshire b. c 1520
2 CONT *Children
2 CONT **Mary Mainwaring+9,2,4,6,7 b. c 1541, d. b 14 Jun 1578
2 CONT **Elizabeth Mainwaring10,4 b. c 1548, d. 9 Aug 1601
2 CONT **Sir George Mainwaring10,4 b. c 1560
2 CONT
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S2799] Unknown author, Plantagenet Ancestry of 17th Century Colonists, by David Faris, p. 1, 181; Burke's Dormant and Extinct Baronetcies, p. 335.
2 CONT * 2.[S5] Douglas Richardson, Plantagenet Ancestry, p. 486.
2 CONT * 3.[S5] Douglas Richardson, Plantagenet Ancestry, p. 557.
2 CONT * 4.[S16] Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry, 2nd Edition, Vol. III, p. 94.
2 CONT * 5.[S16] Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry, 2nd Edition, Vol. III, p. 279.
2 CONT * 6.[S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. I, p. 106-107.
2 CONT * 7.[S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. IV, p. 2.
2 CONT * 8.[S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. IV, p. 283.
2 CONT * 9.[S5] Douglas Richardson, Plantagenet Ancestry, p. 33.
2 CONT * 10.[S11576] A Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Extinct and Dormant Baronetcies of England, Ireland, and Scotland, by John Burke, Esq. and John Bernard Burke, Esq., p. 25.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p366.htm#i10992
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''MAINWARING, Sir Arthur (c.1525-90), of Ightfield, Salop.
2 CONT *b. c.1525, 1st. s. of Sir Richard Mainwaring by Dorothy, da. of Robert Corbet of Moreton Corbet, Salop. educ. G. Inn 1542. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m. by 1557, Margaret, da. and coh. of Sir Randolph Mainwaring''' of Peover, Cheshire, 1s. George 3da. Kntd. Oct. 1547. suc. fa. 1558.1
2 CONT
2 CONT *Mainwaring was a considerable landowner, much of whose property lay outside Shropshire. The death of his father-in-law in September 1557 brought him extensive estates, from the Cheshire branch of his family, in Bradley, Stretton 
2 CONC and elsewhere in that county, together with houses in the city of Chester. In Shropshire he owned the manor of Ightfield, a large house at Cotton, and land in Steele, Whitchurch and Whixall. Through his mother he was related to Si
2 CONC r Andrew Corbet, the other Shropshire knight of the shire in 1559.3
2 CONT
2 CONT *Soon after he came of age Mainwaring served at Berwick under John Dudley†, Earl of Warwick, who knighted him there in October 1547. During the last year of Mary’s reign he was in the north again for the Scottish campaign, in char
2 CONC ge of 200 Shropshire men, and in May 1560 he took part in the attack on Leith.4
2 CONT
2 CONT *After this he settled in the ‘rude and barbarous soil’ of Shropshire, where he was an active official for nearly 30 years. The bishop of Chester classified him as favourable to the established church in 1564. Mainwaring was a dep
2 CONC uty lieutenant at the time of the Armada, and in June 1588 sent a reprimand to officials of several hundreds in the county for not furnishing adequate information about arms and equipment. He died 2 Sept. 1590, having made his wil
2 CONC l that day, asking to be buried in Ightfield parish church. He left the greater part of his property to the executor and heir, George, who was then over 40. The only other members of the family mentioned were a daughter, Elizabeth
2 CONC , wife of Thomas Aston, her son Arthur, and Mainwaring’s brother James of Cotton.5
2 CONT
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/mainwaring-sir-arthur-1525-90
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT *MAINWARING, George (bef.1551-1628), of Ightfield, Salop.
2 CONT ** b. bef. 1551, o.s. of '''Sir Arthur Mainwaring by Margaret, da. and coh. of Sir Randolph Mainwaring''' of Peover, Cheshire. educ. Shrewsbury 1562; I. Temple 1565. 
2 CONT * m. Anne, da. of William More I of Loseley, Surr., 4s. 1da. suc. fa. 1590. Kntd. c.1593.
2 CONT
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/mainwaring-george-1551-1628 
2 CONT
2 CONT The above was doubled entered probably as a result of a merge, I deleted  to  clean-up profile - JEB
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Source: The Genealogist, vol. 5, pg. 158-171, by Neil D. Thompson, F.A.S.G. Source: Royal Descents of 500 Immigrants, Gary Boyd Roberts (1993) Source: Weis Ancestral Roots, 6th edition (1988) Source: Medieval English Ancestors of 
2 CONC Robert Abell;Carl Boyer, 3rd (2001) pg171 Source: Royal Ancestry: A Study in Colonial and Medieval Families (Douglas Richardson); 2013; Vol. 4:1 Source: AFN: HNJ3-VF
