1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Mackworth-76
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Agnes Mackworth''' was the daughter of Richard Mackworth, Esq. and Dorothy Cranage.[1][2][3] Her date of birth is unknown, but she was likely born by 1607, based on her age at her first marriage.[1] She may have been born in Sh
2 CONC ropshire, where her father held lands in Sutton, Betton Grange and Colneham.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes married first to Richard Watts, son of Sir John Watts, Mayor of London, and Margaret Hawes.[1][2] A parish record gives the following: "Richard Watts and Annis Mackworth" were married by licence dated 9 May 1926 at St Dunsta
2 CONC n in the West, London.[4] They had one daughter and three sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Dorothy,[5] baptized at Waltham Abbey, Essex, 29 January 1628/9, buried 9 May 1660, married first to Edward Cludd, with whom she had children; she married second to Richard Bagott (no issue)[1]
2 CONT # Richard, eldest son,[5] baptized at Epping, Essex 3 June 1630,[6] buried 14 December 1666, married Katherine Werden and had issue[1]
2 CONT # George,[5] baptized at Epping 1 October 1631,[6] apprenticed to Thomas Jevon of the Fishmongers; Company, London, 28 January 1647/8[1]
2 CONT # Humphrey,[2] baptized at Epping 28 November 1632,[6] no further information[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes' husband, Richard Watts, was buried 15 May 1635 at St. Bartholomew the Less, London.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes married second Col. William Crowne, Esq.,[2][7] on 16 November 1637 at St. Savior, Southwark, Surrey.[1][8] They had three sons and two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT # John,[2][7] baptized at St Martin in the Fields, London, 6 April 1641, buried 27 April 1712 in London, never married; he was in America from 1657-1660 attending Harvard College and returne to London as a leading playwright[1]
2 CONT # Agnes, buried at St. Dunstan, Stepney, Middlesex, 6 January 1642/3[1]
2 CONT # William,[2] baptized at St Giles in the Fields, London, 11 March 1643/4, burial not found, not named in his father's will[1]
2 CONT # Henry,[2] was living at New Castle, New Hampshire, 1678/9.[2] Henry was born about 1647/8 in England,[7] died a resident of Portsmouth, New Hampshire, before 24 June 1698 (administration bond); married Alice Rogers and had issue
2 CONC [1]
2 CONT # Agnes,[2] again, was named in her father's 1682 will[1][7]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Crowne is said to have immigrated to Nova Scotia in 1657, where he built a house. He removed to Boston, Massachusetts by 1659 and was made freeman in 1660. He briefly returned to England in 1661 and, on his return to New E
2 CONC ngland in 1662, he was granted 500 acres in Mendon, Massachusetts. He lived in Mendon for many years, but likely spent the last years of his life in Boston, where he was living 15 August 1679.[2] No proof has been found about whet
2 CONC her Agnes immigrated to America with her husband. However, a 2015 blog asserts that "Agnes never set foot in the New World. Though William Crowne was ordered by the General Court of Massachusetts in 1674 to return to his wife in E
2 CONC ngland or pay a £20 fine,[9] he must have chosen the fine. This is the last time Agnes appears in records, and her date and place of death has not been uncovered."[4][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes' date of death is unknown, but it can be narrowed down to before 24 December 1682 (date of her husband's will),[2] and after 1673 when she was living in England.[1] William died as a resident of Boston, before 24 February 16
2 CONC 82/3, when one of his legatees accepted a bequest paid by Henry Crowne.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/crowne_william_1E.html
2 CONT
2 CONT CROWNE (Crown), WILLIAM, colonizer, independent minister in the Parliament of Cromwell, colonel in the British militia, Rouge-Dragon; b. 1617 (nothing is known of his place of birth or of his education); d. 1682 in Boston, North A
2 CONC merica.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT William Crowne married, between the years 1635–40, Agnes (Mackworth) Watts, widow of Richard Watts and daughter-in-law of John Watts, an alderman and lord mayor of London, 1606. Mrs. Crowne was the sister of Humphrey Mackworth of 
2 CONC Breton Grange, county of Shrewsbury. Three children were born to Agnes and William Crowne; the eldest, John, became a well-known English dramatist.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes Crowne did not accompany her husband to America. That she was alive in 1674 is known because at that date Crowne was ordered to return to her in England. John Crowne also stayed in England and was forced by the loss of his p
2 CONC atrimony to earn his living as a playwright.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://royaldescent.blogspot.com/2015/11/edward-iii-descent-for-katherine-nee.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes Mackworth, the wife of Richard Watts of Garnons in Great Munden (d. 1635), appears in most of the genealogy works specializing in royal descents of New World immigrants, including Plantagenet Ancestry, in all of its editions
2 CONC . This is because her second husband, William Crowne (d. 1683), a herald (Rouge Dragon) at the College of Arms, and officer for the Parliamentarian side in the English Civil War, immigrated to Massachusetts in 1657. Though he retu
2 CONC rned to England after the Restoration in 1660, he only stayed a couple years, going back to Massachusetts in 1662 and remaining in New England until his death in Boston in 1682/3 (will dated 24 December 1682, proved 28 February 16
2 CONC 83). Agnes never set foot in the New World. Though William Crowne was ordered by the General Court of Massachusetts in 1674 to return to his wife in England or pay a £20 fine, he must have chosen the fine. This is the last Agnes (
2 CONC Mackworth) (Watts) Crowne appears in record, and her date and place of death has not been uncovered. One of her three sons by her second husband was the playwright John Crowne (1641-1712), who has a ODNB bio. In the 2011 Edition o
2 CONC f Plantagenet Ancestry, Agnes is on pp. 488-489, where it's stated that she "married (1st) before 1632 RICHARD WATTS, of Epping, Essex, younger son of John Watts, Knt., Clothworker, Mayor of London, 1606-7, by Margaret, daughter o
2 CONC f James Hawes, Knt. They had one son, Humphrey." This isn't complete.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her paternal grandmother Agnes Mackworth, Katherine (née Watts), Countess of Dunmore, has a 14-generation descent from Edward III,
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * https://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p2475.htm#i74350
