1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Wikipedia, March 2015:
2 CONT
2 CONT Brilliana, Lady Harley (1598 – 29 October 1643), née Brilliana Conway, was a celebrated English letter-writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [show]  1 Marriage2 Letters3 English Civil War4 Death5 Bibliography6 Notes7 References8 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage[edit]Conway was born at Brill, near Rotterdam in the Netherlands, while her father Sir Edward Conway (later Viscount Conway) was Governor there. She married (as his fourth wife), Sir Robert Harley in 1623,[1] who served a
2 CONC s her father's aide in the Parliament of England, while her father was Secretary of State of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Letters[edit]Some of Lady Harley's 375 letters to her husband and her son Sir Edward Harley survive and show her to be an educated literary woman, at home in several languages. She was able to keep her husband informed of local po
2 CONC litical affairs when he was absent from home at Brampton Bryan in northwest Herefordshire, attending Parliament or for other reasons, and organised the collection on information locally for the Parliamentary Committee on Scandalou
2 CONC s Ministers. She was deeply religious, and her letters frequently repeat religious sentiments and encouraged her family in their chosen Puritan practices. The letters also contain passages relating to personal details of their fam
2 CONC ily life.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT English Civil War[edit]During the English Civil War, in the absence of her husband and sons, Lady Harley defended her home, Brampton Bryan Castle, during a three-month siege by Royalist troops until the troops withdrew because the
2 CONC y were needed at Gloucester. [1] She then compelled her tenants to level the Royalist siege earthworks. She also dispatched 40 troops to raid a local Royalist camp at Knighton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death[edit]Lady Harley died of a cold on 29 October 1643, after bravely defending her castle [2]This was probably as a result of the hardships endured during the siege.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography[edit]Lady Brilliana Harley's published correspondence can be found in three volumes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Harley, Lady Brilliana (1854), Lewis, Thomas Taylor, ed., Letters of the Lady Brilliana Harley, Wife of Sir Robert Harley, of Brampton Bryan, Knight of the Bath, Camden Society  Historical Manuscripts Commission (1904), Calendar o
2 CONC f the manuscripts of the Marquis of Bath, Preserved at Longleat, Wiltshire 1, His Majesty's Stationery Office, p. 8–21  Three letters, two dated August 24 and one August 25, which passed between Lady Harley and Sir J. Scudamore ar
2 CONC e printed from copies at Welbeck on pp. 114, 115 of the first volume of the report on the Harley papers belonging to the Duke of Portland (Fourteenth Report, Appendix. Part II). The British Library holds many unpublished letters f
2 CONC rom Lady Harley.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her letters and portrait 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Letters of Brilliana Conway, Lady Harley
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.jimandellen.org/harley/show.letters.html
