1 OCCU Justice of the Peace; 1660: MP for Newton; 1675-1676: MP for Liverpool; Deputy Lieutenant for Lancashire; Vice-Admiral of the Coast for Lancashire
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ---- [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Banks_(died_1676%29 Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT > Constituency    Dates<br/>
2 CONT > NEWTON          1660<br/>
2 CONT > LIVERPOOL      27 May 1675 - 6 July 1676
2 CONT
2 CONT >Family and Education<br/>
2 CONT >b. c.1636, 3rd but o. surv. s. of '''William Banks''' of Winstanley, being o.s. by 2nd w. Sarah, da. of [https://www.geni.com/people/Walter-Jones/6000000045727546948 Walter Jones] of Chastleton, Oxon. m. 23 Oct. 1656, Frances, da
2 CONC . of [https://www.geni.com/people/Peter-Legh/6000000045837384999 Peter Legh] of Bruch Hall, Lancs., 4s. (1 d.v.p.) 3da. suc. fa. 1666.1
2 CONT
2 CONT >Offices Held<br/>
2 CONT >Dep. lt. Lancs. c. Aug. 1660-2, ?1663-d., commr. for assessment Aug. 1660-74, corporations 1662-3, jt. farmer of excise 1665-74, j.p. 1665-6, 1670-d.; freeman, Liverpool by 1670; v.-adm. Lancs. 1673-d.2
2 CONT
2 CONT > ...
2 CONT
2 CONT > Banks acquired an independent income of £500 at his marriage, and in 1660 succeeded his brother-in-law, [https://www.geni.com/people/Piers-Legh/6000000021943107269 Piers Legh], who had been involved in Booth’s rising, as Member 
2 CONC for Newton. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1660-1690/member/banks-william-i-1636-76 The History of Parliament]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT > Although their chief residence was in Cheshire, the Leghs of Lyme enjoyed the principal property interest in Newton throughout the period, and in 1661 Richard Legh acquired the borough and manor from Sir Thomas Fleetwood. Thus n
2 CONC ot only were most of the burgages held by him, but the steward, who acted as returning officer, was chosen in his court leet.1
2 CONT
2 CONT > Legh’s royalist and Anglican sentiments are unquestionable, but he had sat for Cheshire under the Protectorate, and was therefore eligible at the general election of 1660. His colleague '''William Banks''' had married into anoth
2 CONC er branch of the Legh family, and presumably sat on the same interest.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1660-1690/constituencies/newton Constituency: Newton] in ''The History of Parliament: the House of Commons 1660-1690'' (1983)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
