1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT >Constituency    Dates<br/>
2 CONT > NEWTON         1695 - 1698
2 CONT
2 CONT > Family and Education<br/>
2 CONT > bap. 30 Aug 1666, 4th s. of William Banks I† of Winstanley, nr. Wigan, Lancs. by Frances, da. and event. h. of [https://www.geni.com/people/Peter-Legh/6000000045837384999 Peter Legh] of Burch Hall, Lancs.; bro. of William Banks 
2 CONC II†.  educ. G. Inn 1685.  unm.1
2 CONT
2 CONT > Offices Held<br/>
2 CONT > Burgess, Wigan 1695.2
2 CONT
2 CONT > Biography<br/>
2 CONT > Banks, whose father had sat for Newton and Liverpool, was a cousin and ‘particular friend’ of [https://www.geni.com/people/Piers-Legh/600000004595737287 Peter Legh]† of Lyme, the non-juring patron of Newton. When Legh was accuse
2 CONC d of high treason in the Lancashire Plot (1693–4) Banks was one of the few persons allowed to visit him in the Tower, and, with the assistance of the Irish informer Taaffe, Banks arranged a meeting with John Lunt, the chief witnes
2 CONC s against the accused. He persuaded Lunt that he wished to aid him in the conspiracy to take a share of the forfeited lands of the accused plotters, and Lunt revealed to Banks that his allegations were untrue, and that he had forg
2 CONC ed supposed Jacobite commissions to the alleged plotters. Banks testified against Lunt at the Manchester trials in October 1694, his testimony being vital to the acquittal of the six Catholic gentlemen tried there and to the relea
2 CONC se of Legh when his case was heard at Chester the same month. On 23 Nov. 1694 Banks repeated his account of Lunt’s fabrications to the Commons’ investigation into the Lancashire Plot. Banks’s key role in Legh’s acquittal prompted 
2 CONC Legh to return Banks for Newton in 1695. ... Banks did not stand at the 1698 election, following which he was listed as a member of the Country party ‘out’ of the new Parliament. His life came to a premature and unfortunate end in
2 CONC  late October 1703 when he ‘unhappily drowned crossing the river [Dee] near Chester’.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1690-1715/member/banks-legh-1666-1703 ''The History of Parliament: the House of Commons 1690-1715''] (2002) by Eveline Cruickshanks & Richard Harrison
2 CONT
