1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (5) Mary Assheton (1636-80), born 7 December and baptised 15 December 1636; married Christopher Banastre (c.1635-90) of Bank Hall, Bretherton (Lancs) and had issue two daughters; buried at Croston (Lancs), 20 Octob
2 CONC er 1680;
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Townships: Bretherton', in A History of the County of Lancaster: Volume 6, ed. William Farrer and J Brownbill (London, 1911), pp. 102-108. British History Online http://www.british-history.ac.uk/vch/lancs/vol6/pp102-108 [accessed
2 CONC  15 July 2019].
2 CONT
2 CONT >Henry Banastre died in London 13 June 1641, and was brought to Croston for burial (fn. 35); his eldest son Henry, aged twenty-eight when the pedigree was recorded in 1664, (fn. 36) was killed in Cheshire by a Manxman named Colcot
2 CONC h, (fn. 37) and Bank passed to his brother Christopher, high sheriff in 1669–70. (fn. 38) Christopher, who died in 1690, left two daughters and co-heirs; the elder, Anne, married Thomas Fleetwood, (fn. 39) the first to attempt the
2 CONC  draining of Martin Mere, and their daughter and heir, Henrietta Maria, (fn. 40) carried Bank and the moiety of Bretherton to the Leghs of Lyme, from whom it has descended to Lord Lilford, (fn. 41) who, as stated above, is now lor
2 CONC d of the manor of Bretherton. Courts are held annually.
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT *http://landedfamilies.blogspot.com/2016/05/216-assheton-of-downham-hall-whalley_12.html?m=1
2 CONT * The History of the County Palatine and Duchy of Lancaster, Volume 4 Edward Baines J. Heywood, 1891 - . “Banastre of Banke.”  Page 133. [https://books.google.com/books?id=G904AQAAMAAJ&lpg=PA133&ots=CA7gyvl_hF&dq=%22Banastre%20of%
2 CONC 20Banke%22&pg=PA133#v=onepage&q&f=true GoogleBooks] 
2 CONT * [https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bank_Hall “Bank Hall”] is a Jacobean mansion in Bretherton, Lancashire, England. It is a Grade II* listed building and is at the centre of a private estate, surrounded by parkland. The hall was b
2 CONC uilt on the site of an older house in 1608 by the Banastres who were lords of the manor.  ... The last of the Banastres, Christopher who was High Sheriff of Lancashire in 1670, died in 1690 leaving two daughters. The property was 
2 CONC inherited by the eldest, Anne who married Thomas Fleetwood.
