2 CONT *'''Memorials of Marshfield, and guide book to its localities at Green Harbor (1854)
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/memorialsofmarsh00thoma
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/memorialsofmarsh00thoma#page/46/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.46
2 CONT **		ROUSE. 
2 CONT *'''   John Rouse settled early at Careswell Creek ; his wife was Annis Pabodie, dau. of John and Isabel Pabodie, original settlers at Duxbury. Mrs. Rouse had a brother Francis, who settled at Topsfield ; m. Mary, dau. of Reginald
2 CONC  Foster, ancestor of all the Peabody's in the country; and also John, of Duxbury, who m. Elizabeth, dau. of the primitive John Alden, who is ancestor of all the Pabodies. 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/memorialsofmarsh00thoma#page/47/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.47
2 CONT *'''   The children of John and Annis Rouse were. Anna, who m. Isaac Holmes, 1678 ; Mary, b. 1640, m. a Price; John, b. 1643; Simon, b. 1645; George, b. 1648; and Elizabeth, who m. Thomas Bourn, 1681. John Rouse, sen., d. at Cares
2 CONC well Creek, 1684. John Rouse, jr., m. Mary Rogers, 1656, deceased, 1711. George and Simon were among the purchasers of Little Compton, 1674 ; and probably removed thither. The Careswell Creek estate came into the possession of the
2 CONC  dau., Elizabeth Bourn, or her children. Dea. Rouse Bourn was the last hereditary owner of said estate. He removed to Winthrop, Me., before 1820, where he was recently living at an advanced age. 
2 CONT *'''   The historian of Scituate calls John Rouse, sen., the unfortunate man who was imprisoned and publicly whipped for being a Quaker, on the accusation of Christopher Winter. We believe some of the family of John Rouse became F
2 CONC riends, but he was not the subject of this inhuman persecution. In Sewall's History of this injured people, he is called the son of a gentleman of Barbadoes. We are happy to think the government of Plymouth Colony did not carry it
2 CONC s severities to this people to the awful extent it reached in Massachusetts'''; and we are proud of Isaac Robinson, Timothy Hatherly and James Cudworth, associates of our own Josias Winslow, for refusing to sign the circular sent 
2 CONC them from the Massachusetts Colony recommending capital punishment for worshiping God in a different 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/memorialsofmarsh00thoma#page/48/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.48
2 CONT *form from their own ; — green as Green Harbor ever be their memories !  _____________________
2 CONT *'''Genealogical and family history of the state of New Hampshire : a record of the achievements of her people in the making of a commonwealth and the founding of a nation (1908) Vol. II
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/genealogicalfami02stea
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/genealogicalfami02stea#page/475/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.475
2 CONT **		PEABODY
2 CONT *   In the early New England records this name has various spellings, and the descendants of the original American ancestors are found under names varying considerably in orthography. Many now use the form, Pabodie.
2 CONT *   (I) The first of the family in America as far as can be ascertained was John Paybody, who appears to have emigrated to New England about the year 1635. His name does not appear in the list of passengers which includes that of 
2 CONC his son Francis, and it is probable that he came at another time, perhaps with his youngest son William. The names of these two are found in the list of the original proprietors of Plymouth. In 1637 John Paybody was the owner of t
2 CONC en acres of land at Bluefish. He was admitted a freeman January 2, 1638, and with his son. William, was among the original proprietors of <nowiki>-----</nowiki> in 1645. His will is dated 1649, and he died about 1666. His wife Isa
2 CONC bel survived him. Their children were : Thomas, Frances, William and '''Annis'''.  ______________________________
2 CONT *'''PEABODY, Annis 
