1 NOTE {geni:about_me} By his wife Anne, who was buried by her husband 18 May 1630, [Samuel] Foxe had three sons, Thomas, John, and Robert. Thomas Foxe, M.D. (1591–1652), born at Havering Palace 14 February 1591; matriculated from Magdal
2 CONC en Hall, Oxford, 19 June 1607; was demy of Magdalen College 1608–13, and fellow 1613–30,[3] proceeding B.A. 1611 and M.A. 1614. He was bursar of his college in 1622, and junior proctor of the university 1620–1. He afterwards studi
2 CONC ed medicine, proceeding M.D. at Oxford, and was a candidate of the London College of Physicians 25 June 1623. A letter describing Ben Jonson's reception at Oxford, written by Thomas Foxe to his father, is preserved in ‘MS. Harl.’ 
2 CONC 416, f. 226, and has been printed by Mr. Winters.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 8 May 1634 James Hay, Earl of Carlisle, applied to him for a loan of 500l. He seems to have acquired much property, and to have been friendly with men eminent in literature and society. He died at Warlies 20 November 1662, and 
2 CONC was buried in Waltham Abbey 26 November. He married Anne, daughter of Richard Honeywood of Charing, Kent, and Marleshall, Essex, and granddaughter of Mrs. Mary Honeywood, the pious friend of his grandfather, the martyrologist. By 
2 CONC her he left a daughter Alice, who married Sir Richard Willys, baronet, Robert, Samuel's youngest son, was a captain in the navy, and died in 1646. He wrote to his elder brother an interesting letter descriptive of the trial of the
2 CONC  Earl and Countess of Somerset.
2 CONT
2 CONT from the bio of Samuel Foxe, Wikipedia:   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Foxe
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Foxe was a beneficiary and executor of his father's will:
2 CONT
2 CONT Foxe outlived Heneage by more than 30 years, settling at Warlies, near Waltham Abbey, where he died. He was buried at Waltham 16 Jan. 1630. His will was made at Warlies 29 June 1622, making bequests to his wife and sons Thomas and
2 CONC  Robert, and to the poor of Waltham and Shipton. Executors were his wife and son Thomas, and the overseer was his brother Simeon Foxe, ‘Dr. and Professor of Physic’.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/foxe-samuel-1560-1630
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT TRANSACTIONS OF THE ROYAL HISTORICAL SOCIETY. 
2 CONT
2 CONT DR. SAMUEL FOXE. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Samuel Foxe, the eldest son of the martyrologist, bore a very conspicuous part in the parish of Waltham Abbey (or Holy Cross), where he resided during the greater portion of his life. He was born at Norwich on the last night o
2 CONC f the year 1560; and at the age of twenty-nine he married Anne Leveson, of Eastwell, Kent. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1587, the same year that his father died, he was admitted into the service of Sir Thomas Heneage, of Copt  Hall,* after .which he went abroad. Sir Thomas gave the Palace of Havering-atte- Bower into his custody, a place  renown
2 CONC ed in history as a royal hunting seat. This palace Sir Thomas received by grant from Queen Elizabeth ; and in this splendid mansion was born Thomas Foxe, the physician, February 14, 1591. 
2 CONT
2 CONT DR. THOMAS FOXE. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Thomas Foxe, the eldest son of Dr. Samuel Foxe, was born at Havering Palace, Feb. 14, 1591. In due course he was sent to Magdalen College, Oxford, where he was procurator in 1621. The following year he was chosen bursar, and o
2 CONC n June 25, 1623, was admitted a candidate of the college of Physicians.* He was again chosen bursar the next year. About this time he married Anne Honeywood, the daughter of Robert and Elizabeth Honeywood of Charing, in Kent, and 
2 CONC Markshall, Essex. She was granddaughter of Mrs. Mary (Waters) Honeywood....
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 20th of November 1662, Doct. Thomas Foxe died, and left his lease and executoriall right of the said parsonage of Shipton, with all its members and appertinances, to his only daughter and sole executrix Dame Alice Willys wi
2 CONC fe to Sir Richard Willys of Shipton in the county of Oxon, knt. and bart." * 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Thomas Foxe was buried in Waltham Abbey Church on the 26th day of the same month. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Harl. MSS. 419, fol. 171.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/stream/transactions05royauoft/transactions05royauoft_djvu.txt
