2 CONT *”The Family Bible of Charles Collier record” http://files.usgwarchives.net/nc/statewide/bibles/colier01.txt (caution: unsubstantiated data)
2 CONT * “Collier Family” in The William and Mary Quarterly! Vol. 9, No. 3 (Jan., 1901), pp. 183-185 Published by: Omohundro Institute of Early American History and Culture, DOI: 10.2307/1920292, Stable URL: http://www.jstor.org/stable/1
2 CONC 920292 (caution: “There were several Collier families in Virginia. So one must be sure they have the right one, assuming is never a fact.”)
2 CONT
2 CONT A Note on "Porto Bello"
2 CONT Capt. John Collier of ‘Porto Bello’. This is a historically significant estate in York County, Virginia. It was named after the Battle of Porto Bello (1739). Interestingly, George Washington’s half-brother, Lawrence, named Mount V
2 CONC ernon after Admiral Vernon, the commander at that same battle. The Colliers were neighbors and social peers of the Washington family!
2 CONT
2 CONT The Collier family of "Porto Bello" was part of the elite Virginia gentry, a social circle that interacted closely with the Washingtons, especially during the mid-18th century. 
2 CONT 1. The Legacy of "Porto Bello"
2 CONT The estate, located on the York River in York County, was named in honor of the Battle of Porto Bello (1739). 
2 CONT The Shared Connection: Your ancestor, Colonel Cornelius Collier, served in a Virginia Militia unit under Admiral Edward Vernon during the 1740–1742 expedition against the Spanish.
2 CONT The Mount Vernon Link: This is the exact same Admiral Vernon for whom George Washington's half-brother, Lawrence, named his estate, Mount Vernon. Both families held Admiral Vernon in such high regard that they named their primary 
2 CONC homes after his military victories and person. 
2 CONT 2. Social and Political Standing
2 CONT Collier ancestors were neighbors and social peers of the Washingtons in the Tidewater region. 
2 CONT Estate Ownership: Captain John Collier I (1685–1735) was a prominent planter and Vestryman of the Lower Parish, a role reserved for the local elite. In colonial Virginia, the parish vestry was a center of political and social powe
2 CONC r where families like the Colliers, Dandridges, and Washingtons coordinated community affairs.
2 CONT Military Ties: The family was deeply involved in the Virginia Colonial Militia. For example, Captain John Collier of Porto Bello served as a Captain in the Infantry of King and Queen County. 
2 CONT 3. Geographical Proximity
2 CONT The Porto Bello house, though likely rebuilt around 1800 on the site of your ancestor's original "hunting lodge," still stands today within the Camp Peary grounds in York County. It remains a recognized historical artifact of the 
2 CONC era when the Collier family helped shape the Virginia colony alongside the Washingtons. 
2 CONT Direct Family Overlap with the Washington family:
2 CONT the marriage between Elizabeth Collier and the Dandridge line (Martha Washington's family) formalizes the link between this Collier branch and the first President's household. Your ancestors weren't just distant observers; they we
2 CONC re part of the same "Virginia Dynasty" that provided the leadership for the American Revolution. 
