2 CONT Col. Timothy Crosby Ellis Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 14 Sep 1724 in Dedham, Suffolk, Massachusetts Bay
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Eleazer Ellis Jr DNA confirmed and Mary (Crosbey) Ellis
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Mehitable Thurston (Ellis) Griggs and Timothy Crosby Ellis [half]
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Elizabeth Ellis — married about 1746 (to 29 Apr 1810) in Needham, Suffolk, Massachusetts Bay [uncertain]
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Timothy Ellis Jr., Caleb Ellis, Benjamin Ellis, Joshua Lutus Ellis, Elizabeth Ellis, Julitia Ellis, Julitia (Ellis) Balch, Millet Ellis, Eleazer Ellis and Drusilla Ellis
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 13 Jul 1817 in Keene, Cheshire, New Hampshire, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT 18:11: Bernard Ellis edited the Biography for Timothy Crosby Ellis Sr. (1724-1817). [Thank Bernard for this]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT SAR insignia Timothy Ellis Sr. is an NSSAR Patriot Ancestor. NSSAR Ancestor #: P154213 Rank: Colonel Excerpted from: Biographical Sketches of Dover, MA Soldiers Printed by the Town 1909
2 CONT
2 CONT "TIMOTHY .ELLIS, (9) son of Eleazer, Jr., and Mary (Crosby) Ellis, was born on his father's farm, the Rev. Benjamin Caryl place, Sept. 14, 1724[1]. He was descended in the fourth generation from Richard Ellis, who was a settler in
2 CONC  Dedham in 1642.
2 CONT
2 CONT Timothy Ellis gave an extended service in the- last French and Indian War. He was a sergeant in Capt. Eliphalet Fales' Company in the Crown Point Expedition. He enlisted May 7, 1755, and served to Nov. 18, 1755. He was a lieutenan
2 CONC t in Capt. Simon Slocom's Company from Mar. 31, 1759, to April 2, 1760, and served at Fort Cumberland. He also served in Capt. Slocom's Company from June 1, 1760, to Sept. 26, 1760. His Revolutionary service is found in New Hampsh
2 CONC ire, where he was a Major in the Sixth Regiment of the Militia, in 1777. He marched with the volunteers to Ticonderoga in May, 1777 and was appointed Major of Nichols' Regiment under Stark, and commanded his battalion at Benningto
2 CONC n. Was muster master of the Continental troops from Cheshire County. He was made a colonel of the Sixth Regiment, February 24, 1783, Selectman of Keene, 1770-4, Delegate from Keene to the Provincial Congress at Exeter in April, 17
2 CONC 75, Representative to the Legislature 1776-7-8. Was one of the Committee appointed in 1778 to take possession of the confiscated estates of tories in Cheshire County. He lived on 'The Hills', four miles from the village of Keene, 
2 CONC on what was the Westmoreland road.
2 CONT
2 CONT His name, together with those of three of his sons, Caleb, Joshua and Timothy, Jr., all of whom were born in Dover, [actually Dedham from which Dover was later subdivided in 1784.[2]][3][4] appears on the bronze tablet erected to 
2 CONC the memory of Revolutionary soldiers in the Thayer Memorial Library in Keene."
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a wife Elizabeth Ellis who as daughter of William Ellis and Abigail MIllet, was his second cousin. [5]Children: Timothy, Caleb, Benjamin, Joshua, Elizabeth, Julitia, Millett, Eleazer, and Drusilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT He moved from Dedham with his family about 1763 and settled in Keene, New Hampshire. He died July 13, 1817,[6] and is buried in Hurricane Cemetery, Keene.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT SAR Patriot #: P-154213[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
