1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''General Israel Chapin, Sr.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Revolutionary War General''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A020763 DAR Ancestor #: A020763]
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Israel_Chapin#:~:text=Israel%20Chapin%20(4%20Dec%201740,the%20Five%20Nations%20of%20Indians.
2 CONT * https://fortticonderoga.catalogaccess.com/people/6738
2 CONT * https://archives.lib.rochester.edu/agents/people/1024
2 CONT * https://wardepartmentpapers.org/s/home/item/2764
2 CONT * https://founders.archives.gov/documents/Washington/05-17-02-0023-0003
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/11366232/israel-chapin
2 CONT
2 CONT General Chapin served as a Captain of the Hatfield Militia at Boston and Lexington. He fought at the Battles of Saratoga as a major, as Colonel of the Second Regiment of Hampshire County Militia and as a Brigadier General followin
2 CONC g the Invasion of Quebec
2 CONT
2 CONT At the direction of U.S. President George Washington, he was summoned the Canandaigua Council, which led to the signing, on November 11, 1794, of the Canandaigua Treaty, the final treaty between the six nations of the Haudenosaune
2 CONC e (Iroquois) and the United States Government. The general died a few months after the signing of the treaty at age 54, and Seneca chief Red Jacket gave a moving condolence speech at his graveside. 
2 CONT
2 CONT General Israel Chapin married Elizabeth Marsh around 1763. The couple had seven children, among them Captain Israel Chapin Jr. (1763–1833), who succeeded his father as the Superintendent of Indian Affairs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grafton (Mass.) Vital Records has his birth record as "(CHAPING (see Chapin) Israel, s. Benjamin and Anna, Dec. 4, 1740." [https://archive.org/details/vitalrecordsofgr00graf/page/32/mode/2up <archive.org>]
