1 TITL Maj.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === Major Oliver Lovell ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Major Oliver Lovell was an early pioneer, church leader, and civic official in Rockingham, Vermont. Born on May 1, 1739, in Worcester, Massachusetts, he relocated to Windham County, Vermont, where he became an active figure in loc
2 CONC al municipal and religious establishment. He died on July 17, 1804, in Rockingham, Vermont, and was buried in the local family cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Parentage and Lineage ===
2 CONT
2 CONT The parentage of Major Oliver Lovell is firmly documented through primary town records and compiled regional histories as a multi-generational lineage extending back to early Massachusetts colonists.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Father: Michael Lovell (born circa 1700 – died 1752). He was an early grantee and proprietor who operated the first sawmill in the town of Rockingham.
2 CONT * Mother: Mary (Morse) Lovell (1707 – after 1778).
2 CONT
2 CONT The raw family breakdown naming '''Michael Lovell; Michael Lovell and Mary (Morse) Walker''' stems from typical automated digital profile merges where structural nomenclature has been duplicated or misread. In historical reality, 
2 CONC Mary Morse married Michael Lovell in 1726; she is distinct from the neighboring Walker households of Worcester County, though intermarriages occurred in subsequent generations (such as their daughter Jemima Lovell marrying Abraham
2 CONC  Walker).
2 CONT
2 CONT The direct paternal line ascends through early New England settlers as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. '''Alexander Lovell''' — The immigrant ancestor of Medfield, Massachusetts.
2 CONT 2. '''Nathaniel Lovell''' — Son of Alexander.
2 CONT 3. '''Michael Lovell''' — Son of Nathaniel; married Mary Morse in 1726 and relocated the family to Worcester, Massachusetts, where Oliver was born, before engaging as a primary proprietor in Vermont.
2 CONT 4. '''Major Oliver Lovell''' — Subject, born 1739.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Immediate Family Summary ===
2 CONT
2 CONT * Spouse: Hannah (Smith) Lovell (1744 – March 13, 1799). They married circa 1770 in Rockingham, Vermont. Following her death, Oliver married a second time to Irena.
2 CONT * Siblings: Includes Michael Lovell Jr. and Elijah Lovell (born April 20, 1749).
2 CONT * Children: Mary "Polly" (Lovell) Blanchard, Oliver Lovell, Frink Lovell, Lucy (Lovell) Phelps, Sabra (Lovell) Nourse, Hannah (Lovell) Royce, Sally (Lovell) Fletcher, and Beliza Lovell.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Revolutionary War and Factional Politics ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Major Oliver Lovell's record during the Revolutionary War is defined more by local regional factionalism and military administrative roles than by battlefield engagements.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Militia Commission:''' On October 17, 1775, Oliver Lovell was chosen as '''Second Major''' of the Lower Regiment of the Cumberland County Militia under the revolutionary New York jurisdiction. He was subsequently promoted to 
2 CONC '''First Major''' in 1776.
2 CONT * '''The New York vs. Vermont Divide:''' Because his military commission was tied to the New York colonial governance rather than the newly emerging "Green Mountain" Vermont faction, his position became politically precarious. The
2 CONC  eastern side of Vermont (Cumberland County) was deeply split between those loyal to New York’s authority, those pushing for Vermont's independent statehood, and quiet loyalists who preferred the status quo under the King.
2 CONT * '''Historical Controversy and Local Pushback:''' Local histories and town documents indicate that Major Lovell did not actively embrace the radical patriot or "Liberty" cause as early or as fervently as the majority of Rockingha
2 CONC m's residents. Lyman Simpson Hayes notes in the ''History of the Town of Rockingham'' that '''Evidently Major Lovell did not espouse the Liberty cause and renounce his loyalty to the King as early in the struggle as the larger par
2 CONC t of the citizens of R. did...'''
2 CONT * '''The 1781 Remonstrance:''' This hesitation caused significant local friction. In April 1781, a heavily signed petition from the citizens of Rockingham was formally presented to Vermont Governor Thomas Chittenden and his counci
2 CONC l. The residents actively protested and remonstrated against Major Lovell being commissioned as a justice of the peace, citing his late or questionable allegiance to the revolutionary state government.
2 CONT * '''Resolution:''' Despite the public outcry and local suspicion, his political status was eventually cleared. Governor Chittenden duly commissioned him, and he continued to serve the county in a high-profile civic capacity after
2 CONC  the war. Consequently, he is not named in the standard combat rolls for offensive military actions (such as the Battles of Bennington or Hubbardton); his military title '''Major''' reflects his early administrative militia appoin
2 CONC tment in the initial layout of Cumberland County's defenses.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Public and Civic Service ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Oliver Lovell assumed significant responsibilities in the early infrastructure of Windham County under both New York and early Vermont jurisdictions:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Judicial Appointment: Commissioned as a Justice of the Peace on April 14, 1772, under Cumberland County jurisdiction.
2 CONT * Civic Offices: Served regularly as a Selectman and Field Driver following the early political structuring of Rockingham.
2 CONT * Ecclesiastical Leadership: Active in the settlement of the town's initial meeting house and early congregations. On January 5, 1781, he was chosen as a trustee to oversee structural funding for the Rockingham Meeting House. On A
2 CONC ugust 21, 1785, he was appointed as a churchwarden representing the Church of England for the associated regional districts of Rockingham, Westminster, Chester, and Grafton.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Sources ===
2 CONT
2 CONT * Hayes, Lyman Simpson. ''History of the Town of Rockingham, Vermont, Including the Villages of Bellows Falls, Saxtons River, Rockingham, Cambridgeport and Bartonsville, 1753-1907, with Family Genealogies.'' Bellows Falls, VT: Tow
2 CONC n of Rockingham, 1907. [https://archive.org/details/cu31924028838336]
2 CONT * Peck, Thomas Bellows. ''Vital Records of Rockingham, Vermont: From the Beginning of the Town Records to January 1, 1845.'' Boston: New England Historic Genealogical Society, 1908.
2 CONT * Worcester (Mass.). ''Systematic History Fund: Vital Records of Worcester, Massachusetts, to the End of the Year 1849.'' Worcester, MA: Franklin P. Rice, 1904.
2 CONT * WikiTree Family Tree. "Maj. Oliver Lovell (1739–1804)." [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Lovell-2448]
