1 NAME John /Baxter/ Jr.
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Baxter
2 NSFX Jr.
2 _MARNM Baxter
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 8 OCT 1746
1 DEAT
2 DATE 5 MAR 1832
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Baxter-9235
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT John was born in 1746.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served in the Continental Army [1] during the Revolutionary War.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to his obituary in the Christian Register [2], he
2 CONT
2 CONT "was elected in early life by his native town a Representative in the Legislature, and was continued on that office during the war of the Revolution, except when absent on military duty as an officer in the army. He was a member o
2 CONC f the Convention of this State called to ratify the Federal Constitution, and voted with the majority, and lived long to enjoy the blessings of that compact. During his life he was a Representative between twenty and thirty years.
2 CONC  From youth to old age, he was called upon to sustain the most important municipal trusts, and perhaps no one can be named, in his sphere of action, who has discharged so many public duties, and made so few enemies. He was an able
2 CONC  and impartial magistrate, but his reputation as a peacemaker is the most valuable to his friends. His successful efforts in reconciling contending parties as arbitrator, will long be remembered. Possessing a strong and discrimina
2 CONC ting judgment, and a philanthropic and Christian spirit, he was peculiarly qualified for the office of mediator, and his success was equal to his reputation. He exemplified, through a long life, the spirit of the Master whom he se
2 CONC rved, and for the last fourteen years he was a professor of that faith which had been the rule of his life and conversation. His views of Christianity were decidedly liberal, and in the triumphs of such a faith he expired."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1789 he signed a loyalty oath to the Commonwealth of Massachusetts [3] that reads
2 CONT
2 CONT "Medfield March 2 : 1789 "I do truly and sincerely acknowledge profess testify and declare that the Commonwealth of Massachusetts is and of right ought to be a free sovereign and and(sic) independent state and I do sware that I wi
2 CONC ll bear true faith and allegiance to the said commonwealth and that I will defend the same against traitors conspiracies and all hostile attempts whatsoever: and that I do renounce and abjure all allegiance subjection and obedienc
2 CONC e to the King Queen or Government of Great Britain (as the Case may be) and every other foreign power whatsoever: and that no foreign prince person prelate State or potentate hath or ought to have any jurisdiction superiority pre-
2 CONC eminence authority Dispensing or other power in any matter civil ecclesiastical or spiritual within this Commonwealth except the authority and power which is or may be Vested by the Constituents in the Congress of the United State
2 CONC s: and I do further testify and Declare that no man or body of men hath or have any right to absolve or Discharge me from the obligation of this oath Declaration or affirmation and that I do make this acknowledgement profession te
2 CONC stimony Declaration denials renunciation and abjuration heartily and truly according to the common meaning and acceptation of the foregoing words without any equivocation mental evasion or secret reservation what soever.
2 CONT
2 CONT "So Help me God
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed
2 CONT
2 CONT Select Men Nathan Adams, Eleazar Ellis, Jonathan Smith, Silas Mason, Gershom Adams
2 CONT
2 CONT Town Clerk John Baxter
2 CONT
2 CONT Town Treasurer John Baxter
2 CONT
2 CONT Assessors John Baxter, Nahum Wight, Jonathan Metcalfe
2 CONT
2 CONT Constables Dyer Lovell, Obed Fisher"
2 CONT
2 CONT While acting as a magistrate in Medfield, John maintained a journal of cases. Many names associated with the town of Medfield in the years 1812 - 1822 can be found there.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1814, the Dedham Gazette ran a piece listing John Baxter as a member of the Detecting Society[4], an organization formed "for the purpose of recovering Horses that may be stolen from any of the members, and detecting the thieve
2 CONC s."
2 CONT
2 CONT He passed away in 1832.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Baxter (1746 - 1832)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Squire John Baxter===
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 8 Oct 1746 in Medfield, Suffolk, Massachusetts Bay
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of John Baxter and Rebekah (Fisher) Baxter
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Joseph Baxter, Nathan Baxter, Mary (Baxter) Mason, Richard Baxter and Moses Baxter
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Silence (Wight) Baxter — married 25 Nov 1784 in Medway, Norfolk, Massachusetts, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Sarah (Baxter) Bosworth
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 5 Mar 1832 at age 85 in Medfield, Norfolk, Massachusetts, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile last modified 18 Nov 2021 | Created 17 Aug 2021
2 CONT
2 CONT
