1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Recorded as Written:
2 CONT
2 CONT  ASA FAIRBANKS, of Dublin N.H. ( Ebenezer V, Eleasur IV, Eliesur III, George II, Jonathan I )Born in Sherborn, Mass.( now Medway) Mar. 4, 1762, removed to Dublin in 1784 and settled on Lot 3, Range 2, which was purchased by his fa
2 CONC ther. He died there July 26, 1819, aged 57 yrs., 4 mos., 22 ds. He enlisted in the service of his country at the age of fourteen and performed guard duty around military stores in Sherborn during one summer season. At the age of s
2 CONC ixteen he enlisted again and joined the army in Rhode Island. He was there employed a portion of the time as a ferryman between Tiverton and Greenwich, transporting soldiers, horses, provisions, ammunition etc. On one occasion he 
2 CONC was ordered to take a load of soldiers across the river by night in company with six other boats. During the passage a severe tempest arose. It was stormy and dark. They lost their pilot and lights and in this condition they were 
2 CONC tossed about by the waves, not knowing where they were or whither they were going. In the course of the night they were drifted upon an island where they found another boat load in the same condition. Only one boat of the seven fo
2 CONC und its way that night across the river. When morning came they discovered they were near the enemy. As boats crossed and recrossed the river. they made signals of distress but were not relieved till they had passed two days and t
2 CONC wo nights without food or the means of making a fire to dry themselves. They were supposed by their officers to be lost. In the year 1780 he re- enlisted for six months and served a part of the time at West Point. He was there whe
2 CONC n Arnold attempted to give it into the hands of the British.( See War Rolls) He married Apr. 28, 1784, Hepzibah Adams, daughter of Capt. Moses Adams. She was born in Sherborn, Dec. 8, 1761. Soon after their marriage they settled i
2 CONC n Dublin and lived at first in a log cabin without chimney or doors. They cooked their food for two or three months by the side of a log in the open air.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source''': Genealogy of the Fairbanks Family in America, 1633-1897: Lorenzo Sayles Fairbanks, A. M.: American Printing and Engraving Company: Bosyon, Mass.: 1897
