0 @N6000000001498708298@ NOTE
1 CONT Dvora, age 36, and three boys: Schmuel, age 15, Schlome, age 7, and Chaim Jossel, age 2 came to N.Y. via the S.S. Rotterdam from Rotterdam. They left from Europe on 24 November 1898 and arrived on 5 December 1898. They were going 
1 CONC to her husband, A. Perlmutter at 66 Humbold St., N.Y.
0 @N6000000001498704944@ NOTE
1 CONT Went by the name "Harry."
0 @N6000000001498726937@ NOTE
1 CONT Although he is listed on the passenger manifest, this boy seems to drop out of sight. He is not listed with the family on any record after his arrival.
0 @N6000000001498708593@ NOTE
1 CONT In 1910 at the age of 16, he was a capmaker in a factory. Was often unemployed. Had a squeaky, defective voice. Wife supposedly left him with the children.
0 @N6000000001498708609@ NOTE
1 CONT Served in World War I. Did not have children. His World War II “Old Men’s Draft” has a birthdate of 18 March 1895. He and Margaret were living and working at 69 Boulevard in Bayonne.
0 @N6000000001498726886@ NOTE
1 CONT Died of meningitis.
0 @N6000000001498708623@ NOTE
1 CONT Married after 1930.
0 @N6000000001498704876@ NOTE
1 CONT Was a tailor and philanthropist; may be from Poritsk, Ukraine. Shaindel used to call Sol, named in Yiddish, Shmerel Leib, “zeyde,” which means grandfather. It’s possible that either his father or Shifra’s was named Shmerel Leib.
0 @N6000000001498704343@ NOTE
1 CONT Still alive in 1936.
0 @N6000000001498726858@ NOTE
1 CONT Was not Jewish.
0 @N6000000001498704071@ NOTE
1 CONT Arrived in N.Y. on the S.S. Zeeland from Antwerp 2 December 1913 with her son Sidney, age 4.
1 CONT Was a talented needleworker: crocheted, did embroidery, crewel, etc. Worked as a milliner to support the children when Harry was away.
1 CONT Social Security # 107-20-8974
0 @N6000000001498708556@ NOTE
1 CONT Social Security Death Index says his birthday is 10 Dec 1907. Connecticut Death Cert. File #21605. Index gives birthdate as 21 Dec 1907.
0 @N6000000001498708577@ NOTE
1 CONT CT Death Index #26951
0 @N6000000001498726538@ NOTE
1 CONT Son Meyer called him “Hyman” on his marriage license. Meyer’s tombstone says Mordechai.
1 CONT May have owned or rented a farm on which they raised sheep and sold the wool.
0 @N6000000001498726573@ NOTE
1 CONT Family members must have pronounced the surname “Gervant,” since that was the spelling I originally got. May have left Russia in 1903 to avoid Russian/Japanese war. Went to Odessa, apprenticed himself to a tailor, and then went to
1 CONC  London. Although the family story is that he worked for two years in London before he came to New York, his passenger manifest says that he was only there for 6 weeks. Arrived in Philadelphia on 20 Aug 1905 on the S.S. Haverford 
1 CONC having sailed from Liverpool on 9 Aug 1905. Manifest says he was Meyer Gewantman.
1 CONT
1 CONT Birthdate is approximate. On his Declaration of Intention, it’s listed as 15 June 1878. His Petition for Naturalization says 8 Jun 1878. On World War II “Old Man’s Draft” it is 15 Jan 1878; birthplace is verified as Poritsk.  On L
1 CONC ena’s Petition, she says they married on 15 June 1905 and he was born on 1 May 1873.
1 CONT
1 CONT Died in a horrible auto accident. He was returning to N.Y. in the early morning and was a passenger in a small car hit head-on by a bakery delivery van. He and another man were thrown from the car and crushed by the truck. At the 
1 CONC time of his death, he was living at 1725 Fulton Ave. in the Bronx. A page of gruesome photos appears on page 20 of the same issue of the Daily News.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meyer is buried in the Independent Mishnitzer Young Men’s Association section at Mt. Zion. Myszyniec is the shtetl in which Lena was born.
0 @N6000000001498726491@ NOTE
1 CONT Surname may have been “Becker,” according to his granddaughter, Ruth Rose.
0 @N6000000001498726522@ NOTE
1 CONT Also called himself “Herman.” He returned to Poritsk in 1936 to visit his family, sailing on the S.S. Aquitania from Southampton, England, on 9 September and coming home to N.Y. on 15 September 1936.
1 CONT According to daughter, Ruth Rose Weisberg, the original family name was “Becker.”
0 @N6000000001498704052@ NOTE
1 CONT Arrived in Baltimore, MD 19 December 1909 on the "Breslau" from Bremen, Germany. Family lore said he traveled here twice but was sent back for lack of funds. This was substantiated in 2001 with the discovery of his first passenger
1 CONC  ship record. He, in fact, came on the S.S. Bremen from Bremen arriving in N.Y. on 30 June 1909. He was labeled "LPC" (Likely to be a Public Charge) and deported after 12 days at Ellis Island when he lost his appeal.  Their marria
1 CONC ge was filled with separations and was not a happy one.
1 CONT
1 CONT His Social Security # 132-09-5912
1 CONT
1 CONT Birthdate is uncertain: Petition for Naturalization says 15 Feb 1885; Social Sec. Application says 15 Mar 1883; Death Certificate says 10 Mar 1884. World War I Draft Registration gives the 15 Feb 1885 date.
1 CONT Spent a lot of time at the painters’ union building at Pitkin and Stone (now Mother Gaston Blvd.)
1 CONT
1 CONT 1910 census says “Harry Confor,” and shows him as a packer in a clothing store, but this is the right person; address and head of household match information on sister Chulya’s passenger ship manifest.
0 @N6000000001498699983@ NOTE
1 CONT His aunt, Shaindel Plitt Ellick, used to call him “Zeyde.” It’s possible he was named after the father of either Yehezkiel or Shifra Plitt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Served in the Army Air Corps from 1942–45 as a staff sergeant, stationed in the Aleutian Islands, 39th ADG. Maintained and repaired machine guns on aircraft (airplane armorer). Received training at Buckley Field, Colorado. Honorab
1 CONC ly discharged from Kelly Field, Texas, on 24 Oct 1945.
1 CONT
1 CONT Owned a trucking company called Power Packing & Parcel in the Williamsburg section of Brooklyn, N.Y. from 1946–1980.
1 CONT
1 CONT Retired to Daytona Beach, Florida in 1980. Active in Temple Israel Men’s Club and other activities. For about twenty years, he and Mom bought and sold antiques, selling at flea markets and antique shows. He enjoyed playing poker a
1 CONC nd other card games. Learned to use a computer in the early 1990s and wrote five autobiographical books. When lifting and carrying got too hard, he learned to use a digital camera and scanner and sold antiques on the Internet, via
1 CONC  eBay.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dad died in Memorial Hospital, Ormond Beach, of a septic infection in his abdomen that ruptured his gall bladder.
0 @N6000000001498704097@ NOTE
1 CONT On passenger manifest, his first name is Shike.  His Yiddish name was pronounced She/ya.
0 @N6000000001498704118@ NOTE
1 CONT Before Jay was born, there was a stillborn girl, c. 1936. The baby strangled on the umbilical cord.
1 CONT Lil received several commendations for her work as a fund raiser during World War II. She was active in the Girl Scouts when she lived in Nevada during the 1960s and 70s. Like her father, she was a card player and enjoyed gambling
1 CONC . Like her mother, she was an accomplished knitter. During her years in Daytona Beach, she was active at Temple Israel, working in the gift shop and with Hadassah.
0 @N6000000001498721682@ NOTE
1 CONT Shirley's Hebrew name written on her tombstone was Shifra Chaya. CT Death Index #12318
0 @N6000000001498729464@ NOTE
1 CONT Original name was Liebe. Although she listed 15 August 1902 as her arrival into the U.S. on her petition, she actually arrived on the S.S. Statendam from Rotterdam into N.Y. on 8 September 1903. Her brother, Schie, age 10 was with
1 CONC  her, and they were going to her father at 178 Clinton St., N.Y. They were detained for a few hours and released to the father after lunch.
1 CONT
1 CONT She gave her birthdate as 15 April 1880 on her petition. Meyer said her birthday was 4 May 1880 on his petition. She said she was 20 when she arrived in 1903 and she was 24 on her marriage license in 1906.
0 @N6000000001498729405@ NOTE
1 CONT On 1920 census, her name appears to be “Janine.” On 1930 census, she’s listed as “Jean.” Engagement announcement appeared in the New York Times on 26 Nov 1933.
0 @N6000000001498729419@ NOTE
1 CONT Birth and death in Social Security Death Index. Last payment to Oakland Gardens, Queens. Enlisted in the Armed Forces in the Bronx on 21 Aug 1942. Said his civilian occupation was “multilith or multigraph operator.” May have been 
1 CONC born in Brooklyn.
0 @N6000000001498729431@ NOTE
1 CONT Birthday may be 5 May 1912 in Brooklyn.
0 @N6000000001498729446@ NOTE
1 CONT Birth and death in Social Security Death Index. Last payment to Flushing, Queens.
1 CONT Birthdate may have been in August in Brooklyn. May have divorced Ann.
0 @N6000000001498729265@ NOTE
1 CONT On marriage license, her mother is listed as Minnie Stein.
0 @N6000000001498729289@ NOTE
1 CONT When Hymie Rose died, a notice appeared in the N.Y. Times saying “The cutters of Hampshire Fashions extend deepest sympathy to their bereaved employer, Mack Rose, upon the demise of his beloved father.”
1 CONT An article in the New York Times on 18 June 1971, says that “Mack Rose Originals, Inc., a manufacturer of women’s coats, filed a petition yesterday in Federal Court for ...bankruptcy.” The company was a few hundred thousand dollar
1 CONC s in debt. The office was at 247 West 37th St. in N.Y.C.
0 @N6000000001498729328@ NOTE
1 CONT Never married. Had some sort of sleeping sickness.
0 @N6000000001498729307@ NOTE
1 CONT His Hebrew name appears to be Clem, short for Clement, meaning “merciful” or “gracious.”
0 @N6000000001498729362@ NOTE
1 CONT According to Ruth Rose, Lillian was a twin but the other baby was stillborn.
0 @N6000000001498708134@ NOTE
1 CONT Her nickname, J.J., was how the family knew her. She died of cancer, neuroblastoma.
0 @N6000000001498733596@ NOTE
1 CONT Drafted in 1944 and served in the Navy in World War II.
0 @N6000000001498733646@ NOTE
1 CONT Obituary lists four grandchildren: Jared, Sky, Ben and Jennifer. Says she was the sister of Marilyn Goldman and Irving Rogg.
0 @N6000000001498729694@ NOTE
1 CONT Left N.Y.C. because of asthma and opened a dental practice in Nanuet, Rockland Co., N.Y.
0 @N6000000001498699955@ NOTE
1 CONT Ed was diagnosed with pancreatic cancer in December 2004. He had major surgery in January 2005 and began chemo in April. He battled fiercely against this awful disease until the very end. He died at St. Luke’s Hospital on Wednesda
1 CONC y at 3:05 a.m. leaving a gigantic hole in the hearts of everyone who loved him.
