0 @N6000000006348426619@ NOTE Title:    The Whitin family : historical notes
1 CONT  Author:    Swift, Katharine Whitin, b. 1887.
1 CONT  City of Publication:    Whitinsville, Mass.
1 CONT  Publisher:    E.K. Swift
1 CONT  Date:    1955
1 CONT  Page Count:    ix, 216 p.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Pidge or "Pigge" was an inhabitant of Roxbury and had lands there according to the earliest records of the time.  He was a freeman on May 14, 1634.  He brought from England his wife Mary and seven children, and had afterwar
1 CONC ds Martha, baptized in Roxbury in 1644....He was buried December 30, 1643 and his will was probated December 7, 1644.  He left all to his wife as long as she lived, and after her death his allotment in the 1000 acres lying at Dedh
1 CONC am was to go to his daughter Mary.  The widow married on August 13, 1645, Michael Metcalf, and on his death in 1664, received a room in the house of his son, Thomas Metcalf, in Dedham, 16 pounds in money, although she had reserved
1 CONC  her lands and estate  in a covenant made before her remarriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is from:
1 CONT Robert Charles Anderson, The Great Migration Begins:  Immigrants to New England 1620-1633,  (New England Historic Genealogical Society, 1995, 2000).
1 CONT
1 CONT THOMAS PIDGE 
1 CONT
1 CONT ORIGIN: Saffron Walden, Essex
1 CONT MIGRATION: 1633
1 CONT FIRST RESIDENCE: Roxbury
1 CONT CHURCH MEMBERSHIP: "Thomas Pigge" was admitted to Roxbury church as member #84, among those who arrived in 1633 [RChR 79].
1 CONT FREEMAN: 14 May 1634 [MBCR 1:368].
1 CONT ESTATE: In the Roxbury land inventory of about 1652 the heirs of "Thomas Pigge" held ten parcels of land, all of which had been granted to him: "house, barn and lot with a swamp, three and a half acres"; three acres upon Black Nec
1 CONC k; one acre and a half in the calves' pasture; one acre and a half of salt marsh; five acres of land on Dorchester Brook; five acres of land; eight acres of land; two acres of fresh meadow; twenty-four and a half acres, being the 
1 CONC twentieth lot in the second allotments in the last division; and twenty acres in the thousand acres near Dedham [RBOP 31-32].
1 CONT    In his undated will, proved 12 (or 14) September 1644, "Thomas Pig" bequeathed to "his son Thomas Pig the house with the homelot & two acres of fresh marsh & ... my lot by the dead swamp & ... all the land in the neck both upla
1 CONC nd & marsh & the five acres at the great lots end, he paying to his brother John ten pounds, five pounds at twenty-one years of age & the other five pounds a year after & this also I do give to my son Thomas Pig the land in the ca
1 CONC lves' pasture paying to his sister Hany Pig six pounds, three pounds a year after his mother's death & the other three pound the year after that," to "my daughter Sary & my daughter Mathew [i.e., Martha] my eight acre lot lying up
1 CONC on Pig's Hill & I also give to them my last division of ground," to "my daughter Mary my allotment in the thousand acres lying at Dedham," and to "my wife ... all I have so long as she lives to bring up my children & after her dea
1 CONC th all my children to have their portions as aforesaid" [APop(p28012,90,108);">SPR 1:33].
1 CONT BIRTH: By about 1594 based on date of marriage.
1 CONT DEATH: Roxbury 30 December 1643 ("Thomas Pig died of a dropsy, a godly Christian man. He had a fall & a bruise on his back, which hurt his kidneys & not carefully cured they utterly wasted away & many other of his entrails" [RChR 
1 CONC 171]).
1 CONT MARRIAGE: Saffron Walden, Essex, 1 November 1619 Mary Sothy; "Mary Pigge the wife of Thomas Pigge" was admitted to Roxbury church as member #85 [RChR 79]. She married (2) Dedham 13 August 1645 Michael Metcalf, as his second wife [
1 CONC DeVR 126].
1 CONT CHILDREN:
1 CONT     i   MARY, b. say 1622; m. by 1642 Nicholas Wood [TAG 36:112, 117]. (On 25 December 1642 John Eliot recorded "Mary Wood [and] Sarah Wood, twins, daughters of [blank] Wood of the Church of Bra[i]ntree who married our brother Pig
1 CONC 's daughter and she lying in childbed in this town, they were baptized here by communion of churches" [RChR 14].) 
1 CONT
1 CONT     ii   ANN, bur. Saffron Walden 9 March 1626/7. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     iii   SARAH, bp. Saffron Walden 30 November 1626; bur. there 26 August 1629. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     iv   HANNAH, bp. Saffron Walden 25 May 1629; living unmarried in 1644, named in her father's will; no further record. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     v   THOMAS, bp. Saffron Walden 12 December 1631; bur. there 25 July 1632. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     vi   THOMAS, b. about 1635 (deposed 20 June 1653 aged eighteen [Pope 360, citing Middlesex Files]); d. unmarried on or before 5 September 1660 (not clear whether this is date of death or date of inventory), administration gran
1 CONC ted to "John Pigge his brother in behalf of himself and his sisters," his estate totalling £34 19s. 2d., including a Bible valued at 3s. [SPR 4:1, Case #250]. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     vii   SARAH, b. say 1637; m. (as "Sarah Paige") Dedham 12 September 1656 Thomas Metcalfe (her stepbrother) [DeVR 127]. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     viii   JOHN, b. say 1640; m. Dedham 27 April 1667 Mary Farrington [DeVR 10]. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     ix   MARTHA, b. Roxbury 12 March 1642/3; m. Dedham 5 April 1659 Benjamin Bullard [DeVR 127]. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT COMMENTS: The definitive article on Thomas Pidge and his family was published in 1960 by Waldo Chamberlain Sprague [TAG 36:110-117]. He presented three generations of ancestry for Thomas, based on the assumption that the immigrant
1 CONC  to New England was the Thomas Pidge, minor, named as a son in the 1603 will of Nicholas Pigg of Newport, Essex; this assumption may be true, but additional proof should be sought.
