0 @N6000000003148563156@ NOTE [1335.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT They came from St. Albans perish, Hereford, England in 1636.  He was one
1 CONT of the proprietors of Bridgewater, Mass. in 1649.  He died at Daxbury,
1 CONT Mass.
1 CONT His will is dated July 16, 1649.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following was taken from the Internet on June 22, 1998
1 CONT
1 CONT 1 JOHN PAYBODY1 was born about 1590 in England. He probably arrived in the
1 CONT Plymouth Colony in 1635 or 1636, for his name appears on the list of
1 CONT freemen of the colony dated March 7, 1636/37, and he was admitted and
1 CONT sworn with others whose names were on that list on Jan. 2, 1637/8. He
1 CONT married Isabel Harper, sister of Thomas Harper of London, England. John
1 CONT Peabody received a grant of 10 acres of land Jan. 1, 1637/8, on Duxburrow
1 CONT side, lying betwixt the lands of William Tubs on the north side and those
1 CONT of Experience Mitchell on the south side, and from the sea in the west;
1 CONT and from Blew Fish River in the easte. Another tract, granted him Nov. 2,
1 CONT 1640, was 30 acres with meadow to it at North River.
1 CONT He was a member of the jury that convicted three young Englishmen of the
1 CONT murder of an Indian on Sept. 4, 1638, and of the Grand Inquest at the
1 CONT court June 4, 1639. He was one of the sureties on a neighbor's bond June
1 CONT 4, 1645.
1 CONT In 1645, John Paybody and his son, William, were among the original
1 CONT proprietors of Bridgewater, Mass. He was a witness with Thomas Winterton
1 CONT against Edward Richards when he was tried in court at Salem 4:9:1645, on a
1 CONT charge of making a false statement to them concerning shipboard killings.
1 CONT Richards was convicted and fined 10 shillings and ordered to acknowledge
1 CONT his sin before the congregation at Lynn.
1 CONT John's will, dated July 16, 1649, at Duxbury, was proved Apr. 27, 1666/7
1 CONT at Boston:
1 CONT In and about the sixteenth of July in the yeare of our Lord 1649 I John
1 CONT Paybody of Duxbrook in the Collonie of New Plymoute planter being in
1 CONT prfect health and sound in memory God be blessed for it doe ordaine and
1 CONT make this my last Will and Testament In maner and forme as foloweth;
1 CONT Imprimis I bequeath my soule to God that gave it hopeing to be saved by
1 CONT the Meritt of Christ
1 CONT my blessed Saviour and Redeemer; as for my worldly goods as followeth
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto Thomas my eldest sonne one shilling
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto ffrancis Paybody my second son one
1 CONT shilling.
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto William Paybody my youngest son one
1 CONT shilling.
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto Annis Rouse my daughter one shilling.
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto John Rouse the son of John Rouse my lands
1 CONT att Carswell in Marshfield after my wifes decease;
1 CONT Item I give unto John Pabodie the son of William my lott of Land att the
1 CONT new plantation,
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath all the Rest of my goods that are my mine
1 CONT liveing and dead
1 CONT unto my wife Isabell Paybody whome I make my sole executrix of this my
1 CONT last Will and Testament;
1 CONT memorandum all these legasyes before sett downe are to yayed by William
1 CONT Paybody my youngest son when they shallbe demanded
1 CONT John Paybody
1 CONT John ffernesyde
1 CONT
1 CONT Boston in New England
1 CONT the 27th of April, 1667
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr John ffernesyde came before mee under written and deposed that by
1 CONT order of
1 CONT Paybody above written and mentioned: hee wrote what is above written and
1 CONT Read it to the said John
1 CONT Paybody on the day of the date thereof and declared the same to be his
1 CONT Last Will and that when hee soe did hee was of a sound disposing mind to
1 CONT his best knowlidg and alsoe subscribed his name thereunto John ffernesyde
1 CONT as a witness;
1 CONT As Attesteth Edward
1 CONT Rawson Recorder
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources: Records and Files of the Quarterly Courts of Essex County,
1 CONT [Mass.], I, 1911;
1 CONT Plymouth Colony Wills and Inventories, Mayflower Descendant XVII:1
1 CONT (January
1 CONT 1915);
1 CONT Justin Winsor, History of the Town of Duxbury, Massachusetts, 1849; Eugene
1 CONT Willard Montgomery, Willard Peabody Genealogy, 1915; Charles Henry Pope,
1 CONT Pioneers of Massachusetts, 1900; William Richard Cutter and William
1 CONT Frederick Adams, Genealogical and Personal Memoirs Relating to the
1 CONT Families of the State of Massachusetts, II, 1910; C.M. Endicott, Genealogy
1 CONT of the Peabody Family, 1867, revised and corrected by William S. Peabody
1 CONT with a partial record of the Rhode Island Branch by B. Frank Peabody,
1 CONT cited hereafter as Peabody Genealogy, 1867; Selim Hobart Peabody, Peabody
1 CONT (Paybody,
1 CONT Pabody, Pabodie) Genealogy, 1909, cited hereafter as Peabody Genealogy,
1 CONT 1909; Leon Clark Hills, History and Genealogy of the Mayflower Planters
1 CONT (Cape Cod Series), I, 1975 reprint of 1936 1941 ed.; Dorothy A. Sherman
1 CONT Lainson, John Paybodie (Peabody) English Immigrant to Plymouth Duxbury,
1 CONT 1635, 1972; Goldie Peabody Brownyard and Theodore Lucius Brownyard,
1 CONT Ancestors and Descendants of Charles Elmer Peabody, 1980; Richard Gentry,
1 CONT Gentry Family in America, 1909; Detroit Society for Genealogical Research
1 CONT Magazine XXX
