0 @N6000000004972785273@ NOTE From p.3 of "Elizaeth (Alden) Pabodie and Descendants" by Mary Langford Taylor Alden:
1 CONT
1 CONT In "Windsor's History of Duxbury" and elsewhere, we find "John Pabodie 1637 ; admitted freeman Jan. 2, 1637-8 ; proprietor of Bridgewater 1645 ; will dated July 16, 1649 ; proved June 27, 1666. He married Isabel, who  survuved him
1 CONC . In his will he speaks of Thomas, Francis, and William, and Amie, wife of John Rouse, grandson John Rouse and Grandson John Pabodie, son of son William.
1 CONT
1 CONT **************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT From p.205 of Old Homesteads and Historic Buildings, Genealogy and Family Lore : Architectural-Pictorial-Historical Exhibition by Kan Parsons
1 CONT
1 CONT Found at: http://content.ancestry.com/browse/bookview.aspx?dbid=30163&iid=dvm_LocHist013788-00106-1&rc= &pid=202&ssrc=&fn=&ln=Record+from+Old+homesteads+and+historic+buildings%2c+genealogy+and+family+lore+%3a+architectural-pictori
1 CONC al-historical+exhibition&st=g
1 CONT
1 CONT PABODIE
1 CONT
1 CONT John Pabodie came from England and lived 1645 at Bridgewater, also at Little Compton, Bristol County Massachusetts, until 1747, then in Rhode Island. His will was probated July 16, 1659.
1 CONT
1 CONT **************************************
0 @N6000000003122811362@ NOTE Arrived in New England on ship "Planter", 1635.  Freeman, Plymouth, 1638.
0 @N6000000003148563186@ NOTE [1335.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Came to America on the "Planter", Nicholis Travier, Master, when he was 21
1 CONT years of age.  He married Lydia.  On the 24th of June, 1662, Francis
1 CONT Peabody made deposition giving his age as 50 years (File Essex Co.
1 CONT quarterly Court)
1 CONT He lived in Ipsvich in 1636 and was made freeman on May 18, 1642.  His
1 CONT wife was mentioned as "Lydia Pebody" Nov. 4, 1645, when she was slandered
1 CONT by Eunice Cole and the latter ordered to set in stocks at Hampton, N.H.
1 CONT and acknowledge her guilt. After 1649 Francis married Mary, daughter of
1 CONT Reginald Foster, and had Sara, Hepzibia, Mary, Ruth, Damaris, Samuel,
1 CONT Jacob, Hannah and Nathan.  It is said that Mary was the widow of Daniel
1 CONT Wood, who died about 1648.  The will of Reginal Foster mentions his
1 CONT daughter Mary, wife of Francis Peabody, dated Ipswich 30, April 1680
1 CONT (Ancestry of Franklin M. Peabody.)
1 CONT
1 CONT The following was taken from the Internet on June 22, 1998:
1 CONT
1 CONT Francis Peabody's Halberd
1 CONT
1 CONT When Lieut. Francis Peabody left St. Albans, England, for America in 1635,
1 CONT he took with him a personal weapon of war known as a halberd or halbert.
1 CONT This instrument, used in the 15th and 16th centuries and consisting of a
1 CONT battle ax and pike mounted on a handle, was passed down through the family
1 CONT for a number of generations. It found its way before 1852 into the hands
1 CONT of Col. Francis Peabody, a descendant of the original Francis. It was
1 CONT while it was the possession of the latter Francis Peabody, of Salem,
1 CONT Massachusetts, that the weapon was described and sketched for the
1 CONT historical publication Centennial Celebration at Danvers, Mass., June 16,
1 CONT 1852, Boston, 1852. The materials with reference to this ancient weapon
1 CONT were submitted to the publication committee by C. M. Endicott of Salem, a
1 CONT highly regarded historian and antiquarian who was well acquainted with the
1 CONT Peabody family. It was Endicott who published extensive material on the
1 CONT Peabodys in the New England Historic and Genealogical Register in 1848 and
1 CONT 1849 and whose work on the family was published in 1867 as the first
1 CONT general
1 CONT genealogy of the family in America, known as Genealogy of the Peabody
1 CONT Family. On Nov. 22, 1852, Endicott wrote the publication committee, in
1 CONT part:
1 CONT
1 CONT      "My Dear Sir, - I send you a drawing of the 'halbard' once borne by
1 CONT Lieut. Francis Peabody, who emigrated from St. Albans, England, to this
1 CONT country, in
1 CONT 1635, - the year in which so large a number of the friends of of religious
1 CONT           liberty,  by a simultaneous movement, determined to seek a
1 CONT refuge from civil      and ecclesiatical oppression on the bleak and
1 CONT inhospitable shores of New      England,  however 'fiercely the wide ocean
1 CONT might open its mouth to swallow      them, or with what terrors the wintry
1 CONT wilderness might threaten them.' This           movement resulted, before
1 CONT the close of that year, in the emigration of some         three thousand
1 CONT persons, and among this great number was Francis Pabody of      St.
1 CONT Albans, the first American ancestor of Geo. Peabody, now of London, the
1 CONT   liberal benefactor of your town, and also of all others in America who
1 CONT spell          the name in this manner.  have no doubt whatever of the
1 CONT authenticity of this      ancient relic, having in the course of my labors
1 CONT in 'digging out the roots, and       following out the branches of the
1 CONT Peabody tree,' traced the possession of it,       as an heir-loom in the
1 CONT family, directly  from Lieut. Francis down through the        descendants
1 CONT of his fourth son Isaac to its  present owner, Col. Francis
1 CONT Peabody, of this city...  "Lieut. Francis Peabody at his death left his
1 CONT  homestead, with all the goods and chattels it contained, to his fourth
1 CONT son     Isaac, and among them was this halbard.
1 CONT     Isaac's son Isaac inherited the same after him. The last Isaac never
1 CONT married,  and at his death his effects were divided among his brothers and
1 CONT sisters, and     this halbard fell to his brother Matthew's portion. From
1 CONT Matthew it descended     to his son John, and from John again to his son
1 CONT John; from the latter it     descended to his son Joel R. Peabody, of
1 CONT Topsfield, of whom it was obtained     by its present owner, who is also a
1 CONT descendant of Isaac.
1 CONT     "A wooden leg, said by tradition to have belonged to the first Isaac,
1 CONT was also
1 CONT handed down in the family of Matthew with this halbard, until the
1 CONT generation
1 CONT preceding Joel, when by some means the leg was lost. This tradition I have
1 CONT        since found to be confirmed by the following clause in his father's
1 CONT will: 'And        this I would have noted, that I have left the more to my
1 CONT son Isaak, in     consideration of the providence of God disinabling him
1 CONT by the loss of one of     his legs.'"
1 CONT
1 CONT FRANCIS PABODY2 (John1), who came to America in the spring of 1635, was
1 CONT born in England about 1614. The approximate birthdate is derived from
1 CONT several sources, including a passenger list for the ship Planter found in
1 CONT the Record Office of the British government in London. This record,
1 CONT believed to have been written in the time of Charles I, contains this
1 CONT heading and entry:
1 CONT
1 CONT 2 Aprilis 1635
1 CONT
1 CONT Theis under written names are to be transported to New England imbarqued
1 CONT in the Planter Nico: Trarice Mr bound thither the pties have brought
1 CONT certificates from the Minister of St Albons in Hertfordshier & Attestacon
1 CONT from the Justices of peace according to the Lord's order.
1 CONT
1 CONT Husb: man Franc's Peboddy . . . . 21
1 CONT
1 CONT In such documents, the age set down after a name often was a mere
1 CONT approximation. The papers of Essex County Court in Massachusetts contain a
1 CONT deposition, dated the 24th of the 4th month 1662 (June 24, 1662) giving
1 CONT the testimoni of ffrancis Pabody aged fifti years.
1 CONT Another deposition, given Nov. 19, 1690, gives his age as 78 years.
1 CONT While the ship list would place his birth at 1612 or 1614, the depositions
1 CONT would set it at 1612. The Planter sailed from London about Apr. 10, 1635,
1 CONT and arrived in Boston, Mass., June 7, 1635. Francis Peabody, a farmer and
1 CONT miller, settled first at Ipswich, Essex County, Massachusetts, where he
1 CONT was living in 1636 near Labour in Vaine on a road that led to the beach.
1 CONT The first reference to Francis in the Ipswich records appears in a
1 CONT description of lands owned by Samuel Hall:
1 CONT
1 CONT Eight acres of planting ground, by an act of the Towne as in the old book
1 CONT Anno 1636, lying near the highway going to Labour in vaine meadowe,
1 CONT butting to ye East upon ye planting ground of John Seaborn and on ye North
1 CONT planting ground of Francis Pebody.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1638 or 1639, the settler joined with the Rev. Stephen Bachilor and 12
1 CONT others in the original settlement of Hampton, New Hampshire. He became a
1 CONT freeman of Hampton May 18, 1642. In 1645, he held a share in the common
1 CONT lands of Hampton and was also acting as a member of the committee that
1 CONT oversaw the finishing of the meeting house.
1 CONT The Francis Peabody residence in Hampton was near those of Isaac Perkins
1 CONT and William Cole. Cole's wife, Eunice, apparently had difficulty
1 CONT controlling her tongue and sometimes lashed out at her neighbors. An entry
1 CONT in the records of the Ipswich Court, under date of Nov. 4, 1645, says
1 CONT Eunice Cole is to sit in the stocks at Hampton and to make
1 CONT acknowledgement of her slanderous speeches concerning Susan Parkings &
1 CONT lidia pebodye and to pay to the witnes
1 CONT Isaac Perkings 7d and the feas of the court. This Mrs. Cole subsequently
1 CONT was tried for witchcraft.
1 CONT After spending about 12 years in Hampton, Francis Peabody on March 25,
1 CONT 1650, sold his Hampton property to Robert Drake and moved to Topsfield,
1 CONT Mass., which had two years earlier been set off from his former home of
1 CONT Ipswich. Here he spent the rest of his life. On moving to Topsfield, he
1 CONT bought 250 acres of land from Samuel Symonds. On Apr. 20, 1666, he
1 CONT purchased from his near neighbor, William Evans, an acre located beside
1 CONT Pye Brook, on which he established in 1672 the first grist mill in the
1 CONT town. His house, situated on the bank of
1 CONT the brook, survived until 1846. He built a second, two story house a
1 CONT little farther up the brook in 1692, and this stood until Oct. 4, 1914. A
1 CONT deposition made by Francis Peabody in 1690, when he was 78, provides some
1 CONT interesting detail:
1 CONT
1 CONT The testimony of francis Pabody aged 78 years is that I having bought on
1 CONT half of mr Simmonds farm: ye said ffeild mr Simmonds sent up goodman gage
1 CONT & goodman Brag to shew me the bounds of that farme, and they did shew me a
1 CONT stak in a medow which medow is now on the back sid of the dweling house of
1 CONT Isaac Cumins senr & on the north sid of the brook several rods: to be the
1 CONT bounds on the north east corner of the farm: & from thence to a tree on
1 CONT the end of pains hill near to a medow where Ipswich commity setled the
1 CONT bounds; afterward I got ensign howlet to help me run the line on the south
1 CONT est side of the farme between goodman every & me & he with others brought
1 CONT the line to the same place on the south est corner: to which place my
1 CONT fence did then & now doth point.
1 CONT Swore november 19th 1690 before me Samll Appleton Asst.
1 CONT
1 CONT Little is known of Francis Peabody's first wife. She is clearly identified
1 CONT as Lydia in documents of the period, but her maiden name, ancestry and
1 CONT origin are unknown. She died before 1649 and he married about 1650 Mary
