1 CONT John Warburton [Parents] was born in 1459 in Arley,Cheshire,England. He died in 1524/1525. He married Johanna Stanley about 1489.
1 CONT
1 CONT     Sir John Warburton administered to the effects of father Feb.
1 CONT     14, 1495, but in 1487 Piers Warburton, his father, had contracted for his
1 CONT     marriage with Johanna (called Joan and Jane. In the 32nd year of
1 CONT     Elizabeth, 1590, the Court of the King's Bench were agreed that these
1 CONT     names were all one name). Johanna Stanley was the daughter of Sir
1 CONT     William Stanley, of Holt, and he was the son of Thomas Stanley and
1 CONT     Joanna Goushill, descended from de Bohuns and Edward I. References:
1 CONT     Ormerod's Chester--Vol. 1, pp. 428-9, Warburton; Vol. 3, p. 306, Stanley.
1 CONT     Browning's M. C. Barons, p. 265; p. 301 gives 8 barons for Joan Goushill,
1 CONT     adding Robert de Vere, Roger and Hugh Begod to those mentioned above.
1 CONT     DATE 8 AUG 2002
1 CONT     TIME 21:03:00
1 CONT
1 CONT Johanna Stanley [Parents] was born about 1463 in Holt Castle,Weever,Cheshire,England. She died in 1525. She married John Warburton about 1489.
1 CONT
1 CONT     NICK Jane
1 CONT     DATE 17 NOV 2002
1 CONT     TIME 10:47:57
1 CONT
1 CONT They had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT       M i Piers Warburton
1 CONT       F ii Blanch Warburton
1 CONT
1 CONT =======================================================
0 @N6000000001715457445@ NOTE Succeeded his father in Mann and his other estates in 1432. He had been knighted some years before his father's death. In the same year he was appointed Lieutenant of Ireland for six years, and shortly afterwards Comptroller of th
1 CONC e King's Household. During the first year of his rule in Ireland he called together a Parliament for the redress of grievances; but, being called to England by the King's command soon afterwards, that kingdom fell into great disor
1 CONC der, and he was obliged to return to it in 1435, when he successfully repressed a serious revolt. In 1441 he was appointed one of the Lieutenant justices of Chester, at a salary of £40 per annum. He was one of the Commissioners wh
1 CONC o treated with the Scotch for a truce in 1448, and, when it was concluded, he became one of its conservators. He also served on a commission for the custody and defence of the town and castle of Calais from 1450 to 1455. During th
1 CONC e year 1451 he held the office of sole Judge of Chester, and in 1452 he was commissioned to treat for a new truce with Scotland. In 1456 he was summoned to the House of Peers as Baron Stanley, being made Lord Chamberlain of the Ki
1 CONC ng's Household, and, in the following year, one of the Council of Edward, Prince of Wales. He was again appointed one of the Ambassadors to treat with the Scotch in 1460, "but, dying the latter end of the year, the nation was depr
1 CONC ived of this very great and valuable person, and the King of one of his best subjects... He was brave in the field, wise in the Senate, just to his Prince, an honour to his country, and an ornament to his family". He married Joan,
1 CONC  daughter and heiress of Sir Robert Goushill, by whom he had issue three sons, Thomas, William, and John; and three daughters
