0 @N6000000000930017855@ NOTE !     Thomas was named in document of 14 November, 1668 as living with his 
1 CONT fat her John Whicker, on property called Codlins, Colyton.
1 CONT Thomas married ca. 1680 -82.  Wife's name cannot be found, and she
1 CONT apparently died by 1690 when Thomas  remarried.  from the following
1 CONT indenture it
1 CONT is apparent that Thomas left En gland the last of September 1685 or early
1 CONT October and arrived Virginia 1 Decem ber 1685 on Capt. Walter Lyie's ship;
1 CONT John
1 CONT Lyle, Master.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:  Staffor d County, Va court Records - May 1684 - 1687/8 and 11
1 CONT December 1689-1693, page  4.
1 CONT {A note typed in the back of this volume states the following: When
1 CONT Wilm er L.
1 CONT Hall was Virginia State Librarian, this volume was certified to contain
1 CONT true
1 CONT and photostatic copies of the original volume, as of January 7, 1942.}
1 CONT
1 CONT Page 3: At a Stafford Court, 12 Oct 1689.... Justices present:  Col. Wm
1 CONT Fit zhugh, Mr. Samuel Hayward, Capt. George Mason, Mr. Edward Thomason,
1 CONT Mr. John
1 CONT Withera, Mr. William Buckner, and Mr. Mathew Thompson.
1 CONT Page 4: "Thos. Whicker s and George Spillers by Capt. George Brent their
1 CONT attorney humbly showeth that  the petitioners being in England their native
1 CONT country about the 29th of Septe mber 1685 at the town of Topsham in the
1 CONT County
1 CONT of Devon and minding to trans port themselves into Virginia, did then and
1 CONT there
1 CONT agree with John Lyle....tr ansportation, clothing, and fourth, did indent
1 CONT each
1 CONT respectively with the sa id John Lyle to serve him or his assigns after the
1 CONT arrival in Virginia four ye ars, which said reciprocal indentures were
1 CONT sealed
1 CONT and executed before and at tested by William Glyde, Esq., a Justice of the
1 CONT Peace
1 CONT of the toen of Topsham , and afterwards your petitioners came into
1 CONT Virginia in
1 CONT Capt. Lyle's ship an d their Master John Lyle after their arrival which
1 CONT was the
1 CONT first day of Dece mber 1685, sold them to Richard Aylisse with whom they
1 CONT have
1 CONT only served ever  since and showed their Indentures to divers people which
1 CONT they
1 CONT safely kept u ntil about 3 or 4 months when they were taken out of the room
1 CONT where they lodge d by some sinister practice which the said Aylisse
1 CONT refuseth to
1 CONT discharge you r petitioners or to pay them their corn and clothes,
1 CONT wherefore
1 CONT they have bro ught their humble complaint to your worships, and do most
1 CONT humbly
1 CONT pray that t heir witnesses may be sworne to prove their Indentures, that
1 CONT they
1 CONT may be dis charged from their service, and the said Aylosse may be ordered
1 CONT to
1 CONT pay your Petitioners there corn and clothes, together with suhh loss as
1 CONT they
1 CONT shall be  out to the Clerke for the said order."
1 CONT
1 CONT "And now forasmuch as the said Thoma s Whicker and George Spillers did in
1 CONT Court prove by the Oaths of Two Sufficien t Evidences upon the holy
1 CONT Evangelists
1 CONT of Almighty God that they had each of them respectively lawful and
1 CONT authentick
1 CONT Indentures for four years time as af oresaid, therefore tis ordered that
1 CONT the
1 CONT said Thomas Whickers and George Spil lers (sic) of and from Richard Aylisse
1 CONT their master shall be free and that the  said Richard Aylisse shall pay
1 CONT each of
1 CONT them their corn and clothes with all  country dues respectively with all
1 CONT loss at
1 CONT Execution."
1 CONT
1 CONT About 1690, Thoma s married a second time, but her name is not found.  She
1 CONT died Before 1704.  It  appears Thomas sent Benjamin to England before 1704
1 CONT when
1 CONT he wrote his will in Westmoreland County.
1 CONT Thomas Wicker was one of five witness to the will of J ohn Scott of
1 CONT Westmoreland County 20 May 1700.  A Codicil was proved by Charles 
1 CONT Tankersley
1 CONT and Thomas Whickers on November 26, 1701.
1 CONT There dose not seem t o be any relationship, but among the Provisions of
1 CONT this will was one that Scot t's son John, when age 8 or 9 years, was to be
1 CONT sent
1 CONT to England to his uncle Gustavus to be educated.  It may be that Benjamin
1 CONT Wicker was sent to England a t the same time.
1 CONT
1 CONT Will of Thomas Whicker, dated 4 April 1704, Westmoreland Co unty, Virginia
1 CONT proved 26 July 1704. Reads:
1 CONT
1 CONT In the name of God Amen!  I, Tho mas Wicker of Appamattox in Westmoreland
1 CONT County,
