0 @N6000000000930017762@ NOTE !     John and his brother Benjamin were exiled to Barbados in 1685. this 
1 CONT is the
1 CONT account of that exile.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke of Monmouth, James Scott (1649-1685), w as a claimant to the
1 CONT English throne, a natural son of Charles II.  After Popis h Plot agitation
1 CONT in
1 CONT 1678, supporters of Protestant succession championed him  as the King's
1 CONT heir.
1 CONT Upon accession of James II in 1685, the Duke landed in England and raised
1 CONT a
1 CONT small force which included John and Benjamin Whicker. T he Duke was
1 CONT defeated,
1 CONT captured, and beheaded in 1685.
1 CONT Benjamin and John Whi cker were arrested for high treason for levying
1 CONT against the King in 1685 and w ere on a list of rebels in Dorchester
1 CONT prison.  On
1 CONT 8 Jan 1685/6 Benjamin and John were transported to Barbados on board the
1 CONT "Betty" of London from the port  of Weymouth.(Hottton).  On arrival in
1 CONT Barbadose, Benjamin was indentured to B arnabas Chater and John to Thomas &
1 CONT Company.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following letter was writt en from Barbados, 1 June 1686 by Thomas
1 CONT Frankling, also in exile.
1 CONT
1 CONT From the Barbados June 1, 1686,
1 CONT
1 CONT Dearest and ever loving wife my heartfelt love to you  and to my children,
1 CONT hoping in God.... will find you and my children all in go od health as I
1 CONT am at
1 CONT the present writing, for which I have great reason to b less God for it,
1 CONT for out
1 CONT of sixtie of our men that .... come to the Island . ... I having sent you
1 CONT four
1 CONT letters before this which I make no doubt but som e of them did come to
1 CONT your
1 CONT hands.  Peter Bagwell and John Whicker have had a  letter of . . . . to
1 CONT John
1 CONT Whicker from his father, and Peter from Sarah Tom son which  makes me
1 CONT wonder
1 CONT that you did neglect or that you cannot find any way to send me a line or
1 CONT two
1 CONT to satisfy me how it is with you, and to know w hether there are any hopes
1 CONT for
1 CONT my redemption for I did understand by Peter B agwell's letter that there
1 CONT was
1 CONT some hope of deliverance if it . . . . . well  looked after.
1 CONT So wife, I would desire that you would guard your (words or ton gue) that I
1 CONT might again enjoy the . . . . of my wife and childern and relation s in
1 CONT good old
1 CONT England as formerly I have . . . . and if it may be the will o f God to
1 CONT have it,
1 CONT so in short my kind my kind and heartie love to all my fri ends and
1 CONT relations.
1 CONT So all at present but preferring to . . . . you and my c hildern, and all
1 CONT to the
1 CONT protection of the almightie whom I hope and trust wi ll provide for you
1 CONT both
1 CONT spiritually and materially.
1 CONT So I remain your ever t ried and faithful husband until death,
1 CONT (signed)  Thomas Frankling
1 CONT
1 CONT The addre ss on the outside of the letter cannot be read completely.  What
1 CONT can be read " . . . . Franklin, living _________ Luppitt with care I pray
1 CONT . . .
1 CONT to be dire cted to Mr. Richard Newtons in Willan and from thence to be
1 CONT directed
1 CONT as I ha ve said with care and . . . . ."
1 CONT
1 CONT There is a will of record in Goochland Coun ty, Va in 1737 which may well
1 CONT be
1 CONT the same Thomas Frankling.
1 CONT
1 CONT John and Benj amin both returned to Colyton about 1687.
