0 @N6000000003807029086@ NOTE [From MF] The will of John Bass of Windham CT written 16 Apr 1752, probated
1 CONT 15 Oct 1753, included as an heir, dau. Elizabeth, wife of John Kingsley.
1 CONT Elizabeth signed for her share of father's estate 8 Nov 1753, but husband John
1 CONT Kingsley receipted alone for John Bass's legacy 9 Feb 1754 calling Elizabeth
1 CONT "my former wife."
1 CONT John Kingsley sold 33 acres in the Second Society in Windham to Benjamin
1 CONT Bidlack 17 Jan 1749/1750. There is no probate record for him in Windham
1 CONT District, and no death record has been found.
0 @N6000000003807029104@ NOTE Jonathan's last two children seem to be 'mixed up' in terms of birth date!
1 CONT
1 CONT Windham Herald, 8 Aug 1795
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr. Jonathon Kingsley of Woodstock, Vt. now in his 77th year of his age, was
1 CONT born in 1718, at Windham, Ct- and at the age of 26 years was married to a
1 CONT young woman, ( by whom he had 9 children) and died the 18th of July, 1793:
1 CONT and, on the 26th of Jun 1794, he was again married to a young women of the
1 CONT age of 25 years, who on the 10th of Apr 1795, was delivered of a beautiful
1 CONT daughter.-The youngest child whom he had with his 1st wife, was a
1 CONT grandmother, before he was married to his present one. The number of
1 CONT children, and great grandchildren, are 100. Farm. Jour.
0 @N6000000001503966187@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT     Emigrated in May 1760 on the ship "Wolfe" to Cornwallis, NS, and is mentioned as one of the grantees receiving part of the 100,000 acre land grant by George II, after the expulsion of the French. There are 5 Woodworths named i
1 CONC n the list of grantees: Amasa, Benjamin, Silas, Thomas, and William. [The Descendants of Walter Woodworth, by William Atwater Woodworth]
1 CONT
1 CONT     According to "Nova Scotia Immigrants" Silas received lot 2 [66 acres], Div. 4, Cornwallis Township Grant.
1 CONT
1 CONT     >Why did Wm and Sarah and the rest of the clan move on up to NS? Too early to have been Royalists! Did they see the handwriting on the wall? Was NS still part of the US at that time - 1761, with a grant in 1763.
1 CONT
1 CONT     In 1761, the United States did not exist. It was the New England Colony, and subject to the King of England. Nova Scotia was as well. It had been taken from the French during the war between France and England and was referred
1 CONC  to as Acadia. In 1755, while experiencing difficulty with some local (French) farmers, the English decided to expel some dissident families to relieve some of the turmoil. This was known as the Expulsion of the Acadians. Some of 
1 CONC these people went to Quebec, some returned to France and some went to Louisiana, where France maintained another colony. These people became known as "Cajun's". In all, about one third of the entire population was re-located, a to
1 CONC tal of about 6,000 people.
1 CONT     The English now had a more docile population to rule and in a couple of years the Governor decided that a larger, more friendly populace was required. The English went to their other Colony, the New England Colony, and suggest
1 CONC ed that there was excellent farm land available in Nova Scotia. Several thousand New England families re-located there and settled. The early grants were given in 1760 through 1765 in several counties.
1 CONT     The Expulsion has been immortalized in Longfellow's "Evangeline". It is an exagerated but emotional view of the plight of one person during that awful time.
0 @N6000000003807026887@ NOTE [From MF] John Bass of Windham CT, in his will written 16 Apr 1752, probated 15 Oct 1753, nam ed Priscilla wife of Eldad Kingsley as one of his daughters. Priscilla Kingsley was admitte d to the Scotland Cong'l Church 1 Nov 1741 a
1 CONC nd children Oliver, Eunice and Lydia were bapt. t here 8 Nov 1741. No probate record for Eldad or Priscilla appears in Windham CT District, whi ch included Lebanon in 1787. Both Eldad & his wife Priscilla are buried in Scoville an
1 CONC d Bucki ngham Cemetery, Lebanon, CT. The first five of their children were born in Windham, CT, the o thers in Lebanon, CT.
0 @N6000000003807029200@ NOTE Windham Herald 21 Feb 1795
1 CONT
1 CONT Died at Lebanon- Mr Nathan Kingsley
0 @N6000000006348426368@ NOTE CHILDREN
1 CONT of Henry5 and Deborah (Darling) Bosworth
1 CONT born in Mendon, Mass.
1 CONT 418+    i--ELIZABETH6, b. about 1733; d. ((???)); m. Theophilus Sweet.
1 CONT 419    ii--(A dau., see father's history above.) b. abt. 1735.
1 CONT 420   iii--MARY6, b. (???) 1737; m. Caleb Buxton.
1 CONT 421    iv--RACHEL6, b. (???) 1739; m. Aaron Buxton.
1 CONT 422     v--EUNICE6, b. (???) 1744; m. John Stafford.
1 CONT              The last three lived in Smithfield, R. I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mendon is now Bellingham.
1 CONT
1 CONT The next son was Henry, who, as has been before stated, married a daughter of Capt. John Darling of Bellingham. They were married in Smithfield, R. I., a town just south of Cumberland, in 1732. They afterward lived in Mendon, the 
1 CONC town from which Bellingham had been set off. There is abundant proof of these facts as will be shown later in the history of Henry.
1 CONT
1 CONT Clarke, Mrs. Mary Bosworth. Bosworth Genealogy vol. 3. San Francisco: n.p., 1928.
1 CONT
1 CONT HENRY5 BOSWORTH, son of Ichabod4 (Jonathan, Jr.3, Jonathan2 Edward1) and 1st wife Sarah (Stacy), born probably in Rehoboth about 1710, or very early in 1711;(*) place and date of death unknown; married at Smithfield, R. I., Apr. 2
1 CONC 5, 1732, DEBORAH DARLING, youngest daughter of Capt. John2 (Dennis1) and 3rd wife Elizabeth (Morse) Darling, born in Mendon, Mass. (later set off as Bellingham) Jan. 8, 1713_14; died before Mar. 17, 1750, on which date she is ment
1 CONC ioned in her father's will, as "daughter Deborah Bosworth deceased."
1 CONT
1 CONT NOTE: Capt. John Darling was son of Dennis, born in Braintree, Sept. 2, 1664 and died at Bellingham May 29, 1753 in his 90th year. He was a great business man and landed proprietor. He headed the petition for setting off the town 
1 CONC of Bellingham which was incorporated in 1719. He had land in Mendon as early as 1691. He had three wives (1) Elizabeth Thompson (2) Anne Rockwood and (3) Elizabeth Morse. By the latter he had eleven children, the youngest of whom 
1 CONC was Deborah, wife of Henry Bosworth.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry Bosworth, like his father Ichabod and his grandfather Jonathan Bosworth, Jr., was an "Anabaptist." (See Part II p. 156). So also was Capt. John Darling and family. They all attended the Anabaptist meeting in Mendon (Bellingh
1 CONC am) and Dec. 20, 1741 were excused from paying "Ministerial rates" on this account. (See "Annals of Mendon.") Sylvanus Scott, who married the widow of Ichabod Bosworth--Henry's brother--was also an "Anabaptist." He was a witness t
1 CONC o Capt. Darling's will.
1 CONT
1 CONT July 16, 1733, John Darling of Bellingham, yeoman for œ30 deeds to Henry Bosworth of Mendon, Cooper, 32 acres of land in Mendon; also 10 acres more of common land to be laid out.
1 CONT
1 CONT                                                    (Worcester Deeds 13_232) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Nov. 26, 1733, Henry Bosworth of Mendon, cooper, for œ30, buys 21 3/4 acres of land in Mendon and 10 acres in Uxbridge.
1 CONT
1 CONT                                                  (Worcester Deeds 34_75) 
1 CONT
1 CONT July 26, 1738, John Darling of Mendon "in ye county of Worcester" for œ100 deeds 20 acres of land to Henry Bosworth of Mendon, cooper.
1 CONT
1 CONT                                                    (Worcester Deeds 13_229) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Feb. 29, 1739_40, Henry Bosworth was one of the partners in the Land Bank.
1 CONT
1 CONT July 15, 1740, land was laid out for "Henry Borsworth" 2 a. 10 rds. "in the tenth division of land laid Joyning to his other land on the East side in the great river near Philip Amedowns. . .a three cornerd peace"; and on the same
1 CONC  day 2 a. in the ninth division, joining the same, "laid out by Thomas White, surveyor; benjamin darling, henry borsworth, chainmen."
1 CONT
1 CONT                                       (Proprietors Records, Mendon, p. 669) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Sept. 23, 1740, Henry Bosworth of Mendon, husbandman, for œ110, buys of William Phillips 20 a. of land with a dwelling house, adjoining land of John Darling.
1 CONT
1 CONT                                                    (Worcester Deeds 13_230) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Jan. 1, 1741, Henry's name in the Worcester Registry returns for Land Bank.
1 CONT
1 CONT                                                   (N. E. Hist. Reg. 50_192) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Nov. 28, 1741, Henry and Deborah for œ360 sold 70 a. of land in Mendon and 14 a. in Mendon and Uxbridge, to John Darling.
1 CONT
1 CONT                                                     (Worcester Deeds 19_23) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Nov. 15, 1744, and Jan. 2, 1745_6, Henry Bosworth's name in the News Letter of the Land Bank. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Mar. 15, 1754, After Capt. Darling's death, his son, Samuel Darling was apt. Guardian to the three younger children of Henry Bosworth "now or late of the province of New Jersey;" Mary, 17; Rachel, 15; and Eunice, 10; their mother,
1 CONC  Deborah being dead and their grandfather, Capt. John Darling, having left them property in his will. All were living "out of the province."
1 CONT
1 CONT                                                     (Boston Probate 49_137) 
1 CONT
1 CONT June 17, 1763, Theophilus Sweet and Elizabeth his wife both of Nine Partners, "Dukes County" (Dutchess), New York, sell to Timothy Darling of Bellingham, county of Suffolk Mass. Bay, "one share in five in a small tract of land tha
1 CONC t was set off to our mother Deborah Bosworth deceased out of our grandfather John Darlings real estate as her portion in said estate . . . situated in Bellingham aforesaid containing about Thirty acres."
1 CONT
1 CONT Signed sealed and delivered in presence of Samuel Very, Aaron Buxton, Eunice Bosworth.
1 CONT
1 CONT                                                      (Boston Deeds 122_157) 
1 CONT
1 CONT NOTE: The above deed plainly shows that there were five children, the three youngest are named above and this shows the elder was named Elizabeth, from Deborah's mother. The name of the second has not been discovered, but the comp
1 CONC iler wonders if her name was not Sarah, from Henry's mother.
1 CONT
1 CONT Feb. 20, 1765, John Stafford and wife Eunice, for œ8, give a quit-claim deed to "our cousin Timothy Darling of Bellingham," to all the real estate that came to them from their "hond grandfather Capt. John Darling deced . . . and w
1 CONC as set off . . . to the heirs of our mother Deborah Bosworth deceased, the youngest daughter of said Capt. John Darling," etc.
1 CONT
1 CONT                                                      (Boston Deeds 122_185) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Timothy Darling was a son of Ebenezer, Deborah's brother.
1 CONT
1 CONT CHILDREN
1 CONT of Henry5 and Deborah (Darling) Bosworth
1 CONT born in Mendon, Mass.
1 CONT
1 CONT 418+    i--ELIZABETH6, b. about 1733; d. ((???)); m. Theophilus Sweet. 
1 CONT 419    ii--(A dau., see father's history above.) b. abt. 1735.  [Believed to be Sarah]
1 CONT 420   iii--MARY6, b. (???) 1737; m. Caleb Buxton. 
1 CONT 421    iv--RACHEL6, b. (???) 1739; m. Aaron Buxton. 
1 CONT 422     v--EUNICE6, b. (???) 1744; m. John Stafford. 
1 CONT              The last three lived in Smithfield, R. I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Clarke, Mrs. Mary Bosworth. Bosworth Genealogy vol. 4. San Francisco: n.p., 1928.
1 CONT
1 CONT U.S. and International Marriage Records, 1560-1900
1 CONT Name: Henry Bosworth
1 CONT Gender: male
1 CONT Spouse Name: Deborah Darling
1 CONT Spouse Birth Year: 1713
1 CONT Number Pages: 1
